window.PEATAR = [{"id":"pes11-bones","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Locate the bones that support movement","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Locate the bones that support movement</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"The skeleton provides support, protects organs and supplies attachment sites for muscles. A bone does not actively shorten to create a movement. Muscular force transmitted through tendons turns bones around joints. Learn locations together with a sporting action rather than memorising a disconnected list. The humerus forms the upper arm; radius and ulna form the forearm.","html":"The skeleton provides support, protects organs and supplies attachment sites for muscles. A bone does not actively shorten to create a <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>. Muscular <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> transmitted through tendons turns bones around joints. Learn locations together with a sporting action rather than memorising a disconnected list. The humerus forms the upper arm; radius and ulna form the forearm.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"The femur is the thigh bone, the patella lies at the front of the knee, and tibia and fibula form the lower leg. The pelvis connects trunk and lower limbs. Sternum and ribs protect the thorax. Carpals form the wrist, metacarpals the palm and phalanges the fingers. Tarsals form the rear and mid foot, metatarsals the forefoot and phalanges the toes.","html":"The femur is the thigh bone, the patella lies at the front of the knee, and tibia and fibula form the lower leg. The pelvis connects trunk and lower limbs. Sternum and ribs protect the thorax. Carpals form the wrist, metacarpals the palm and phalanges the fingers. Tarsals form the rear and mid foot, metatarsals the forefoot and phalanges the toes.","diagram":"skeleton"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"In a basketball pass, the humerus and forearm bones move while the trunk supplies a stable base. Naming a bone alone does not identify the joint action or the muscle producing it. When a drawing is simplified, use anatomical position as the reference: standing upright, palms facing forward. Radius is on the thumb side in that position.","html":"In a basketball pass, the humerus and forearm bones move while the trunk supplies a stable base. Naming a bone alone does not identify the joint action or the <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> producing it. When a drawing is simplified, use anatomical position as the reference: standing upright, palms facing forward. Radius is on the thumb side in that position.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Practise tracing the sequence from pelvis through femur, tibia and tarsals to the toes, then from humerus to the fingers. Use location plus function in an explanation. Distinguish a bone from a muscle, ligament or tendon. Ligaments connect bones at joints; tendons transmit muscle force to bone. This lesson identifies structures, not injuries.","html":"Practise tracing the sequence from pelvis through femur, tibia and tarsals to the toes, then from humerus to the fingers. Use location plus function in an explanation. Distinguish a bone from a <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark>, ligament or tendon. Ligaments connect bones at joints; tendons transmit muscle <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> to bone. This lesson identifies structures, not injuries.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-bones-w1","prompt":"A student labels a basketball player’s upper arm radius and the lower leg femur. Correct both labels and name the two forearm bones.","steps":["Upper arm: humerus, not radius.","Lower leg: tibia and fibula, not femur; femur is in the thigh.","The forearm contains radius and ulna. Radius is on the thumb side in anatomical position."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-bones-p1","prompt":"Put these structures in order from wrist to fingertip and ankle region to toe: phalanges, metatarsals, carpals, tarsals, metacarpals. Explain how bones contribute to a pass.","steps":["Wrist to fingertip: carpals → metacarpals → phalanges.","Ankle region to toes: tarsals → metatarsals → phalanges.","Muscles pull through tendons to rotate bones around joints; bones provide support and leverage. They do not contract themselves."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-bones-p2","prompt":"Apply locate the bones that support movement to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: The skeleton provides support, protects organs and supplies attachment sites for muscles. A bone does not actively shorten to create a movement. Muscular force transmitted through tendons turns bones around joints. Learn locations together with a sporting action rather than memorising a disconnected list. The humerus forms the upper arm; radius and ulna form the forearm.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: A schematic shows relationships, not a diagnostic anatomical image.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on locate the bones that support movement?","o":["Upper arm: humerus, not radius.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Upper arm: humerus, not radius. A schematic shows relationships, not a diagnostic anatomical image."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["A schematic shows relationships, not a diagnostic anatomical image.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"A schematic shows relationships, not a diagnostic anatomical image. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-muscles-upper","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Upper-body muscles and attachments","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Upper-body muscles and attachments</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Skeletal muscles pull on attachments when they develop tension. Biceps brachii is associated with elbow flexion; triceps brachii with elbow extension. Deltoid contributes to shoulder abduction, pectorals to movements bringing the arm across the body, and latissimus dorsi to shoulder extension and adduction. Actions depend on which fibres act and the starting position.","html":"Skeletal muscles pull on attachments when they develop <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark>. Biceps brachii is associated with elbow flexion; triceps brachii with elbow extension. Deltoid contributes to shoulder abduction, pectorals to movements bringing the arm across the body, and latissimus dorsi to shoulder extension and adduction. Actions depend on which fibres act and the starting position.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Trapezius acts on the shoulder girdle rather than simply bending the elbow. Abdominal muscles help control the trunk. Origin conventionally names the less mobile attachment and insertion the more mobile one. During a closed-chain action such as a chin-up, which attachment moves most can change. These labels do not mean that one attachment is always motionless.","html":"Trapezius acts on the shoulder girdle rather than simply bending the elbow. Abdominal muscles help control the trunk. Origin conventionally names the less mobile attachment and insertion the more mobile one. During a closed-chain action such as a chin-up, which attachment moves most can change. These labels do not mean that one attachment is always motionless.","diagram":"antagonists"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"An antagonist produces an opposing joint action to an agonist. During a simple elbow-flexion action, biceps contributes as an agonist while triceps opposes flexion. Antagonists can co-contract to stabilise a joint; they do not always completely switch off. A muscle can perform different roles in different movements. Explain the role in the actual phase observed.","html":"An antagonist produces an opposing joint action to an agonist. During a simple elbow-flexion action, biceps contributes as an agonist while triceps opposes flexion. Antagonists can co-contract to stabilise a joint; they do not always completely switch off. A <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> can perform different roles in different movements. Explain the role in the actual phase observed.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"A push-up rising phase includes elbow extension by triceps and shoulder actions involving pectorals. A single-muscle explanation misses the coordinated nature of the task. Identify the joint, the direction of motion and the relevant muscle action before describing attachments. Do not infer exact muscle activity or force solely from an external photograph.","html":"A push-up rising phase includes elbow extension by triceps and shoulder actions involving pectorals. A single-<mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> explanation misses the coordinated nature of the task. Identify the joint, the direction of motion and the relevant muscle action before describing attachments. Do not infer exact muscle activity or <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> solely from an external photograph.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-muscles-upper-w1","prompt":"Explain why triceps can be an agonist in a chest pass but an antagonist during an elbow curl.","steps":["The role depends on the joint action and movement phase, not a permanent label.","During elbow extension in the pass, triceps produces the action and acts as an agonist.","During elbow flexion in a curl, biceps contributes to flexion and triceps produces the opposing extension action. Co-contraction may also stabilise the joint."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-muscles-upper-p1","prompt":"A student says insertion always stays still and muscles push bones. Correct the claim and identify an upper-body muscle relevant to shoulder abduction.","steps":["Muscles develop pulling tension through attachments; they do not push bones away.","Insertion conventionally refers to the more mobile attachment, but actual movement depends on whether the action is open or closed chain.","Deltoid contributes to shoulder abduction; state the joint and direction rather than claiming it produces all shoulder movement alone."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-muscles-upper-p2","prompt":"Apply upper-body muscles and attachments to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Skeletal muscles pull on attachments when they develop tension. Biceps brachii is associated with elbow flexion; triceps brachii with elbow extension. Deltoid contributes to shoulder abduction, pectorals to movements bringing the arm across the body, and latissimus dorsi to shoulder extension and adduction. Actions depend on which fibres act and the starting position.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Visible motion does not prove which individual fibres are active.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on upper-body muscles and attachments?","o":["The role depends on the joint action and movement phase, not a permanent label.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The role depends on the joint action and movement phase, not a permanent label. Visible motion does not prove which individual fibres are active."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Visible motion does not prove which individual fibres are active.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Visible motion does not prove which individual fibres are active. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-muscles-lower","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Lower-body muscles in running and jumping","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Lower-body muscles in running and jumping</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Quadriceps are at the front of the thigh and contribute to knee extension. Hamstrings lie posteriorly and contribute to knee flexion and hip extension. Gluteus maximus contributes to hip extension; hip flexors contribute to hip flexion. Adductors draw the thigh towards the midline. Some muscles cross more than one joint, so their role depends on both joint positions.","html":"Quadriceps are at the front of the thigh and contribute to knee extension. Hamstrings lie posteriorly and contribute to knee flexion and hip extension. Gluteus maximus contributes to hip extension; hip flexors contribute to hip flexion. Adductors draw the thigh towards the midline. Some muscles cross more than one joint, so their role depends on both joint positions.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Gastrocnemius and soleus contribute to plantarflexion at the ankle. Gastrocnemius also crosses the knee; soleus does not. Tibialis anterior contributes to dorsiflexion, lifting the forefoot towards the shin. During a jump, coordinated hip and knee extension and ankle plantarflexion contribute to propulsion. This is an action sequence, not proof that each muscle reaches maximum force simultaneously.","html":"Gastrocnemius and soleus contribute to plantarflexion at the ankle. Gastrocnemius also crosses the knee; soleus does not. Tibialis anterior contributes to dorsiflexion, lifting the forefoot towards the shin. During a jump, coordinated hip and knee extension and ankle plantarflexion contribute to propulsion. This is an action sequence, not proof that each <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> reaches maximum <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> simultaneously.","diagram":"joint-actions"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"During landing, quadriceps can be active while lengthening to control knee flexion. The joint moves into flexion even though knee extensors develop force. This eccentric action is different from claiming the muscle must shorten whenever it works. Isometric tension can also stabilise a position with little change in joint angle.","html":"During landing, quadriceps can be active while lengthening to control knee flexion. The joint moves into flexion even though knee extensors develop <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>. This eccentric action is different from claiming the <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> must shorten whenever it works. Isometric <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> can also stabilise a position with little change in joint angle.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Analyse take-off and landing as different phases. State a muscle, the joint it influences, the action and whether the muscle mainly shortens, lengthens under load or holds approximately steady. Use the whole chain from trunk through hip, knee and ankle. Practical technique should be developed with the teacher; text alone cannot establish safe landing proficiency.","html":"Analyse take-off and landing as different phases. State a <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark>, the joint it influences, the action and whether the muscle mainly shortens, lengthens under load or holds approximately steady. Use the whole chain from trunk through hip, knee and ankle. Practical technique should be developed with the teacher; text alone cannot establish safe landing proficiency.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-muscles-lower-w1","prompt":"Compare quadriceps action in upward jump propulsion and controlled landing.","steps":["During propulsion the knee extends; quadriceps can shorten concentrically to contribute to extension.","During a controlled landing the knee flexes while quadriceps develops tension and lengthens eccentrically.","The muscle controls deceleration; active does not always mean shortening. Hip and ankle actions also contribute."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-muscles-lower-p1","prompt":"Identify the ankle action and relevant muscles when a runner pushes off, then when the forefoot is raised. Name a hip extensor and hip flexor group.","steps":["Push-off involves plantarflexion, with gastrocnemius and soleus contributing.","Raising the forefoot involves dorsiflexion, with tibialis anterior contributing.","Gluteus maximus contributes to hip extension; hip flexors contribute to hip flexion. Roles vary with movement phase and joint positions."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-muscles-lower-p2","prompt":"Apply lower-body muscles in running and jumping to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Quadriceps are at the front of the thigh and contribute to knee extension. Hamstrings lie posteriorly and contribute to knee flexion and hip extension. Gluteus maximus contributes to hip extension; hip flexors contribute to hip flexion. Adductors draw the thigh towards the midline. Some muscles cross more than one joint, so their role depends on both joint positions.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: These examples explain actions; they are not an exercise prescription.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on lower-body muscles in running and jumping?","o":["During propulsion the knee extends; quadriceps can shorten concentrically to contribute to extension.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: During propulsion the knee extends; quadriceps can shorten concentrically to contribute to extension. These examples explain actions; they are not an exercise prescription."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["These examples explain actions; they are not an exercise prescription.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"These examples explain actions; they are not an exercise prescription. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-properties","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Muscle properties and opposing pairs","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Muscle properties and opposing pairs</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Excitability is the ability of muscle tissue to respond to stimulation. Contractility is the ability to develop tension and shorten when conditions allow. Extensibility is the ability to be stretched, and elasticity is the tendency to return towards original length after the stretching force is removed. These properties describe tissue behaviour rather than four separate training methods.","html":"Excitability is the ability of <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> tissue to respond to stimulation. Contractility is the ability to develop <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> and shorten when conditions allow. Extensibility is the ability to be stretched, and elasticity is the tendency to return towards original length after the stretching <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> is removed. These properties describe tissue behaviour rather than four separate training methods.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A nerve signal can initiate activation, but the resulting joint movement depends on the external load, muscle length and coordination. In an isometric action the muscle develops force without an appreciable change in overall muscle length. Contractility therefore does not imply that every active muscle visibly shortens in every task.","html":"A nerve signal can initiate activation, but the resulting joint <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> depends on the external load, <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> length and coordination. In an isometric action the muscle develops <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> without an appreciable change in overall muscle length. Contractility therefore does not imply that every active muscle visibly shortens in every task.","diagram":"antagonists"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Antagonistic pairs create opposing actions around a joint. Biceps and triceps are a familiar elbow example; quadriceps and hamstrings provide a knee example. During stabilisation both sides may activate. Tendons transmit tension, while ligaments contribute passive joint restraint. Mixing these structures leads to an incorrect explanation of how the movement is produced.","html":"Antagonistic pairs create opposing actions around a joint. Biceps and triceps are a familiar elbow example; quadriceps and hamstrings provide a knee example. During stabilisation both sides may activate. Tendons transmit <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark>, while ligaments contribute passive joint restraint. Mixing these structures leads to an incorrect explanation of how the <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> is produced.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain a sport action in an ordered chain: stimulation leads to muscle tension, that tension pulls through an attachment, and the resulting torque can move or stabilise a joint. Link each property to the correct part of the chain. Elasticity is not the same as being extremely flexible, and excitability does not mean emotional excitement.","html":"Explain a sport action in an ordered chain: stimulation leads to <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark>, that tension pulls through an attachment, and the resulting torque can move or stabilise a joint. Link each property to the correct part of the chain. Elasticity is not the same as being extremely flexible, and excitability does not mean emotional excitement.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-properties-w1","prompt":"An athlete holds an elbow angle steady against a load. Does absence of visible shortening show the muscle is inactive? Explain.","steps":["No. An isometric action can develop tension with little overall length change.","Excitability permits response to a stimulus and contractility permits force development.","The muscle’s torque can balance the external torque, maintaining the angle; opposing muscles may also contribute to stability."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-properties-p1","prompt":"Match ability to respond, develop tension, be stretched and return towards original length to the four properties. Explain one antagonist pair.","steps":["Respond: excitability; develop tension: contractility; be stretched: extensibility; return: elasticity.","At the elbow, biceps contributes to flexion and triceps to extension.","Their agonist and antagonist roles depend on the task; co-contraction can stabilise the joint."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-properties-p2","prompt":"Apply muscle properties and opposing pairs to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Excitability is the ability of muscle tissue to respond to stimulation. Contractility is the ability to develop tension and shorten when conditions allow. Extensibility is the ability to be stretched, and elasticity is the tendency to return towards original length after the stretching force is removed. These properties describe tissue behaviour rather than four separate training methods.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Overall muscle length and individual fibre behaviour are not always identical.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on muscle properties and opposing pairs?","o":["No. An isometric action can develop tension with little overall length change.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: No. An isometric action can develop tension with little overall length change. Overall muscle length and individual fibre behaviour are not always identical."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Overall muscle length and individual fibre behaviour are not always identical.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Overall muscle length and individual fibre behaviour are not always identical. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-joint-actions","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Name joint actions precisely","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Name joint actions precisely</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Flexion generally reduces the angle between adjoining bones and extension increases it. Abduction moves away from the body’s midline, while adduction moves towards it. Rotation turns a segment about its long axis. Circumduction combines actions so the distal end traces a circle; it is not simply another word for rotation. Always identify the joint as well as the action.","html":"Flexion generally reduces the angle between adjoining bones and extension increases it. Abduction moves away from the body’s midline, while adduction moves towards it. Rotation turns a segment about its long axis. Circumduction combines actions so the distal end traces a circle; it is not simply another word for rotation. Always identify the joint as well as the action.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Pronation and supination describe forearm rotation relative to anatomical position. With the elbow held at the side, supination turns the palm towards an upward orientation and pronation turns it towards downward. Dorsiflexion brings the top of the foot towards the shin, while plantarflexion points the foot away. These terms cannot be assigned from a single ambiguous camera view without context.","html":"Pronation and supination describe forearm rotation relative to anatomical position. With the elbow held at the side, supination turns the palm towards an upward orientation and pronation turns it towards downward. Dorsiflexion brings the top of the foot towards the shin, while plantarflexion points the foot away. These terms cannot be assigned from a single ambiguous camera view without context.","diagram":"joint-actions"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A shoulder may flex while the elbow extends during part of a pass. Saying the whole arm extends blurs two different joint actions. A throw also involves trunk rotation and coordinated lower-limb actions. Describing the whole skill requires its phases and relevant joints, not one global movement label.","html":"A shoulder may flex while the elbow extends during part of a pass. Saying the whole arm extends blurs two different joint actions. A throw also involves trunk rotation and coordinated lower-limb actions. Describing the whole skill requires its phases and relevant joints, not one global <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> label.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Use a before-and-after description to decide the direction. Ask which segment moves relative to which, locate the reference position and check the plane. A motion can combine several actions. The named action describes movement, not necessarily which muscle is shortening; controlling an external load may require eccentric work by an opposing action group.","html":"Use a before-and-after description to decide the direction. Ask which segment moves relative to which, locate the reference position and check the plane. A motion can combine several actions. The named action describes <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>, not necessarily which <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> is shortening; controlling an external load may require eccentric work by an opposing action group.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-joint-actions-w1","prompt":"During a pass the elbow straightens while the upper arm moves forward at the shoulder. Name both actions and explain why one whole-arm label is inadequate.","steps":["Elbow straightening is extension.","Forward elevation of the upper arm in this stated sagittal movement is shoulder flexion.","Different joints perform different actions, so a single arm-extension label loses the distinction."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-joint-actions-p1","prompt":"Distinguish forearm pronation from shoulder rotation, and circumduction from plantarflexion. Give an adduction example.","steps":["Pronation is forearm rotation turning the palm from the supinated reference towards the pronated orientation. Shoulder rotation concerns the humerus about its long axis.","Circumduction traces a circular path through combined joint actions; plantarflexion points the foot away from the shin.","Bringing an abducted arm back towards the trunk is shoulder adduction."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-joint-actions-p2","prompt":"Apply name joint actions precisely to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Flexion generally reduces the angle between adjoining bones and extension increases it. Abduction moves away from the body’s midline, while adduction moves towards it. Rotation turns a segment about its long axis. Circumduction combines actions so the distal end traces a circle; it is not simply another word for rotation. Always identify the joint as well as the action.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Direction must be specified relative to anatomical position and the joint.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on name joint actions precisely?","o":["Elbow straightening is extension.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Elbow straightening is extension. Direction must be specified relative to anatomical position and the joint."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Direction must be specified relative to anatomical position and the joint.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Direction must be specified relative to anatomical position and the joint. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-circulation","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Trace circulation through heart and vessels","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Trace circulation through heart and vessels</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"The circulatory system transports oxygen, nutrients and other materials and helps distribute heat. The right side of the heart pumps blood towards the lungs; the left side pumps it to the body. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins return it. Direction, rather than oxygen content, defines these vessel categories.","html":"The circulatory system transports <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark>, nutrients and other materials and helps distribute heat. The right side of the heart pumps blood towards the lungs; the left side pumps it to the body. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins return it. Direction, rather than oxygen content, defines these vessel categories.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle towards the lungs. Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood to the left atrium. Systemic arteries then deliver blood from the left ventricle to tissues. Capillaries provide thin exchange surfaces between blood and tissues; haemoglobin in red blood cells carries much of the blood’s oxygen.","html":"Pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle towards the lungs. Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood to the left atrium. Systemic arteries then deliver blood from the left ventricle to tissues. Capillaries provide thin exchange surfaces between blood and tissues; haemoglobin in red blood cells carries much of the blood’s <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark>.","diagram":"circulation"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"During exercise, active muscles need more oxygen delivery and waste transport. Increased cardiac output and changed distribution support these demands. Blood flow to skin may also rise to assist heat loss. It is inaccurate to say all blood goes to muscle or that blood simply becomes completely oxygen-free after one circuit.","html":"During exercise, active muscles need more <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark> delivery and waste transport. Increased cardiac output and changed distribution support these demands. Blood flow to skin may also rise to assist heat loss. It is inaccurate to say all blood goes to <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> or that blood simply becomes completely oxygen-free after one circuit.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Trace a red blood cell through the full circuit: right heart, lungs, left heart, body and back to the right heart. Separate the pump from the exchange site. Explain oxygen loading at pulmonary capillaries and unloading at systemic tissues through gradients. Do not infer an athlete’s heart health from a classroom heart-rate value alone.","html":"Trace a red blood cell through the full circuit: right heart, lungs, left heart, body and back to the right heart. Separate the pump from the exchange site. Explain <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark> loading at pulmonary capillaries and unloading at systemic tissues through gradients. Do not infer an athlete’s heart health from a classroom heart-rate value alone.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-circulation-w1","prompt":"Correct: all arteries carry oxygenated blood. Trace blood from the right ventricle to the left ventricle.","steps":["Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart.","Right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lung capillaries → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle.","Pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood and pulmonary veins oxygenated blood."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-circulation-p1","prompt":"Explain how a capillary differs functionally from an artery and why muscles need increased circulation during exercise.","steps":["An artery carries blood away from the heart; capillaries form exchange networks with thin walls and close tissue contact.","Active muscle requires increased oxygen and substrate delivery and removal of metabolic products.","Higher cardiac output and redistribution support the demand; skin circulation can also contribute to heat regulation."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-circulation-p2","prompt":"Apply trace circulation through heart and vessels to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: The circulatory system transports oxygen, nutrients and other materials and helps distribute heat. The right side of the heart pumps blood towards the lungs; the left side pumps it to the body. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins return it. Direction, rather than oxygen content, defines these vessel categories.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Blood oxygen content is graded, not simply present or absent.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on trace circulation through heart and vessels?","o":["Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart. Blood oxygen content is graded, not simply present or absent."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Blood oxygen content is graded, not simply present or absent.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Blood oxygen content is graded, not simply present or absent. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-breathing","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Ventilation and gas exchange","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Ventilation and gas exchange</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Ventilation moves air into and out of lungs; gas exchange moves gases across respiratory surfaces. During quiet inhalation, the diaphragm contracts and descends. Thoracic volume rises and pressure inside the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure, drawing air inward. This sequence is mechanical and does not mean the lungs actively suck by muscular contraction of lung tissue.","html":"Ventilation moves air into and out of lungs; gas exchange moves gases across respiratory surfaces. During quiet inhalation, the diaphragm contracts and descends. Thoracic volume rises and <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> inside the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure, drawing air inward. This sequence is mechanical and does not mean the lungs actively suck by muscular contraction of lung tissue.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"During quiet exhalation the diaphragm relaxes and elastic recoil reduces lung volume. Pressure rises relative to the atmosphere and air flows outward. Forceful exhalation during exercise can involve additional muscles. In the alveoli, oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse down partial-pressure gradients across a thin exchange surface into or out of adjacent capillary blood.","html":"During quiet exhalation the diaphragm relaxes and elastic recoil reduces lung volume. <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> rises relative to the atmosphere and air flows outward. Forceful exhalation during exercise can involve additional muscles. In the alveoli, <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark> and carbon dioxide diffuse down partial-pressure gradients across a thin exchange surface into or out of adjacent capillary blood.","diagram":"ventilation"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Breathing rate and tidal volume generally rise with exercise intensity, increasing minute ventilation. Minute ventilation equals breathing frequency multiplied by tidal volume. This total airflow is not the same as oxygen consumed: some air occupies conducting passages and gas exchange depends on perfusion and gradients as well as ventilation.","html":"Breathing rate and tidal volume generally rise with exercise intensity, increasing minute ventilation. Minute ventilation equals breathing frequency multiplied by tidal volume. This total airflow is not the same as <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark> consumed: some air occupies conducting passages and gas exchange depends on perfusion and gradients as well as ventilation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain movement of air with volume, pressure and direction in that order. Explain gas exchange separately with surface area, short diffusion distance and gradients. A deeper breath is not proof of greater aerobic fitness, and exercise adaptations should not be explained as adult lungs simply growing larger.","html":"Explain <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> of air with volume, <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> and direction in that order. Explain gas exchange separately with surface area, short diffusion distance and gradients. A deeper breath is not proof of greater aerobic fitness, and exercise adaptations should not be explained as adult lungs simply growing larger.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-breathing-w1","prompt":"Breathing frequency is 24 breaths/min and tidal volume 1.5 L/breath. Calculate minute ventilation and explain quiet inhalation.","steps":["24 × 1.5 = 36 L/min.","Diaphragm contracts and descends, increasing thoracic volume.","Pressure falls relative to atmospheric pressure, so air moves inward; ventilation is distinct from oxygen uptake."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-breathing-p1","prompt":"Compare quiet exhalation with inhalation and explain why 36 L/min airflow is not 36 L/min oxygen consumption.","steps":["Quiet exhalation involves diaphragm relaxation and elastic recoil; volume falls, pressure rises and air flows outward.","Inhalation involves diaphragm contraction, increased volume and reduced pressure.","Air contains mixed gases, some airflow ventilates conducting spaces, and only part of delivered oxygen is taken up. Gas exchange also depends on perfusion and gradients."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-breathing-p2","prompt":"Apply ventilation and gas exchange to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Ventilation moves air into and out of lungs; gas exchange moves gases across respiratory surfaces. During quiet inhalation, the diaphragm contracts and descends. Thoracic volume rises and pressure inside the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure, drawing air inward. This sequence is mechanical and does not mean the lungs actively suck by muscular contraction of lung tissue.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: This is normal respiratory physiology, not assessment of breathing symptoms.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on ventilation and gas exchange?","o":["24 × 1.5 = 36 L/min.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: 24 × 1.5 = 36 L/min. This is normal respiratory physiology, not assessment of breathing symptoms."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["This is normal respiratory physiology, not assessment of breathing symptoms.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"This is normal respiratory physiology, not assessment of breathing symptoms. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-acute","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Acute exercise responses","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Acute exercise responses</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"An acute response occurs during or shortly after a bout of exercise. Heart rate usually rises, stroke volume often rises and cardiac output increases to support working tissues. Systolic blood pressure generally rises in dynamic exercise, while the diastolic response depends on the task and conditions. Avoid the blanket statement that both pressures always increase by the same amount.","html":"An acute response occurs during or shortly after a bout of exercise. Heart rate usually rises, stroke volume often rises and cardiac output increases to support working tissues. Systolic blood <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> generally rises in dynamic exercise, while the diastolic response depends on the task and conditions. Avoid the blanket statement that both pressures always increase by the same amount.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Cardiac output is heart rate multiplied by stroke volume. With stroke volume in millilitres per beat, the product is millilitres per minute; divide by 1000 for litres per minute. Respiratory rate and tidal volume support increased ventilation. Perspiration and changes in skin blood flow help manage heat but depend on temperature, humidity and the person.","html":"Cardiac output is heart rate multiplied by stroke volume. With stroke volume in millilitres per beat, the product is millilitres per minute; divide by 1000 for litres per minute. Respiratory rate and tidal volume support increased ventilation. Perspiration and changes in skin blood flow help manage heat but depend on temperature, humidity and the person.","diagram":"cardiac-output"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Blood is redistributed towards active muscles and, when needed, skin. Other organ flow changes vary; it does not cease altogether. The immediate response is different from the long-term adaptation obtained from repeated training. A high heart rate in one fictional task cannot by itself diagnose dehydration, fitness level or illness.","html":"Blood is redistributed towards active muscles and, when needed, skin. Other organ flow changes vary; it does not cease altogether. The immediate response is different from the long-term adaptation obtained from repeated training. A high heart rate in one fictional task cannot by itself diagnose dehydration, fitness level or illness.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Interpret data using the activity intensity, environment and measurement timing. State a supported pattern, calculate where appropriate and explain the physiological mechanism. Separate a description, such as heart rate increased, from its functional explanation, such as delivering a greater volume of blood per minute to meet demand.","html":"Interpret data using the activity intensity, environment and measurement timing. State a supported pattern, calculate where appropriate and explain the physiological mechanism. Separate a description, such as heart rate increased, from its functional explanation, such as delivering a greater volume of blood per minute to meet demand.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"cardiac"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-acute-w1","prompt":"Heart rate rises from 60 to 150 beats/min and stroke volume from 80 to 120 mL/beat. Calculate cardiac output at both points.","steps":["Rest: 60 × 80 = 4800 mL/min = 4.8 L/min.","Exercise: 150 × 120 = 18000 mL/min = 18 L/min.","Output increases by 13.2 L/min, supporting greater oxygen transport and substrate delivery. Both factors contribute."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-acute-p1","prompt":"A player’s breathing rate and perspiration rise in a warm match. Explain two different functions and one limit on interpreting these observations.","steps":["Increased ventilation supports gas exchange as metabolic demand increases.","Sweat evaporation can remove heat; sweating alone is not proof that all sweat evaporates.","Responses depend on workload and environment. These observations alone do not establish hydration status, fitness or a medical diagnosis."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-acute-p2","prompt":"Apply acute exercise responses to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: An acute response occurs during or shortly after a bout of exercise. Heart rate usually rises, stroke volume often rises and cardiac output increases to support working tissues. Systolic blood pressure generally rises in dynamic exercise, while the diastolic response depends on the task and conditions. Avoid the blanket statement that both pressures always increase by the same amount.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: All values in the worked data are invented for learning.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on acute exercise responses?","o":["Rest: 60 × 80 = 4800 mL/min = 4.8 L/min.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Rest: 60 × 80 = 4800 mL/min = 4.8 L/min. All values in the worked data are invented for learning."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["All values in the worked data are invented for learning.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"All values in the worked data are invented for learning. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-chronic","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Chronic cardiovascular and respiratory adaptations","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Chronic cardiovascular and respiratory adaptations</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Chronic adaptations develop through repeated training over time. Endurance training can increase stroke volume through structural and functional cardiac adaptations and greater blood volume. Resting heart rate may fall because an adequate resting cardiac output can be supplied with fewer beats. Cardiac hypertrophy in this context means a training-related adaptation, not that every enlarged heart is healthy.","html":"Chronic adaptations develop through repeated training over time. Endurance training can increase stroke volume through structural and functional cardiac adaptations and greater blood volume. Resting heart rate may fall because an adequate resting cardiac output can be supplied with fewer beats. Cardiac hypertrophy in this context means a training-related adaptation, not that every enlarged heart is healthy.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Greater capillary supply and oxidative capacity in trained muscle support oxygen delivery and use. Maximal oxygen uptake can improve, although response varies with initial fitness, training and other factors. Total haemoglobin mass and blood volume can change; this should not be confused with assuming haemoglobin concentration must always increase because concentration also depends on plasma volume.","html":"Greater capillary supply and oxidative capacity in trained <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> support <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark> delivery and use. Maximal oxygen uptake can improve, although response varies with initial fitness, training and other factors. Total haemoglobin mass and blood volume can change; this should not be confused with assuming haemoglobin concentration must always increase because concentration also depends on plasma volume.","diagram":"cardiac-output"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Ventilatory efficiency and respiratory-muscle function can improve. At the same submaximal workload a trained athlete may experience lower relative strain. Do not explain this as the lungs necessarily becoming anatomically much bigger. Blood-pressure adaptations depend on the person and baseline values; a training programme is not a guarantee of an identical pressure change for everyone.","html":"Ventilatory efficiency and respiratory-<mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> function can improve. At the same submaximal workload a trained athlete may experience lower relative strain. Do not explain this as the lungs necessarily becoming anatomically much bigger. Blood-<mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> adaptations depend on the person and baseline values; a training programme is not a guarantee of an identical pressure change for everyone.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"To identify adaptation, compare comparable measurements before and after a period of training. Control relative workload, environmental conditions and timing. A heart-rate difference measured in different activities is not enough. Explain the link between a changed structure or function and a performance demand while acknowledging other possible influences.","html":"To identify adaptation, compare comparable measurements before and after a period of training. Control relative workload, environmental conditions and timing. A heart-rate difference measured in different activities is not enough. Explain the link between a changed structure or function and a performance demand while acknowledging other possible influences.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"cardiac"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-chronic-w1","prompt":"Before training: resting HR 72 and SV 70 mL. Afterwards: HR 60 and SV 84 mL. Compare resting cardiac output.","steps":["Before: 72 × 70 = 5040 mL/min.","After: 60 × 84 = 5040 mL/min.","Output is unchanged at 5.04 L/min in this fictional dataset; greater SV permits fewer beats. Lower HR does not imply less necessary resting blood delivery."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-chronic-p1","prompt":"A student claims aerobic training guarantees larger lungs and higher haemoglobin concentration. Replace this with a qualified explanation of two adaptations.","steps":["Improved stroke volume and blood volume can support circulation; resting heart rate may decrease.","Muscle capillarisation and oxidative capacity can support oxygen use. Ventilatory function may become more efficient without requiring greatly enlarged adult lungs.","Haemoglobin mass and concentration differ; plasma-volume changes influence concentration. Individual adaptation varies."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-chronic-p2","prompt":"Apply chronic cardiovascular and respiratory adaptations to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Chronic adaptations develop through repeated training over time. Endurance training can increase stroke volume through structural and functional cardiac adaptations and greater blood volume. Resting heart rate may fall because an adequate resting cardiac output can be supplied with fewer beats. Cardiac hypertrophy in this context means a training-related adaptation, not that every enlarged heart is healthy.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Training responses vary; the lesson does not interpret clinical results.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on chronic cardiovascular and respiratory adaptations?","o":["Before: 72 × 70 = 5040 mL/min.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Before: 72 × 70 = 5040 mL/min. Training responses vary; the lesson does not interpret clinical results."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Training responses vary; the lesson does not interpret clinical results.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Training responses vary; the lesson does not interpret clinical results. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-nutrients","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Macronutrients as exercise fuels and building materials","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Macronutrients as exercise fuels and building materials</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Carbohydrate, fat and protein are macronutrients with different roles. Carbohydrate supplies glucose and stored glycogen, supporting high-rate energy provision as well as aerobic metabolism. Fat stores substantial energy and is an important aerobic fuel. Protein provides amino acids for tissue repair and many functional molecules; its main sporting role is not simply being the preferred fuel for every sprint.","html":"Carbohydrate, fat and protein are macronutrients with different roles. Carbohydrate supplies glucose and stored glycogen, supporting high-rate energy provision as well as aerobic metabolism. Fat stores substantial energy and is an important aerobic fuel. Protein provides amino acids for tissue repair and many functional molecules; its main sporting role is not simply being the preferred fuel for every sprint.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"The body continually resynthesises ATP, the immediate energy currency used by contracting muscle. Food nutrients are not directly interchangeable with ATP at the contractile machinery. Their oxidation and other metabolic pathways contribute to replenishing ATP. Relative carbohydrate and fat contribution changes with intensity, duration, diet and training; both can contribute during the same activity.","html":"The body continually resynthesises <mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark>, the immediate energy currency used by contracting <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark>. Food nutrients are not directly interchangeable with ATP at the contractile machinery. Their oxidation and other metabolic pathways contribute to replenishing ATP. Relative carbohydrate and fat contribution changes with intensity, duration, diet and training; both can contribute during the same activity.","diagram":"energy"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A prolonged match involves repeated intense efforts and lower-intensity periods. Stored carbohydrate can be important for maintaining repeated work, while adequate protein supports recovery and adaptation. Fat is not useless in sport, and removing a nutrient group is not a sound deduction from a simplified energy-system table. Nutrient requirements are individual.","html":"A prolonged match involves repeated intense efforts and lower-intensity periods. Stored carbohydrate can be important for maintaining repeated work, while adequate protein supports <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> and adaptation. Fat is not useless in sport, and removing a nutrient group is not a sound deduction from a simplified energy-system table. Nutrient requirements are individual.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"For an educational response, identify the demand, the nutrient’s role and timing considerations, without prescribing a personal diet. Distinguish fuel availability from muscle repair. Examine a claim such as protein powder automatically improves performance by asking whether it addresses a real dietary need and whether the wider training and eating pattern is appropriate.","html":"For an educational response, identify the demand, the nutrient’s role and timing considerations, without prescribing a personal diet. Distinguish fuel availability from <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> repair. Examine a claim such as protein powder automatically improves performance by asking whether it addresses a real dietary need and whether the wider training and eating pattern is appropriate.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-nutrients-w1","prompt":"Explain why a repeated-sprint player needs more than the claim protein builds muscle.","steps":["Carbohydrate availability supports repeated high-rate ATP resynthesis and glycogen stores.","Fat contributes aerobic fuel, especially during lower-intensity work; all systems overlap.","Protein supplies amino acids for repair and adaptation, but extra protein is not a substitute for adequate energy, training and recovery."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-nutrients-p1","prompt":"Critique: fat supplies no energy in exercise and carbohydrate can be replaced entirely by a protein shake.","steps":["Fat contributes energy through aerobic metabolism. Its relative contribution depends on intensity and duration.","Carbohydrate supports glucose and glycogen availability, particularly important during demanding repeated efforts.","Protein has repair and functional roles; one shake is not evidence of a complete diet or a universal replacement for carbohydrate."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-nutrients-p2","prompt":"Apply macronutrients as exercise fuels and building materials to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Carbohydrate, fat and protein are macronutrients with different roles. Carbohydrate supplies glucose and stored glycogen, supporting high-rate energy provision as well as aerobic metabolism. Fat stores substantial energy and is an important aerobic fuel. Protein provides amino acids for tissue repair and many functional molecules; its main sporting role is not simply being the preferred fuel for every sprint.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Nutrition examples teach mechanisms, not personal meal plans.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on macronutrients as exercise fuels and building materials?","o":["Carbohydrate availability supports repeated high-rate ATP resynthesis and glycogen stores.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Carbohydrate availability supports repeated high-rate ATP resynthesis and glycogen stores. Nutrition examples teach mechanisms, not personal meal plans."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Nutrition examples teach mechanisms, not personal meal plans.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Nutrition examples teach mechanisms, not personal meal plans. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-energy-systems","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Three overlapping energy systems","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Three overlapping energy systems</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"ATP must be resynthesised as muscle uses it. The ATP–CP system uses phosphocreatine to regenerate ATP rapidly and has limited capacity. Anaerobic glycolysis supplies ATP from carbohydrate without requiring oxygen directly in those steps. The syllabus also uses the label lactic acid system. Aerobic metabolism can use carbohydrate and fat with a comparatively large capacity.","html":"<mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark> must be resynthesised as <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> uses it. The ATP–CP system uses phosphocreatine to regenerate ATP rapidly and has limited capacity. Anaerobic glycolysis supplies ATP from carbohydrate without requiring <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark> directly in those steps. The syllabus also uses the label lactic acid system. Aerobic metabolism can use carbohydrate and fat with a comparatively large capacity.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"All three systems contribute from the beginning of activity. Dominance depends on intensity, duration, recovery and training rather than a timer that turns one system off and another on. A short maximal acceleration often relies heavily on ATP–CP, but that does not make aerobic contribution zero. Aerobic processes also support recovery between repeated efforts.","html":"All three systems contribute from the beginning of activity. Dominance depends on intensity, duration, <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> and training rather than a timer that turns one system off and another on. A short maximal acceleration often relies heavily on <mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark>–CP, but that does not make aerobic contribution zero. Aerobic processes also support recovery between repeated efforts.","diagram":"energy"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Lactate is a metabolic product and can be used as fuel. It is not simply a waste substance that independently explains all fatigue or delayed-onset muscle soreness. The rate of glycolytic ATP provision, associated metabolic changes and limits across systems affect performance. Explain energy supply separately from over-simplified claims about one fatigue chemical.","html":"Lactate is a metabolic product and can be used as fuel. It is not simply a waste substance that independently explains all fatigue or delayed-onset <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> soreness. The rate of glycolytic <mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark> provision, associated metabolic changes and limits across systems affect performance. Explain energy supply separately from over-simplified claims about one fatigue chemical.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Compare rate and capacity: rapid supply is useful for explosive efforts, while large capacity supports sustained work. Analyse a whole sporting sequence rather than assigning an entire sport to one system. A rally, pause and next rally have different demands. Recovery duration changes phosphocreatine restoration and the capacity to repeat intense efforts.","html":"Compare rate and capacity: rapid supply is useful for explosive efforts, while large capacity supports sustained work. Analyse a whole sporting sequence rather than assigning an entire sport to one system. A rally, pause and next rally have different demands. <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> duration changes phosphocreatine restoration and the capacity to repeat intense efforts.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-energy-systems-w1","prompt":"A tennis point lasts 6 seconds, followed by a pause and another point. Explain dominant supply and recovery without a switch model.","steps":["Rapid high-intensity actions may rely heavily on ATP–CP, with glycolytic and aerobic contributions also present.","Aerobic processes support recovery, including phosphocreatine restoration.","Repeated efforts and incomplete recovery can increase contributions from other pathways; no system switches completely off."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-energy-systems-p1","prompt":"Compare a maximal short acceleration and a steady prolonged run. Correct: lactate causes all fatigue and next-day soreness.","steps":["Acceleration has a high ATP demand rate with a major ATP–CP contribution. A prolonged run generally relies predominantly on aerobic ATP resynthesis.","All systems overlap in both activities; intensity and duration affect relative contributions.","Fatigue is multifactorial, lactate can be used as fuel, and delayed soreness is not explained by retained lactic acid."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-energy-systems-p2","prompt":"Apply three overlapping energy systems to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: ATP must be resynthesised as muscle uses it. The ATP–CP system uses phosphocreatine to regenerate ATP rapidly and has limited capacity. Anaerobic glycolysis supplies ATP from carbohydrate without requiring oxygen directly in those steps. The syllabus also uses the label lactic acid system. Aerobic metabolism can use carbohydrate and fat with a comparatively large capacity.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: No fixed time boundary perfectly separates the systems.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on three overlapping energy systems?","o":["Rapid high-intensity actions may rely heavily on ATP–CP, with glycolytic and aerobic contributions also present.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Rapid high-intensity actions may rely heavily on ATP–CP, with glycolytic and aerobic contributions also present. No fixed time boundary perfectly separates the systems."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["No fixed time boundary perfectly separates the systems.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"No fixed time boundary perfectly separates the systems. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-fitness","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Health and performance fitness components","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Health and performance fitness components</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Health-related components include cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition. Cardiorespiratory endurance supports sustained delivery and use of oxygen. Strength concerns high force capacity, whereas muscular endurance concerns repeated or sustained muscular work. Flexibility describes available joint range; body composition concerns relative body tissues, not a direct score for sporting talent.","html":"Health-related components include cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition. Cardiorespiratory endurance supports sustained delivery and use of <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark>. Strength concerns high <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> capacity, whereas muscular endurance concerns repeated or sustained muscular work. Flexibility describes available joint range; body composition concerns relative body tissues, not a direct score for sporting talent.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Performance-related components include agility, balance, coordination, reaction time, speed and power. Agility involves changing direction effectively, often with perceptual demands. Balance maintains equilibrium; coordination organises movement. Reaction time is the delay between a relevant stimulus and response initiation, while speed concerns rapid movement. Power concerns rate of work or energy transfer and depends on both force and velocity.","html":"Performance-related components include agility, balance, coordination, reaction time, speed and power. Agility involves changing direction effectively, often with perceptual demands. Balance maintains equilibrium; coordination organises <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>. Reaction time is the delay between a relevant stimulus and response initiation, while speed concerns rapid movement. Power concerns rate of work or energy <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark> and depends on both <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> and <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark>.","diagram":"fitness"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A netball interception requires recognising a pass, initiating a response, moving quickly and coordinating the catch. Several components interact. A test samples one aspect under particular conditions and cannot represent every game demand. Fast completion of a pre-planned shuttle is not the same as reacting to unpredictable opponents.","html":"A netball interception requires recognising a pass, initiating a response, moving quickly and coordinating the catch. Several components interact. A test samples one aspect under particular conditions and cannot represent every game demand. Fast completion of a pre-planned shuttle is not the same as reacting to unpredictable opponents.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Select a component by explaining the task demand rather than listing all eleven. Choose a repeatable assessment, explain validity and keep conditions comparable. Body-composition discussions should avoid ranking appearance or assuming that a particular physique guarantees health or performance. Use appropriately supervised school procedures for any practical measurements.","html":"Select a component by explaining the task demand rather than listing all eleven. Choose a repeatable assessment, explain validity and keep conditions comparable. Body-composition discussions should avoid ranking appearance or assuming that a particular physique guarantees health or performance. Use appropriately supervised school procedures for any practical measurements.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-fitness-w1","prompt":"A badminton player sees a shot, starts moving and changes direction to reach it. Distinguish reaction time, speed and agility.","steps":["Reaction time is the delay from the relevant stimulus to initiating the response.","Speed concerns how quickly the player moves.","Agility concerns effective direction change, including responding to the shot rather than merely running a fixed route."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-fitness-p1","prompt":"Compare muscular strength and endurance, then explain why one shuttle-run score cannot describe all basketball fitness.","steps":["Strength concerns capacity to produce high force; endurance concerns repeated or sustained muscular work.","Basketball also demands reaction, coordination, balance, speed, power and aerobic recovery capacity.","A shuttle result samples specific conditions and may omit decision demands, skill quality and other components."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-fitness-p2","prompt":"Apply health and performance fitness components to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Health-related components include cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition. Cardiorespiratory endurance supports sustained delivery and use of oxygen. Strength concerns high force capacity, whereas muscular endurance concerns repeated or sustained muscular work. Flexibility describes available joint range; body composition concerns relative body tissues, not a direct score for sporting talent.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: A fitness component is a capacity, not a complete performance prediction.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on health and performance fitness components?","o":["Reaction time is the delay from the relevant stimulus to initiating the response.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Reaction time is the delay from the relevant stimulus to initiating the response. A fitness component is a capacity, not a complete performance prediction."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["A fitness component is a capacity, not a complete performance prediction.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"A fitness component is a capacity, not a complete performance prediction. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-training-principles","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"FITT, overload, specificity and reversibility","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>FITT, overload, specificity and reversibility</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"FITT describes frequency, intensity, time and type of training. These variables give a programme enough detail to interpret its demands. Progressive overload means increasing an appropriate training stimulus over time as adaptation occurs; it does not mean making every session maximal or increasing all variables at once. Recovery is part of adaptation.","html":"FITT describes frequency, intensity, time and type of training. These variables give a programme enough detail to interpret its demands. Progressive overload means increasing an appropriate training stimulus over time as adaptation occurs; it does not mean making every session maximal or increasing all variables at once. <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> is part of adaptation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Specificity means adaptations reflect the demands trained, including muscles, energy supply, movement patterns and skills. A programme for a distance runner and one for repeated explosive jumps may share activities but need different emphases. Reversibility means some adaptations decline when the relevant training stimulus is reduced or removed; the rate and extent vary.","html":"Specificity means adaptations reflect the demands trained, including muscles, energy supply, <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> patterns and skills. A programme for a distance runner and one for repeated explosive jumps may share activities but need different emphases. Reversibility means some adaptations decline when the relevant training stimulus is reduced or removed; the rate and extent vary.","diagram":"training"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A fictional programme that suddenly doubles volume and adds maximal intensity lacks a reasoned progression. Before proposing a change, identify the goal, current demands, readiness and recovery. School tasks can evaluate written plans without asking students to carry them out. Actual activity should follow the teacher’s programme and appropriate supervision.","html":"A fictional programme that suddenly doubles volume and adds maximal intensity lacks a reasoned progression. Before proposing a change, identify the goal, current demands, readiness and <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark>. School tasks can evaluate written plans without asking students to carry them out. Actual activity should follow the teacher’s programme and appropriate supervision.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Justify a training choice by connecting stimulus to intended adaptation and sporting demand. Include how progress would be monitored and how the plan would be adjusted when fatigue or performance signals change. A generic harder is better statement ignores both specificity and the possibility that excessive load reduces useful performance.","html":"Justify a training choice by connecting stimulus to intended adaptation and sporting demand. Include how progress would be monitored and how the plan would be adjusted when fatigue or performance signals change. A generic harder is better statement ignores both specificity and the possibility that excessive load reduces useful performance.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-training-principles-w1","prompt":"A written plan changes from two moderate sessions to six maximal sessions immediately. Evaluate it using FITT and overload.","steps":["Frequency triples and intensity also rises sharply; session time and type must also be specified.","Progressive overload requires a reasoned gradual stimulus aligned to adaptation, rather than simultaneous extreme changes.","The plan lacks recovery and monitoring information. A written alternative should match the goal and allow adjustment; do not implement this fictional plan."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-training-principles-p1","prompt":"Explain why a basketball jump goal and a long-distance running goal need different specificity, and what reversibility means during a long break.","steps":["Jump performance emphasises explosive force, coordination and relevant movement patterns. Distance running emphasises sustained aerobic demand and endurance-specific movement.","Shared fitness work can support both, but the dominant training demands differ.","During a long reduction in relevant training, some acquired adaptations may decline; this is reversibility, with variable time course."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-training-principles-p2","prompt":"Apply fitt, overload, specificity and reversibility to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: FITT describes frequency, intensity, time and type of training. These variables give a programme enough detail to interpret its demands. Progressive overload means increasing an appropriate training stimulus over time as adaptation occurs; it does not mean making every session maximal or increasing all variables at once. Recovery is part of adaptation.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Fictional programme analysis is not an individual training prescription.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on fitt, overload, specificity and reversibility?","o":["Frequency triples and intensity also rises sharply; session time and type must also be specified.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Frequency triples and intensity also rises sharply; session time and type must also be specified. Fictional programme analysis is not an individual training prescription."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Fictional programme analysis is not an individual training prescription.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Fictional programme analysis is not an individual training prescription. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-training-methods","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Continuous, interval, fartlek and circuit methods","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Continuous, interval, fartlek and circuit methods</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Continuous training maintains activity without planned rest, usually at an intensity sustainable for the intended duration. Fartlek varies pace within ongoing activity, combining changes in effort with continued movement. Interval training alternates work and recovery periods. The lengths and intensities of both work and recovery determine which demands the session emphasises.","html":"Continuous training maintains activity without planned rest, usually at an intensity sustainable for the intended duration. Fartlek varies pace within ongoing activity, combining changes in effort with continued <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>. Interval training alternates work and <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> periods. The lengths and intensities of both work and recovery determine which demands the session emphasises.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Short intervals can emphasise rapid high-intensity efforts, while longer intervals can support sustained aerobic work. These are broad tendencies, not guarantees from the word interval alone. A circuit organises stations with selected exercises, work duration and recovery; it can be designed for different fitness goals. The format itself does not specify an energy-system outcome.","html":"Short intervals can emphasise rapid high-intensity efforts, while longer intervals can support sustained aerobic work. These are broad tendencies, not guarantees from the word interval alone. A circuit organises stations with selected exercises, work duration and <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark>; it can be designed for different fitness goals. The format itself does not specify an energy-system outcome.","diagram":"training"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A teacher comparing two written interval plans should consider work-to-rest structure and actual intensity, not just the number of repetitions. Active recovery differs from complete rest. Repeated maximal efforts with very short recovery cannot maintain the same output indefinitely. Decreasing skill quality or changing fatigue signals may alter the purpose of the activity.","html":"A teacher comparing two written interval plans should consider work-to-rest structure and actual intensity, not just the number of repetitions. Active <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> differs from complete rest. Repeated maximal efforts with very short recovery cannot maintain the same output indefinitely. Decreasing skill quality or changing fatigue signals may alter the purpose of the activity.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Match method to a stated performance demand and provide enough fictional detail for analysis. Explain how the recovery period supports the next effort or creates a repeated-work challenge. A circuit of slow low-load movements is not automatically power training, and running faster for brief portions of an otherwise continuous session differs from a fully stopped interval design.","html":"Match method to a stated performance demand and provide enough fictional detail for analysis. Explain how the <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> period supports the next effort or creates a repeated-work challenge. A circuit of slow low-load movements is not automatically power training, and running faster for brief portions of an otherwise continuous session differs from a fully stopped interval design.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-training-methods-w1","prompt":"Compare a continuous steady run, a run with changing pace but no stops, and repeated work bouts separated by rest.","steps":["Steady uninterrupted work is continuous training.","Changing pace without stopping is fartlek.","Repeated work separated by planned recovery is interval training; duration, intensity and recovery determine its emphasis."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-training-methods-p1","prompt":"Two circuits have identical station counts: one uses controlled sustained work and one brief explosive tasks with ample recovery. Explain why the same circuit label does not imply the same adaptation.","steps":["The exercise type and work intensity differ.","Sustained controlled work can emphasise endurance, while brief explosive work with suitable recovery can emphasise power-related demands.","Station count alone omits duration, load, movement quality and recovery; specificity requires those details."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-training-methods-p2","prompt":"Apply continuous, interval, fartlek and circuit methods to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Continuous training maintains activity without planned rest, usually at an intensity sustainable for the intended duration. Fartlek varies pace within ongoing activity, combining changes in effort with continued movement. Interval training alternates work and recovery periods. The lengths and intensities of both work and recovery determine which demands the session emphasises.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Examples compare methods; actual session loads require supervision.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on continuous, interval, fartlek and circuit methods?","o":["Steady uninterrupted work is continuous training.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Steady uninterrupted work is continuous training. Examples compare methods; actual session loads require supervision."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Examples compare methods; actual session loads require supervision.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Examples compare methods; actual session loads require supervision. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-resistance","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Resistance, flexibility and plyometrics","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Resistance, flexibility and plyometrics</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"An isometric action produces tension with little overall length change. Isotonic is commonly used in school training descriptions for dynamic resistance work, although muscle tension in ordinary free-weight movements is not literally constant throughout the motion. Concentric action shortens active muscle; eccentric action lengthens active muscle under load. Isokinetic action maintains a set angular velocity using specialised equipment.","html":"An isometric action produces <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> with little overall length change. Isotonic is commonly used in school training descriptions for dynamic resistance work, although <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> tension in ordinary free-weight movements is not literally constant throughout the motion. Concentric action shortens active muscle; eccentric action lengthens active muscle under load. Isokinetic action maintains a set angular <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> using specialised equipment.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Resistance training can target different outcomes depending on load, speed, repetition structure and recovery. Flexibility methods aim to develop or maintain range of motion. Plyometric activities exploit a rapid stretch–shortening sequence to support explosive performance; they are not simply any repeated jump and require appropriate technique and progression.","html":"Resistance training can target different outcomes depending on load, speed, repetition structure and <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark>. Flexibility methods aim to develop or maintain range of motion. Plyometric activities exploit a rapid stretch–shortening sequence to support explosive performance; they are not simply any repeated jump and require appropriate technique and progression.","diagram":"force-length"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A wall hold, a controlled dynamic lift and an isokinetic device illustrate distinct action conditions. Classifying the method does not establish whether it is appropriate for a student. The load imposed, technique and supervision matter. A movement can include concentric and eccentric phases, so one label for the entire repetition may hide useful information.","html":"A wall hold, a controlled dynamic lift and an isokinetic device illustrate distinct action conditions. Classifying the method does not establish whether it is appropriate for a student. The load imposed, technique and supervision matter. A <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> can include concentric and eccentric phases, so one label for the entire repetition may hide useful information.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain a method through its mechanical feature and a relevant sport demand. Avoid treating soreness as proof of a successful session. Compare written plans, observe teacher-approved demonstrations and describe phases without instructing unsupervised maximal lifting or high-impact jumping. A classroom model of a lever is a conceptual tool, not a personal lifting recommendation.","html":"Explain a method through its mechanical feature and a relevant sport demand. Avoid treating soreness as proof of a successful session. Compare written plans, observe teacher-approved demonstrations and describe phases without instructing unsupervised maximal lifting or high-impact jumping. A classroom model of a lever is a conceptual tool, not a personal lifting recommendation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-resistance-w1","prompt":"Classify an elbow hold, raising and lowering a load, and a device that controls joint speed.","steps":["The hold is primarily isometric at the stated angle.","Raising can involve concentric muscle action and controlled lowering eccentric action; the movement is dynamic resistance work.","A device controlling angular velocity can produce isokinetic exercise."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-resistance-p1","prompt":"Explain a stretch–shortening cycle and correct: every dynamic lift has exactly constant muscle tension.","steps":["A rapid active lengthening phase followed by shortening can contribute to explosive performance; timing, coordination and elastic behaviour matter.","Plyometric work uses this principle and needs appropriate supervised progression.","Ordinary dynamic resistance often changes joint torque and muscle force through its range, despite the conventional school label isotonic."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-resistance-p2","prompt":"Apply resistance, flexibility and plyometrics to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: An isometric action produces tension with little overall length change. Isotonic is commonly used in school training descriptions for dynamic resistance work, although muscle tension in ordinary free-weight movements is not literally constant throughout the motion. Concentric action shortens active muscle; eccentric action lengthens active muscle under load. Isokinetic action maintains a set angular velocity using specialised equipment.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: No loads, maximal tests or jump volumes are prescribed here.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on resistance, flexibility and plyometrics?","o":["The hold is primarily isometric at the stated angle.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The hold is primarily isometric at the stated angle. No loads, maximal tests or jump volumes are prescribed here."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["No loads, maximal tests or jump volumes are prescribed here.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"No loads, maximal tests or jump volumes are prescribed here. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-space","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Anatomy, physiology and foundations","area":"Skills and tactics","title":"Space, positioning and simple tactical decisions","subtitle":"Skills and tactics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Skills and tactics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Skills and tactics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Space, positioning and simple tactical decisions</h3><p>Skills and tactics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A tactic is a purposeful response to a game situation. Technique concerns how a skill is executed; a tactic concerns when, where and why it is selected. Creating space can open passing lanes and stretch a defence. Moving into space without considering the ball, opponent and teammates may instead remove support from the player who needs it.","html":"A tactic is a purposeful response to a game situation. Technique concerns how a skill is executed; a tactic concerns when, where and why it is selected. Creating space can open passing lanes and stretch a defence. Moving into space without considering the ball, opponent and teammates may instead remove support from the player who needs it.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Positioning changes with possession, field or court location and the phase of play. In an invasion game, an attacker may offer width or depth while another supports behind the ball. A defender may reduce space between the ball and goal while maintaining awareness of an opponent. These are principles to adapt, not universal formations for every sport.","html":"Positioning changes with possession, field or court location and the phase of play. In an invasion game, an attacker may offer width or depth while another supports behind the ball. A defender may reduce space between the ball and goal while maintaining awareness of an opponent. These are principles to adapt, not universal formations for every sport.","diagram":"tactics"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Decision making depends on information: opponent position, teammate readiness, available space and risk. A low-risk pass may preserve possession, while an available scoring opportunity may justify a different choice. Evaluate the choice using the actual evidence rather than the result alone; a sound decision can fail through execution, and a poor decision can succeed by chance.","html":"Decision making depends on information: opponent position, teammate readiness, available space and risk. A low-risk pass may preserve possession, while an available scoring opportunity may justify a different choice. Evaluate the choice using the actual <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> rather than the result alone; a sound decision can fail through execution, and a poor decision can succeed by chance.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Use a simple court diagram to explain two options and the information that would favour each. Distinguish the chosen action from its expected benefit and possible limitation. Then transfer the principle to the school’s sport with teacher feedback. A written answer cannot certify skill execution in a competitive setting.","html":"Use a simple court diagram to explain two options and the information that would favour each. Distinguish the chosen action from its expected benefit and possible limitation. Then <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark> the principle to the school’s sport with teacher <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark>. A written answer cannot certify skill execution in a competitive setting.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-space-w1","prompt":"A netball ball-carrier faces a marked teammate near goal and an unmarked wide teammate. Explain a possession-preserving option and a condition that might change it.","steps":["Passing to the unmarked wide teammate may maintain possession and create a new angle.","The decision uses defender position, passing lane and teammate readiness.","An emerging clear scoring lane or a rapidly closing wide defender could change the preferred option; outcome alone does not establish decision quality."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-space-p1","prompt":"In a small-sided soccer situation, explain how one teammate offering width and another depth can help. Identify one risk of both running towards the ball.","steps":["Width can stretch defenders and offer a lateral passing lane; depth offers a different distance and angle of support.","If both converge, passing options and usable space may shrink.","Evaluate positions relative to opponents and phase of play; fixed instructions are not appropriate for every situation."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-space-p2","prompt":"Apply space, positioning and simple tactical decisions to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A tactic is a purposeful response to a game situation. Technique concerns how a skill is executed; a tactic concerns when, where and why it is selected. Creating space can open passing lanes and stretch a defence. Moving into space without considering the ball, opponent and teammates may instead remove support from the player who needs it.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Schematic positions illustrate decisions, not a compulsory sport formation.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on space, positioning and simple tactical decisions?","o":["Passing to the unmarked wide teammate may maintain possession and create a new angle.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Passing to the unmarked wide teammate may maintain possession and create a new angle. Schematic positions illustrate decisions, not a compulsory sport formation."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Schematic positions illustrate decisions, not a compulsory sport formation.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Schematic positions illustrate decisions, not a compulsory sport formation. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-skill-types","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Classify sporting skills","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Classify sporting skills</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Gross skills use comparatively large muscle groups and whole-body actions; fine skills emphasise precise smaller movements. Open skills occur in changing, less predictable environments, while closed skills occur in relatively stable conditions. These classifications describe continua and context, rather than assigning every sport permanently to one box.","html":"Gross skills use comparatively large <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> groups and whole-body actions; fine skills emphasise precise smaller movements. Open skills occur in changing, less predictable environments, while closed skills occur in relatively stable conditions. These classifications describe continua and context, rather than assigning every sport permanently to one box.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Discrete skills have a recognisable beginning and end, such as one free throw. Serial skills link discrete elements in an ordered sequence. Continuous skills repeat without an obvious endpoint built into each cycle, such as ongoing running. A drill can combine continuous movement with discrete responses; select the specific action being classified.","html":"Discrete skills have a recognisable beginning and end, such as one free throw. Serial skills link discrete elements in an ordered sequence. Continuous skills repeat without an obvious endpoint built into each cycle, such as ongoing running. A drill can combine continuous <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> with discrete responses; select the specific action being classified.","diagram":"skill"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A basketball free throw in a stable practice setting is relatively closed, whereas choosing and executing a pass against moving defenders is more open. The same physical technique can face different information demands when moved from isolated practice to a game. A controlled drill can improve execution while omitting perception and decision requirements.","html":"A basketball free throw in a stable practice setting is relatively closed, whereas choosing and executing a pass against moving defenders is more open. The same physical technique can face different information demands when moved from isolated practice to a game. A controlled drill can improve execution while omitting perception and decision requirements.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Classify by naming the feature and providing evidence from the situation. Avoid writing closed because it happens indoors: weather exposure is only one possible environmental influence. Opponents, teammates and timing can make an indoor task open. Explain how classification should influence the practice conditions needed for transfer.","html":"Classify by naming the feature and providing <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> from the situation. Avoid writing closed because it happens indoors: weather exposure is only one possible environmental influence. Opponents, teammates and timing can make an indoor task open. Explain how classification should influence the practice conditions needed for <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark>.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-skill-types-w1","prompt":"Classify a stationary free-throw attempt and a pass against moving defenders by environmental predictability and temporal structure.","steps":["The free throw is relatively closed in the stated stable conditions and discrete.","The contested pass is relatively open because defenders and options change, and the individual pass is discrete.","Both use substantial upper-body action; classify the actual task rather than the entire sport."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-skill-types-p1","prompt":"A student says an indoor badminton rally is closed and all running is discrete. Correct both claims.","steps":["Indoor location does not make the rally predictable: opponent shots and positions make the task relatively open.","Ongoing running is continuous; a defined start or finish of a race does not turn each running cycle into a discrete skill.","A drill may contain several skill types, so specify the action analysed."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-skill-types-p2","prompt":"Apply classify sporting skills to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Gross skills use comparatively large muscle groups and whole-body actions; fine skills emphasise precise smaller movements. Open skills occur in changing, less predictable environments, while closed skills occur in relatively stable conditions. These classifications describe continua and context, rather than assigning every sport permanently to one box.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Classification is a continuum and depends on the precise task.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on classify sporting skills?","o":["The free throw is relatively closed in the stated stable conditions and discrete.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The free throw is relatively closed in the stated stable conditions and discrete. Classification is a continuum and depends on the precise task."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Classification is a continuum and depends on the precise task.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Classification is a continuum and depends on the precise task. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-learning-stages","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Cognitive, associative and autonomous learning","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Cognitive, associative and autonomous learning</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"In the cognitive stage, a learner is developing an understanding of what to do. Performance may be inconsistent and demand considerable conscious attention. Clear demonstrations and a small number of useful cues can help. In the associative stage, the learner refines coordination and links useful feedback with changes in execution.","html":"In the cognitive stage, a learner is developing an understanding of what to do. Performance may be inconsistent and demand considerable conscious <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark>. Clear demonstrations and a small number of useful cues can help. In the associative stage, the learner refines coordination and links useful <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> with changes in execution.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"In the autonomous stage, familiar skill execution requires less conscious attention, allowing attention to tactical information. Automaticity is not the same as perfect performance under every condition. An experienced athlete learning a new technique or responding to an unfamiliar constraint may still require deliberate attention and renewed practice.","html":"In the autonomous stage, familiar skill execution requires less conscious <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark>, allowing attention to tactical information. Automaticity is not the same as perfect performance under every condition. An experienced athlete learning a new technique or responding to an unfamiliar constraint may still require deliberate attention and renewed practice.","diagram":"learning"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Fitts and Posner’s stages are a useful model, not a rigid timetable. Learners can differ across skills within the same sport. A player may execute a familiar pass automatically while learning a new serve cognitively. Assigning a stage from age alone ignores actual consistency, attentional demands and experience.","html":"Fitts and Posner’s stages are a useful model, not a rigid timetable. Learners can differ across skills within the same sport. A player may execute a familiar pass automatically while learning a new serve cognitively. Assigning a stage from age alone ignores actual consistency, attentional demands and experience.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Choose coaching cues to match the learner and task. Too many instructions can overload a beginner. A more experienced learner may benefit from specific feedback about a remaining error or tactical application. Explain evidence for the stage, a suitable support strategy and what improvement would be observed, rather than simply naming the model.","html":"Choose coaching cues to match the learner and task. Too many instructions can overload a beginner. A more experienced learner may benefit from specific <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> about a remaining error or tactical application. Explain <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> for the stage, a suitable support strategy and what improvement would be observed, rather than simply naming the model.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-learning-stages-w1","prompt":"A novice volleyball server repeatedly asks where to stand and how to contact the ball. Identify the likely stage and an appropriate coaching response.","steps":["The stated uncertainty about the action and inconsistent execution suggest the cognitive stage.","Use a clear demonstration and one or two relevant cues, then allow attempts and understandable feedback.","Do not overload the learner or assume the stage solely from age."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-learning-stages-p1","prompt":"An experienced basketball player executes passing with little conscious attention but struggles with a newly introduced technique. Explain why one permanent stage label is misleading.","steps":["Stages relate to specific skills and conditions.","A familiar pass can be relatively autonomous while the new technique requires cognitive learning.","Observe consistency and attention demand, then adapt cues and feedback to the particular skill."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-learning-stages-p2","prompt":"Apply cognitive, associative and autonomous learning to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: In the cognitive stage, a learner is developing an understanding of what to do. Performance may be inconsistent and demand considerable conscious attention. Clear demonstrations and a small number of useful cues can help. In the associative stage, the learner refines coordination and links useful feedback with changes in execution.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The stage model is descriptive, not a fixed age schedule.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on cognitive, associative and autonomous learning?","o":["The stated uncertainty about the action and inconsistent execution suggest the cognitive stage.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The stated uncertainty about the action and inconsistent execution suggest the cognitive stage. The stage model is descriptive, not a fixed age schedule."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The stage model is descriptive, not a fixed age schedule.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The stage model is descriptive, not a fixed age schedule. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-cues","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Visual, verbal and proprioceptive cues","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Visual, verbal and proprioceptive cues</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A cue directs attention towards useful information. Visual cues include demonstrations, targets and observed positions. Verbal cues are spoken instructions or brief reminders. Proprioceptive information concerns the body’s sensed position and movement, arising from receptors and integrated sensory processing rather than simply looking at oneself.","html":"A cue directs <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark> towards useful information. Visual cues include demonstrations, targets and observed positions. Verbal cues are spoken instructions or brief reminders. Proprioceptive information concerns the body’s sensed position and <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>, arising from receptors and integrated sensory processing rather than simply looking at oneself.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A useful cue is short, relevant and linked to the learner’s task. For a pass, a visual target can show direction, a verbal reminder can focus a key action, and sensed balance can help the learner recognise position. Different learners may use combinations; claiming everyone belongs to one fixed visual or verbal learning style is not supported by this classification.","html":"A useful cue is short, relevant and linked to the learner’s task. For a pass, a visual target can show direction, a verbal reminder can focus a key action, and sensed balance can help the learner recognise position. Different learners may use combinations; claiming everyone belongs to one fixed visual or verbal learning style is not supported by this classification.","diagram":"processing"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Timing matters. A long explanation delivered during a rapid action can compete with information the athlete needs from the environment. A brief cue before practice and targeted feedback afterwards may be clearer. The coach should check whether the learner understood the cue and whether performance changes, rather than assuming the cue works because it sounds memorable.","html":"Timing matters. A long explanation delivered during a rapid action can compete with information the athlete needs from the environment. A brief cue before practice and targeted <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> afterwards may be clearer. The coach should check whether the learner understood the cue and whether performance changes, rather than assuming the cue works because it sounds memorable.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Describe the information supplied, the movement feature it targets and how the learner can test it. Internal body sensations and externally observed outcomes provide different kinds of evidence. Neither is infallible, so a learner’s feeling of a successful movement should be compared with observation or task outcome.","html":"Describe the information supplied, the <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> feature it targets and how the learner can test it. Internal body sensations and externally observed outcomes provide different kinds of <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark>. Neither is infallible, so a learner’s feeling of a successful movement should be compared with observation or task outcome.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-cues-w1","prompt":"A coach demonstrates a serve, says contact in front, and asks the learner to notice weight shifting. Classify the three cue sources.","steps":["The demonstration is a visual cue.","The spoken phrase is verbal.","Noticing sensed weight shift uses proprioceptive information; it is distinct from watching a video."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-cues-p1","prompt":"Explain why giving six shouted instructions during a fast rally may be less useful than one cue before practice and feedback afterwards.","steps":["Too many instructions can overload attention and compete with perception of the rally.","A focused pre-practice cue can establish the intended feature.","Feedback afterwards can link the attempt to an observed result; suitability depends on learner and task rather than one universal rule."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-cues-p2","prompt":"Apply visual, verbal and proprioceptive cues to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A cue directs attention towards useful information. Visual cues include demonstrations, targets and observed positions. Verbal cues are spoken instructions or brief reminders. Proprioceptive information concerns the body’s sensed position and movement, arising from receptors and integrated sensory processing rather than simply looking at oneself.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Cues are information sources, not fixed learner personality types.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on visual, verbal and proprioceptive cues?","o":["The demonstration is a visual cue.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The demonstration is a visual cue. Cues are information sources, not fixed learner personality types."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Cues are information sources, not fixed learner personality types.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Cues are information sources, not fixed learner personality types. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-processing","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Information processing in decisions","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Information processing in decisions</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"An information-processing model describes input, decision processes, output and feedback. Input includes available stimuli, such as an opponent’s movement. Stimulus identification concerns detecting and interpreting relevant information. Response selection concerns deciding what action to take. Response programming and execution prepare and produce the movement.","html":"An information-processing model describes input, decision processes, output and <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark>. Input includes available stimuli, such as an opponent’s <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>. Stimulus identification concerns detecting and interpreting relevant information. Response selection concerns deciding what action to take. Response programming and execution prepare and produce the movement.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Feedback from the action and its outcome informs later responses. The model simplifies a continuous interacting process; real sport does not always pause neatly between boxes. Skilled anticipation uses advance information to prepare responses, while misleading cues can produce incorrect anticipation. Faster movement alone does not explain every reduction in total response time.","html":"<mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> from the action and its outcome informs later responses. The model simplifies a continuous interacting process; real sport does not always pause neatly between boxes. Skilled anticipation uses advance information to prepare responses, while misleading cues can produce incorrect anticipation. Faster <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> alone does not explain every reduction in total response time.","diagram":"processing"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"In a badminton receive, the player observes racket and shuttle information, decides on a direction and executes movement. Failure can arise from misreading a cue, selecting an unsuitable response or executing it inaccurately. A coach should identify which part seems limiting before changing practice. Repeating the same movement does not necessarily fix poor reading of opponents.","html":"In a badminton receive, the player observes racket and shuttle information, decides on a direction and executes <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>. Failure can arise from misreading a cue, selecting an unsuitable response or executing it inaccurately. A coach should identify which part seems limiting before changing practice. Repeating the same movement does not necessarily fix poor reading of opponents.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain a concrete sequence and give a feedback loop. Use relevant input rather than listing every sensory stimulus in the venue. Distinguish reaction time from movement time: the first concerns delay before initiation, the second execution after initiation. Total response can improve through either, and accuracy must be considered alongside speed.","html":"Explain a concrete sequence and give a <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> loop. Use relevant input rather than listing every sensory stimulus in the venue. Distinguish reaction time from <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> time: the first concerns delay before initiation, the second execution after initiation. Total response can improve through either, and accuracy must be considered alongside speed.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-processing-w1","prompt":"A receiver moves quickly to the wrong side after misreading a deceptive shot. Locate the likely difficulty in the processing model.","steps":["The player may have misidentified relevant stimulus information or selected the wrong response.","Fast movement execution does not correct the direction decision.","Practice should include reading appropriate shot cues and checking decisions, not only sprint speed."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-processing-p1","prompt":"Trace a soccer goalkeeper’s response to a shot through input, selection, output and feedback. Distinguish reaction and movement time.","steps":["Input includes ball and shooter information; selection chooses an appropriate response; output executes the movement.","Feedback includes the save outcome and sensed or observed action, informing future attempts.","Reaction time precedes response initiation; movement time covers the executed action. Analyse both accuracy and speed."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-processing-p2","prompt":"Apply information processing in decisions to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: An information-processing model describes input, decision processes, output and feedback. Input includes available stimuli, such as an opponent’s movement. Stimulus identification concerns detecting and interpreting relevant information. Response selection concerns deciding what action to take. Response programming and execution prepare and produce the movement.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The boxes are a teaching model of a continuous process.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on information processing in decisions?","o":["The player may have misidentified relevant stimulus information or selected the wrong response.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The player may have misidentified relevant stimulus information or selected the wrong response. The boxes are a teaching model of a continuous process."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The boxes are a teaching model of a continuous process.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The boxes are a teaching model of a continuous process. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-feedback","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Intrinsic, extrinsic, concurrent and terminal feedback","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Intrinsic, extrinsic, concurrent and terminal feedback</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Intrinsic feedback is information available from the performer’s own sensory systems, such as the feel of contact or seeing where a ball lands. Extrinsic feedback is additional information supplied from outside, such as a coach’s comment or a video review. Intrinsic does not mean always accurate; a learner can misinterpret a sensation.","html":"Intrinsic <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> is information available from the performer’s own sensory systems, such as the feel of contact or seeing where a ball lands. Extrinsic feedback is additional information supplied from outside, such as a coach’s comment or a video review. Intrinsic does not mean always accurate; a learner can misinterpret a sensation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Concurrent feedback is given during the action; terminal feedback follows it. Knowledge of results reports the outcome, such as whether a serve landed in the target. Knowledge of performance concerns the movement process, such as the contact position. A message can combine both. Timing and content are separate dimensions and should be classified separately.","html":"Concurrent <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> is given during the action; terminal feedback follows it. Knowledge of results reports the outcome, such as whether a serve landed in the target. Knowledge of performance concerns the <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> process, such as the contact position. A message can combine both. Timing and content are separate dimensions and should be classified separately.","diagram":"feedback"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Verbal and non-verbal feedback can reinforce useful behaviour and support motivation. Feedback should be specific enough to guide a change and manageable enough to use. Giving corrections after every attempt can create dependence in some contexts; withholding all guidance can also leave a learner repeating an error. Suitability depends on the stage, task and purpose.","html":"Verbal and non-verbal <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> can reinforce useful behaviour and support motivation. Feedback should be specific enough to guide a change and manageable enough to use. Giving corrections after every attempt can create dependence in some contexts; withholding all guidance can also leave a learner repeating an error. Suitability depends on the stage, task and purpose.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Link observation to an actionable focus: your contact was behind the intended position identifies more than bad serve. Check whether the cue produces a useful change and allow the learner to interpret intrinsic information. A positive tone helps communication, but praise without evidence does not explain how performance can improve.","html":"Link observation to an actionable focus: your contact was behind the intended position identifies more than bad serve. Check whether the cue produces a useful change and allow the learner to interpret intrinsic information. A positive tone helps communication, but praise without <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> does not explain how performance can improve.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-feedback-w1","prompt":"After a serve a coach says it landed long because your contact was behind your head. Classify source, timing and content.","steps":["The coach supplies extrinsic feedback.","It is terminal because it follows the serve.","Landed long is knowledge of results; contact behind the head is knowledge of performance. The statement combines both."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-feedback-p1","prompt":"A swimmer senses poor balance during a movement while a coach gives a signal during the action. Classify both and explain one feedback planning consideration.","steps":["Sensed balance is intrinsic feedback. The coach’s added signal is extrinsic and concurrent.","A signal should be interpretable without distracting from the action.","Feedback quantity and timing should suit the learner and task, with opportunities to use intrinsic information."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-feedback-p2","prompt":"Apply intrinsic, extrinsic, concurrent and terminal feedback to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Intrinsic feedback is information available from the performer’s own sensory systems, such as the feel of contact or seeing where a ball lands. Extrinsic feedback is additional information supplied from outside, such as a coach’s comment or a video review. Intrinsic does not mean always accurate; a learner can misinterpret a sensation.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Feedback classifications overlap; source, timing and content answer different questions.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on intrinsic, extrinsic, concurrent and terminal feedback?","o":["The coach supplies extrinsic feedback.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The coach supplies extrinsic feedback. Feedback classifications overlap; source, timing and content answer different questions."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Feedback classifications overlap; source, timing and content answer different questions.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Feedback classifications overlap; source, timing and content answer different questions. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-motion","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Linear, angular and general motion","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Linear, angular and general motion</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Linear motion describes displacement along a straight or curved path where the points of a rigid body share the same translational movement. Angular motion is rotation about an axis. General motion combines translation and rotation, as occurs in many sporting actions. A ball can translate across a court while also spinning.","html":"Linear motion describes displacement along a straight or curved path where the points of a rigid body share the same translational <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>. Angular motion is rotation about an axis. General motion combines translation and rotation, as occurs in many sporting actions. A ball can translate across a court while also spinning.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Speed is distance travelled per unit time and has magnitude only. Velocity is displacement per unit time and includes direction. A player can run a loop and finish at the starting point: average speed is positive but average velocity over the whole loop is zero. Instantaneous velocity during the loop is not zero.","html":"Speed is distance travelled per unit time and has magnitude only. <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> is displacement per unit time and includes direction. A player can run a loop and finish at the starting point: average speed is positive but average velocity over the whole loop is zero. Instantaneous velocity during the loop is not zero.","diagram":"vectors"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Acceleration is change in velocity per unit time, so changing direction at constant speed is still acceleration. Deceleration is commonly used for a reduction in speed; the sign of acceleration depends on the chosen positive direction. State units and reference direction instead of treating every negative number as moving backwards.","html":"Acceleration is change in <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> per unit time, so changing direction at constant speed is still acceleration. Deceleration is commonly used for a reduction in speed; the sign of acceleration depends on the chosen positive direction. State units and reference direction instead of treating every negative number as moving backwards.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Separate the body or segment being analysed, the interval and the quantity requested. In a sprint, a runner’s centre of mass translates while limbs rotate around joints. A summary such as running is linear misses the angular actions contributing to the whole movement. Use a drawing and explicit direction when describing vectors.","html":"Separate the body or segment being analysed, the interval and the quantity requested. In a sprint, a runner’s centre of mass translates while limbs rotate around joints. A summary such as running is linear misses the angular actions contributing to the whole <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>. Use a drawing and explicit direction when describing vectors.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-motion-w1","prompt":"A player runs 40 m around a route in 8 s and returns to the starting point. Calculate average speed and average velocity.","steps":["Average speed = 40 ÷ 8 = 5 m/s.","Net displacement is zero, so average velocity = 0 ÷ 8 = 0 m/s.","This does not mean the player was stationary during the route."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-motion-p1","prompt":"A ball travels towards goal while spinning, and a runner changes direction without changing speed. Classify motion and explain acceleration.","steps":["The ball has general motion: translation plus angular rotation.","The runner’s velocity changes because direction changes.","Therefore acceleration occurs even at constant speed; velocity includes direction."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-motion-p2","prompt":"Apply linear, angular and general motion to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Linear motion describes displacement along a straight or curved path where the points of a rigid body share the same translational movement. Angular motion is rotation about an axis. General motion combines translation and rotation, as occurs in many sporting actions. A ball can translate across a court while also spinning.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Choose the body, interval and reference direction before calculating.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on linear, angular and general motion?","o":["Average speed = 40 ÷ 8 = 5 m/s.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Average speed = 40 ÷ 8 = 5 m/s. Choose the body, interval and reference direction before calculating."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Choose the body, interval and reference direction before calculating.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Choose the body, interval and reference direction before calculating. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-kinematics","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Speed, velocity and acceleration calculations","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Speed, velocity and acceleration calculations</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Average speed equals total distance divided by elapsed time. Average velocity uses displacement instead of total distance. Average acceleration equals final velocity minus initial velocity, divided by time. These formulas describe changes but do not alone identify which force caused them. Units are metres per second for velocity and metres per second squared for acceleration.","html":"Average speed equals total distance divided by elapsed time. Average <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> uses displacement instead of total distance. Average acceleration equals final velocity minus initial velocity, divided by time. These formulas describe changes but do not alone identify which <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> caused them. Units are metres per second for velocity and metres per second squared for acceleration.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Take a chosen positive direction seriously. A ball moving east at 8 m/s and slowing to 2 m/s east has negative acceleration if east is positive. It remains moving east. A ball moving east and then west can have a large velocity change even if its speed at the two endpoints is equal.","html":"Take a chosen positive direction seriously. A ball moving east at 8 m/s and slowing to 2 m/s east has negative acceleration if east is positive. It remains moving east. A ball moving east and then west can have a large <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> change even if its speed at the two endpoints is equal.","diagram":"vectors"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A graph’s axes determine its interpretation. Slope on a displacement–time graph represents velocity; slope on a velocity–time graph represents acceleration. Area under velocity–time gives displacement for the interval, including sign. These relationships require the actual axis quantities, not simply seeing a line rise or fall.","html":"A graph’s axes determine its interpretation. Slope on a displacement–time graph represents <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark>; slope on a velocity–time graph represents acceleration. Area under velocity–time gives displacement for the interval, including sign. These relationships require the actual axis quantities, not simply seeing a line rise or fall.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Show substitution, result, units and a contextual interpretation. Distinguish an average over an interval from an instantaneous value. Classroom calculations often assume constant acceleration for simplicity; state that assumption when using a straight-line model. A measured sport motion may have changing acceleration and measurement uncertainty.","html":"Show substitution, result, units and a contextual interpretation. Distinguish an average over an interval from an instantaneous value. Classroom calculations often assume constant acceleration for simplicity; state that assumption when using a straight-line model. A measured sport motion may have changing acceleration and measurement uncertainty.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-kinematics-w1","prompt":"A runner’s velocity increases from 2 to 8 m/s east over 3 s. Calculate average acceleration with east positive.","steps":["a = (8 − 2) ÷ 3.","a = 2 m/s² east.","This is the average change in velocity per second over the stated interval, not proof that acceleration was constant throughout."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-kinematics-p1","prompt":"A ball slows from 10 to 4 m/s east in 2 s. Calculate acceleration and explain why negative acceleration does not mean westward motion.","steps":["a = (4 − 10) ÷ 2 = −3 m/s² with east positive.","Acceleration is directed west while velocity remains east at the stated endpoints.","The velocity magnitude decreases; direction of motion and direction of acceleration differ."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-kinematics-p2","prompt":"Apply speed, velocity and acceleration calculations to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Average speed equals total distance divided by elapsed time. Average velocity uses displacement instead of total distance. Average acceleration equals final velocity minus initial velocity, divided by time. These formulas describe changes but do not alone identify which force caused them. Units are metres per second for velocity and metres per second squared for acceleration.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Fictional data illustrate average quantities; measurements have uncertainty.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on speed, velocity and acceleration calculations?","o":["a = (8 − 2) ÷ 3.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: a = (8 − 2) ÷ 3. Fictional data illustrate average quantities; measurements have uncertainty."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Fictional data illustrate average quantities; measurements have uncertainty.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Fictional data illustrate average quantities; measurements have uncertainty. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-projectiles","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Projection speed, angle and height","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Projection speed, angle and height</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A projectile moves after release under gravity and any aerodynamic forces. In an ideal model with no air resistance, horizontal velocity is constant and vertical acceleration is downward. The trajectory is parabolic. Real balls can experience drag and spin-induced forces, so the ideal model is a starting point rather than a perfect match to every sport.","html":"A projectile moves after release under gravity and any aerodynamic forces. In an ideal model with no air resistance, horizontal <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> is constant and vertical acceleration is downward. The trajectory is parabolic. Real balls can experience drag and spin-induced forces, so the ideal model is a starting point rather than a perfect match to every sport.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Release speed, release angle and release height influence flight. For equal release and landing heights, fixed release speed and no air resistance, maximum horizontal range occurs at 45 degrees. That result does not apply unchanged when release height differs, speed changes with technique or the aim is clearing a net or reaching a target rather than maximum range.","html":"Release speed, release angle and release height influence flight. For equal release and landing heights, fixed release speed and no air resistance, maximum horizontal range occurs at 45 degrees. That result does not apply unchanged when release height differs, speed changes with technique or the aim is clearing a net or reaching a target rather than maximum range.","diagram":"projectile"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Horizontal and vertical components of release velocity explain the angle trade-off. A greater angle provides more upward velocity but less horizontal velocity at fixed speed. Higher release above landing can increase flight time. An optimal sport angle must account for constraints and the performer’s ability to generate speed at that angle.","html":"Horizontal and vertical components of release <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> explain the angle trade-off. A greater angle provides more upward velocity but less horizontal velocity at fixed speed. Higher release above landing can increase flight time. An optimal sport angle must account for constraints and the performer’s ability to generate speed at that angle.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"When evaluating a throw, state the target and assumptions before recommending a change. A high arc may help clear an obstacle while reducing useful horizontal speed. A faster release can improve range under suitable conditions, but a skill must also preserve accuracy and appropriate technique. Do not infer a universal best angle from an ideal classroom diagram.","html":"When evaluating a throw, state the target and assumptions before recommending a change. A high arc may help clear an obstacle while reducing useful horizontal speed. A faster release can improve range under suitable conditions, but a skill must also preserve accuracy and appropriate technique. Do not infer a universal best angle from an ideal classroom diagram.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"projectile"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-projectiles-w1","prompt":"Why is 45 degrees an ideal maximum-range result but not a universal best basketball shooting angle?","steps":["The 45-degree result assumes equal release and landing height, fixed speed and no air resistance.","A basketball shot has a different target height, required entry geometry and accuracy constraints.","Release speed and technique can also vary with angle; optimise for the actual target rather than maximum ground range."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-projectiles-p1","prompt":"Compare two ideal launches at equal speed and height, one 30 degrees and one 60 degrees. Explain a limitation of transferring the result to a spinning tennis ball.","steps":["In the equal-height no-drag model the complementary angles have equal range; 60 degrees has greater height and flight time.","The 30-degree launch has greater horizontal velocity.","A spinning tennis ball experiences aerodynamic effects and target constraints; ideal symmetry need not hold."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-projectiles-p2","prompt":"Apply projection speed, angle and height to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A projectile moves after release under gravity and any aerodynamic forces. In an ideal model with no air resistance, horizontal velocity is constant and vertical acceleration is downward. The trajectory is parabolic. Real balls can experience drag and spin-induced forces, so the ideal model is a starting point rather than a perfect match to every sport.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Ideal flight neglects drag and spin and is not a technique prescription.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on projection speed, angle and height?","o":["The 45-degree result assumes equal release and landing height, fixed speed and no air resistance.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The 45-degree result assumes equal release and landing height, fixed speed and no air resistance. Ideal flight neglects drag and spin and is not a technique prescription."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Ideal flight neglects drag and spin and is not a technique prescription.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Ideal flight neglects drag and spin and is not a technique prescription. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-balance","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Base of support and centre of gravity","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Base of support and centre of gravity</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"The base of support is the area enclosed by contact points with the support surface. The centre of gravity is the point through which the resultant weight acts in an approximately uniform gravitational field. The line of gravity is the vertical line through it. In a simple static situation, maintaining that line within the base helps resist tipping.","html":"The base of support is the area enclosed by contact points with the support surface. The centre of gravity is the point through which the resultant weight acts in an approximately uniform gravitational field. The line of gravity is the vertical line through it. In a simple static situation, maintaining that line within the base helps resist tipping.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A wider base and lower centre of gravity generally increase static stability in suitable directions. Body mass can affect resistance to an external disturbance, but stability also depends on posture, force direction, friction and active control. More mass is not a guarantee of better balance in every skill. Direction matters: a wide stance may be stable sideways but less stable forwards.","html":"A wider base and lower centre of gravity generally increase static stability in suitable directions. Body mass can affect resistance to an external disturbance, but stability also depends on posture, <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> direction, friction and active control. More mass is not a guarantee of better balance in every skill. Direction matters: a wide stance may be stable sideways but less stable forwards.","diagram":"balance"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Dynamic movement often intentionally moves beyond a static balance condition. A sprint start trades static stability for the ability to accelerate. A defender may lower the centre of gravity and widen support to respond to contact, while retaining enough mobility to change direction. Stability and mobility can create competing demands.","html":"Dynamic <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> often intentionally moves beyond a static balance condition. A sprint start trades static stability for the ability to accelerate. A defender may lower the centre of gravity and widen support to respond to contact, while retaining enough mobility to change direction. Stability and mobility can create competing demands.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Analyse the base, centre height, line position and external forces for the stated phase. Avoid labelling every lean as an error. Use an annotated diagram to explain what would cause tipping in a static model and what controlled movement might achieve in a dynamic task. Actual balance also relies on sensory and muscular control.","html":"Analyse the base, centre height, line position and external forces for the stated phase. Avoid labelling every lean as an error. Use an annotated diagram to explain what would cause tipping in a static model and what controlled <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> might achieve in a dynamic task. Actual balance also relies on sensory and muscular control.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-balance-w1","prompt":"A defender widens the stance and bends the knees before receiving contact. Explain two stability effects and a possible trade-off.","steps":["A wider base increases the support area in relevant directions.","Lowering the centre of gravity can reduce tipping tendency under an external force.","A very wide or rigid stance may limit quick movement; stability must be balanced with mobility."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-balance-p1","prompt":"Why can a sprinter leaning forward be effective even though the line of gravity is near or beyond a static support boundary?","steps":["The start is dynamic and aims to accelerate rather than remain motionless.","A static tipping model alone does not describe controlled forward motion supported by subsequent steps and forces.","Judge the phase, force direction and movement purpose; a lean is not automatically poor balance."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-balance-p2","prompt":"Apply base of support and centre of gravity to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: The base of support is the area enclosed by contact points with the support surface. The centre of gravity is the point through which the resultant weight acts in an approximately uniform gravitational field. The line of gravity is the vertical line through it. In a simple static situation, maintaining that line within the base helps resist tipping.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The diagram is a static model; dynamic control requires additional analysis.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on base of support and centre of gravity?","o":["A wider base increases the support area in relevant directions.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: A wider base increases the support area in relevant directions. The diagram is a static model; dynamic control requires additional analysis."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The diagram is a static model; dynamic control requires additional analysis.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The diagram is a static model; dynamic control requires additional analysis. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-newton","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Newton’s laws in sporting actions","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Newton’s laws in sporting actions</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Newton’s first law states that a body maintains rest or constant velocity unless acted on by a net external force. Inertia is resistance to changes in motion and is related to mass. A moving ball does not need a continuing forward force to keep moving in an ideal frictionless model; drag and other forces explain slowing in real conditions.","html":"Newton’s first law states that a body maintains rest or constant <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> unless acted on by a net external <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>. Inertia is resistance to changes in motion and is related to mass. A moving ball does not need a continuing forward force to keep moving in an ideal frictionless model; drag and other forces explain slowing in real conditions.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Newton’s second law relates net force to mass and acceleration: F = ma for constant mass. Net force is the vector sum of forces, not any one applied force. If horizontal applied force and resistance balance, horizontal acceleration is zero even though both forces are non-zero. Weight and normal force may also balance vertically.","html":"Newton’s second law relates net <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> to mass and acceleration: F = ma for constant mass. Net force is the vector sum of forces, not any one applied force. If horizontal applied force and resistance balance, horizontal acceleration is zero even though both forces are non-zero. Weight and normal force may also balance vertically.","diagram":"newton"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Newton’s third law describes equal and opposite forces on different interacting bodies. A runner pushes the ground backwards, and the ground exerts a forward force on the runner. These two forces do not cancel on the runner because they act on different bodies. Forces acting on one body must be combined separately.","html":"Newton’s third law describes equal and opposite forces on different interacting bodies. A runner pushes the ground backwards, and the ground exerts a forward <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> on the runner. These two forces do not cancel on the runner because they act on different bodies. Forces acting on one body must be combined separately.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"For a sport explanation, identify the body analysed, the interacting body and the direction. Distinguish force from velocity and do not assume that faster motion always means greater current net force. Acceleration, rather than velocity alone, determines net force in the simple second-law relationship.","html":"For a sport explanation, identify the body analysed, the interacting body and the direction. Distinguish <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> from <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> and do not assume that faster motion always means greater current net force. Acceleration, rather than velocity alone, determines net force in the simple second-law relationship.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-newton-w1","prompt":"A 60 kg fictional athlete has a horizontal net force of 180 N. Calculate acceleration and explain the ground interaction.","steps":["a = F ÷ m = 180 ÷ 60 = 3 m/s² in the net-force direction.","The runner exerts a backward force on the ground; the ground exerts a forward force on the runner.","The action–reaction pair acts on different bodies and does not cancel on the runner."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-newton-p1","prompt":"A ball moves at constant horizontal velocity in an ideal model. Is a continuing net forward force required? Explain what changes in a real flight.","steps":["No: the first law permits constant velocity with zero net horizontal force.","In real flight drag usually opposes relative motion and spin can add lift or lateral forces.","These forces change velocity; identify forces acting on the ball rather than inventing a force because it is moving."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-newton-p2","prompt":"Apply newton’s laws in sporting actions to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Newton’s first law states that a body maintains rest or constant velocity unless acted on by a net external force. Inertia is resistance to changes in motion and is related to mass. A moving ball does not need a continuing forward force to keep moving in an ideal frictionless model; drag and other forces explain slowing in real conditions.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The second-law calculation assumes constant mass and the stated net force.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on newton’s laws in sporting actions?","o":["a = F ÷ m = 180 ÷ 60 = 3 m/s² in the net-force direction.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: a = F ÷ m = 180 ÷ 60 = 3 m/s² in the net-force direction. The second-law calculation assumes constant mass and the stated net force."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The second-law calculation assumes constant mass and the stated net force.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The second-law calculation assumes constant mass and the stated net force. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-levers","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Biomechanics","title":"First, second and third class levers","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>First, second and third class levers</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A lever has an axis or fulcrum, an effort force and a resistance or load. Classification depends on their relative positions. In a first-class lever the fulcrum lies between effort and resistance. In a second-class lever the resistance lies between fulcrum and effort. In a third-class lever the effort lies between fulcrum and resistance.","html":"A lever has an axis or fulcrum, an effort <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> and a resistance or load. Classification depends on their relative positions. In a first-class lever the fulcrum lies between effort and resistance. In a second-class lever the resistance lies between fulcrum and effort. In a third-class lever the effort lies between fulcrum and resistance.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Many limb actions act as third-class levers. In a simplified elbow-flexion model, the elbow is the fulcrum, biceps applies effort near the elbow, and a hand-held load is further away. This arrangement often gives speed and range at the distal end while requiring comparatively large muscle force. It is not a mechanical force advantage simply because the movement is effective.","html":"Many limb actions act as third-class levers. In a simplified elbow-flexion model, the elbow is the fulcrum, biceps applies effort near the elbow, and a hand-held load is further away. This arrangement often gives speed and range at the distal end while requiring comparatively large <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>. It is not a mechanical force advantage simply because the <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> is effective.","diagram":"lever"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Mechanical advantage in an ideal lever equals effort-arm length divided by resistance-arm length. The relevant moment arms are perpendicular distances from the axis to each force’s line of action. Anatomy and joint angle can change those distances even when attachment points are unchanged. A straight ruler model therefore simplifies a real joint.","html":"Mechanical advantage in an ideal lever equals effort-arm length divided by resistance-arm length. The relevant moment arms are perpendicular distances from the axis to each <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>’s line of action. Anatomy and joint angle can change those distances even when attachment points are unchanged. A straight ruler model therefore simplifies a real joint.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Draw the positions before assigning a class. Then explain the advantage or trade-off in the task. The toe-standing example is commonly represented as a second-class lever with the forefoot as fulcrum, body weight between it and Achilles effort. Real body systems involve multiple forces, joints and changing geometry, so state the model’s boundaries.","html":"Draw the positions before assigning a class. Then explain the advantage or trade-off in the task. The toe-standing example is commonly represented as a second-class lever with the forefoot as fulcrum, body weight between it and Achilles effort. Real body systems involve multiple forces, joints and changing geometry, so state the model’s boundaries.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"torque"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-levers-w1","prompt":"An elbow lever has effort 0.04 m from the axis and a hand load 0.32 m away. Identify class and ideal mechanical advantage.","steps":["The effort lies between fulcrum and load, so the simplified arrangement is third class.","MA = 0.04 ÷ 0.32 = 0.125.","The short effort arm implies a force disadvantage, while the distal end can gain movement speed and range."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-levers-p1","prompt":"Draw the three orders F–E–R, E–F–R and F–R–E and classify each. Explain why perpendicular moment arms matter.","steps":["F–E–R is third class; E–F–R is first class; F–R–E is second class.","Torque depends on perpendicular distance to a force’s line of action.","A distance measured along the bone is not always the effective moment arm, especially when the force is angled."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-levers-p2","prompt":"Apply first, second and third class levers to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A lever has an axis or fulcrum, an effort force and a resistance or load. Classification depends on their relative positions. In a first-class lever the fulcrum lies between effort and resistance. In a second-class lever the resistance lies between fulcrum and effort. In a third-class lever the effort lies between fulcrum and resistance.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The lever diagram omits other forces and changing anatomy.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on first, second and third class levers?","o":["The effort lies between fulcrum and load, so the simplified arrangement is third class.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The effort lies between fulcrum and load, so the simplified arrangement is third class. The lever diagram omits other forces and changing anatomy."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The lever diagram omits other forces and changing anatomy.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The lever diagram omits other forces and changing anatomy. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-motivation","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Sport psychology","title":"Motivation and self-confidence","subtitle":"Sport psychology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Sport psychology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Sport psychology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Motivation and self-confidence</h3><p>Sport psychology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Motivation influences direction, intensity and persistence of effort. Intrinsic motivation relates to interest or satisfaction in the activity itself; extrinsic motivation relates to external rewards or consequences. They can coexist. An athlete may enjoy improving a skill while also seeking team selection. It is inaccurate to assume every external reward destroys enjoyment.","html":"Motivation influences direction, intensity and persistence of effort. Intrinsic motivation relates to interest or satisfaction in the activity itself; extrinsic motivation relates to external rewards or consequences. They can coexist. An athlete may enjoy improving a skill while also seeking team selection. It is inaccurate to assume every external reward destroys enjoyment.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Self-confidence concerns belief in the ability to meet a demand. It should be distinguished from certainty of winning or inflated claims of superiority. Evidence of preparation, useful feedback and achievable progress can support confidence. Overconfidence may reduce attention to real weaknesses, while very low confidence can impair commitment to an appropriate action.","html":"Self-confidence concerns belief in the ability to meet a demand. It should be distinguished from certainty of winning or inflated claims of superiority. <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> of preparation, useful <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> and achievable progress can support confidence. Overconfidence may reduce <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark> to real weaknesses, while very low confidence can impair commitment to an appropriate action.","diagram":"goals"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Motivational needs differ across individuals and situations. A beginner may need a manageable task and recognition of progress, whereas an experienced player may respond to a demanding specific challenge. Punishment or public comparison can have unintended effects. A strategy should support useful effort, learning and a workable relationship rather than only produce short-term compliance.","html":"Motivational needs differ across individuals and situations. A beginner may need a manageable task and recognition of progress, whereas an experienced player may respond to a demanding specific challenge. Punishment or public comparison can have unintended effects. A strategy should support useful effort, learning and a workable relationship rather than only produce short-term compliance.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Evaluate a strategy through its mechanism and observed effect. Ask whether it strengthens interest, competence, commitment or a clear purpose. Do not infer private motivation solely from a single visible result. A missed shot can occur in a highly motivated athlete, and a successful shot is not proof of confidence or emotional state.","html":"Evaluate a strategy through its mechanism and observed effect. Ask whether it strengthens interest, competence, commitment or a clear purpose. Do not infer private motivation solely from a single visible result. A missed shot can occur in a highly motivated athlete, and a successful shot is not proof of confidence or emotional state.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-motivation-w1","prompt":"A player enjoys refining a serve and also wants team selection. Identify motivational sources and one confidence-building strategy.","steps":["Enjoyment of refinement is intrinsic; selection is an extrinsic incentive. Both can coexist.","A specific achievable process goal with feedback on progress can support confidence through evidence of preparation.","Confidence is belief in meeting the demand, not a guarantee of winning."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-motivation-p1","prompt":"Evaluate a coach’s claim that only the winning player was motivated. Suggest a more informative observation.","steps":["Outcome reflects opponents, execution and chance as well as motivation.","Observe persistence, engagement with practice and response to feedback over time, while allowing the athlete to explain their goals.","Use a strategy matched to the learner rather than treating winning as a direct measure of internal motivation."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-motivation-p2","prompt":"Apply motivation and self-confidence to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Motivation influences direction, intensity and persistence of effort. Intrinsic motivation relates to interest or satisfaction in the activity itself; extrinsic motivation relates to external rewards or consequences. They can coexist. An athlete may enjoy improving a skill while also seeking team selection. It is inaccurate to assume every external reward destroys enjoyment.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Visible performance does not reveal a person’s private mental state.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on motivation and self-confidence?","o":["Enjoyment of refinement is intrinsic; selection is an extrinsic incentive. Both can coexist.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Enjoyment of refinement is intrinsic; selection is an extrinsic incentive. Both can coexist. Visible performance does not reveal a person’s private mental state."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Visible performance does not reveal a person’s private mental state.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Visible performance does not reveal a person’s private mental state. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-attention","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Sport psychology","title":"Concentration and attentional styles","subtitle":"Sport psychology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Sport psychology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Sport psychology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Concentration and attentional styles</h3><p>Sport psychology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Concentration involves directing and maintaining attention on relevant information. Nideffer’s model describes broad or narrow attention and internal or external focus. Broad external attention scans the environment; narrow external attention focuses on a particular external cue or target. Broad internal attention can analyse several ideas; narrow internal attention can focus on one internal sensation or thought.","html":"Concentration involves directing and maintaining <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark> on relevant information. Nideffer’s model describes broad or narrow attention and internal or external focus. Broad external attention scans the environment; narrow external attention focuses on a particular external cue or target. Broad internal attention can analyse several ideas; narrow internal attention can focus on one internal sensation or thought.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A midfielder scanning teammate and opponent positions uses a broad external focus. A player attending to a target before a free throw may use narrow external focus. Reviewing several tactical options can be broad internal, while focusing on one controlled breathing cue can be narrow internal. These categories describe attentional demands, not permanent personality labels.","html":"A midfielder scanning teammate and opponent positions uses a broad external focus. A player attending to a target before a free throw may use narrow external focus. Reviewing several tactical options can be broad internal, while focusing on one controlled breathing cue can be narrow internal. These categories describe attentional demands, not permanent personality labels.","diagram":"attention"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Successful performance may require switching styles. A player scans the court, selects an option and then focuses on the relevant execution cue. Remaining narrow can miss a new passing lane; remaining broad during a precision action can distract from a critical target. The preferred focus depends on the task and its stage.","html":"Successful performance may require switching styles. A player scans the court, selects an option and then focuses on the relevant execution cue. Remaining narrow can miss a new passing lane; remaining broad during a precision action can distract from a critical target. The preferred focus depends on the task and its stage.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain where attention is directed and how much information it includes. A useful strategy can be a cue word or routine that brings attention back to a relevant feature. Avoid instructing everyone to block out all external information: in an open game, important changes must still be detected. The aim is relevant attention, not simply less attention.","html":"Explain where <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark> is directed and how much information it includes. A useful strategy can be a cue word or routine that brings attention back to a relevant feature. Avoid instructing everyone to block out all external information: in an open game, important changes must still be detected. The aim is relevant attention, not simply less attention.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-attention-w1","prompt":"A midfielder scans the whole field, then focuses on a teammate’s movement before passing. Name the two external styles.","steps":["Scanning several positions is broad external attention.","Focusing on the selected teammate’s relevant movement is narrow external attention.","Switching can support both option selection and execution; neither style is universally best."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-attention-p1","prompt":"Classify analysing several tactical choices and attending to one body sensation. Explain a risk of remaining narrow throughout an open game.","steps":["Several tactical thoughts: broad internal. One body sensation: narrow internal.","Persistent narrow focus can miss changing opponents or new options.","The strategy should permit switching to the information required by the phase of play."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-attention-p2","prompt":"Apply concentration and attentional styles to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Concentration involves directing and maintaining attention on relevant information. Nideffer’s model describes broad or narrow attention and internal or external focus. Broad external attention scans the environment; narrow external attention focuses on a particular external cue or target. Broad internal attention can analyse several ideas; narrow internal attention can focus on one internal sensation or thought.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The categories are task-dependent and do not diagnose attention disorders.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on concentration and attentional styles?","o":["Scanning several positions is broad external attention.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Scanning several positions is broad external attention. The categories are task-dependent and do not diagnose attention disorders."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The categories are task-dependent and do not diagnose attention disorders.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The categories are task-dependent and do not diagnose attention disorders. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-arousal","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Sport psychology","title":"Stress, arousal and the inverted-U model","subtitle":"Sport psychology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Sport psychology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Sport psychology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Stress, arousal and the inverted-U model</h3><p>Sport psychology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Arousal describes a level of activation; stress concerns a perceived imbalance between demands and resources. Anxiety can include worry and bodily symptoms. These are related but not identical. A high level of activation may help one task and interfere with another. The same competitive situation can be interpreted differently by different athletes.","html":"Arousal describes a level of activation; stress concerns a perceived imbalance between demands and resources. Anxiety can include worry and bodily symptoms. These are related but not identical. A high level of activation may help one task and interfere with another. The same competitive situation can be interpreted differently by different athletes.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"The inverted-U hypothesis suggests that performance can improve with rising arousal up to an optimum and then decline. It is a model, not a universal law or a precise prediction from a single heart-rate reading. Task complexity, precision, experience and individual differences can change the useful arousal range and the actual relationship.","html":"The inverted-U hypothesis suggests that performance can improve with rising arousal up to an optimum and then decline. It is a model, not a universal law or a precise prediction from a single heart-rate reading. Task complexity, precision, experience and individual differences can change the useful arousal range and the actual relationship.","diagram":"arousal"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A skilled athlete performing a well-practised powerful action may tolerate different activation from a novice learning a complex precision skill. Age and experience can influence demands but do not assign a fixed optimum. Interventions should match the observed problem: energising cues and calming routines serve different purposes.","html":"A skilled athlete performing a well-practised powerful action may tolerate different activation from a novice learning a complex precision skill. Age and experience can influence demands but do not assign a fixed optimum. Interventions should match the observed problem: energising cues and calming routines serve different purposes.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Interpret a fictional graph by identifying its axes and trend, then qualify transfer to real people. A decline under pressure might relate to attention, tension or decision demands, but the graph alone does not prove one cause. Use athlete feedback and repeated observations to evaluate a strategy rather than claiming it will automatically eliminate stress.","html":"Interpret a fictional graph by identifying its axes and trend, then qualify <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark> to real people. A decline under <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> might relate to <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark>, <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> or decision demands, but the graph alone does not prove one cause. Use athlete <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> and repeated observations to evaluate a strategy rather than claiming it will automatically eliminate stress.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-arousal-w1","prompt":"A novice precision performer becomes rushed under pressure. Explain an inverted-U interpretation and one limitation.","steps":["The model suggests arousal may have moved beyond a useful level for the task.","High activation could interfere with attention or control, so a familiar calming routine may be considered.","The model does not prove cause or an exact optimum; task demands and individual responses vary."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-arousal-p1","prompt":"Why might a powerful familiar action and a complex new skill require different arousal strategies?","steps":["The new skill requires substantial conscious attention and coordination, which excessive activation can disrupt.","A familiar powerful task may benefit from greater activation in some athletes.","Match strategy to evidence and task; there is no universal arousal score or identical optimum for all ages."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-arousal-p2","prompt":"Apply stress, arousal and the inverted-u model to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Arousal describes a level of activation; stress concerns a perceived imbalance between demands and resources. Anxiety can include worry and bodily symptoms. These are related but not identical. A high level of activation may help one task and interfere with another. The same competitive situation can be interpreted differently by different athletes.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The curve is a hypothesis illustration, not a diagnostic scale.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on stress, arousal and the inverted-u model?","o":["The model suggests arousal may have moved beyond a useful level for the task.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The model suggests arousal may have moved beyond a useful level for the task. The curve is a hypothesis illustration, not a diagnostic scale."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The curve is a hypothesis illustration, not a diagnostic scale.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The curve is a hypothesis illustration, not a diagnostic scale. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-goals","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Sport psychology","title":"SMARTER goals and goal types","subtitle":"Sport psychology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Sport psychology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Sport psychology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>SMARTER goals and goal types</h3><p>Sport psychology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"An outcome goal concerns a result relative to others, such as winning a match. A performance goal concerns an individual standard, such as improving a measured passing-accuracy result. A process goal concerns actions used to perform, such as scanning before receiving. The types can support one another, but they offer different levels of control.","html":"An outcome goal concerns a result relative to others, such as winning a match. A performance goal concerns an individual standard, such as improving a measured passing-accuracy result. A process goal concerns actions used to perform, such as scanning before receiving. The types can support one another, but they offer different levels of control.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"SMARTER is a planning acronym with variations across resources. Here it means specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound, evaluated and reviewed. Define the version used rather than assuming every source expands each letter identically. A useful goal identifies a clear behaviour or standard and a way to assess progress.","html":"SMARTER is a planning acronym with variations across resources. Here it means specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound, evaluated and reviewed. Define the version used rather than assuming every source expands each letter identically. A useful goal identifies a clear behaviour or standard and a way to assess progress.","diagram":"goals"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Winning is influenced by opponents and conditions. A process goal can focus effort on a controllable action, while a performance measure shows whether a standard improved. Evaluation compares evidence with the target. Review decides whether to retain, adjust or replace the goal. Setting a goal once does not make it effective indefinitely.","html":"Winning is influenced by opponents and conditions. A process goal can focus effort on a controllable action, while a performance measure shows whether a standard improved. Evaluation compares <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> with the target. Review decides whether to retain, adjust or replace the goal. Setting a goal once does not make it effective indefinitely.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Write a goal that fits the athlete’s current task and realistic context. Include the measurement method and timeframe in a fictional plan, then state how feedback would prompt adjustment. Avoid promising a guaranteed result or making a health target from arbitrary body-size values. Effective goals organise learning; they do not replace practice or coaching.","html":"Write a goal that fits the athlete’s current task and realistic context. Include the measurement method and timeframe in a fictional plan, then state how <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> would prompt adjustment. Avoid promising a guaranteed result or making a health target from arbitrary body-size values. Effective goals organise learning; they do not replace practice or coaching.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-goals-w1","prompt":"Change win every game into one process goal and one performance goal for passing, with evaluation and review.","steps":["Process: scan for a free option before receiving during the agreed practice.","Performance: improve the proportion of appropriate completed passes in comparable teacher-observed tasks by the agreed review date, using a baseline.","Evaluate the observations and review the goal with the teacher; exact targets depend on baseline and context, and winning is not fully controllable."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-goals-p1","prompt":"Classify winning a tournament, improving an individual serve-target score, and using a routine before each serve. Explain the SMARTER version used here.","steps":["Winning: outcome; individual score: performance; routine: process.","Specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound, evaluated and reviewed.","Evaluation checks evidence and review adjusts the plan; acronym variants should be stated explicitly."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-goals-p2","prompt":"Apply smarter goals and goal types to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: An outcome goal concerns a result relative to others, such as winning a match. A performance goal concerns an individual standard, such as improving a measured passing-accuracy result. A process goal concerns actions used to perform, such as scanning before receiving. The types can support one another, but they offer different levels of control.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Fictional targets require baseline evidence before practical use.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on smarter goals and goal types?","o":["Process: scan for a free option before receiving during the agreed practice.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Process: scan for a free option before receiving during the agreed practice. Fictional targets require baseline evidence before practical use."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Fictional targets require baseline evidence before practical use.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Fictional targets require baseline evidence before practical use. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes11-unit2-apply","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Learning, biomechanics and psychology","area":"Integrated application","title":"Apply coaching and biomechanics to a skill","subtitle":"Integrated application · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Integrated application · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Integrated application"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Apply coaching and biomechanics to a skill</h3><p>Integrated application · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A strong skill analysis joins observation, mechanical explanation and a suitable coaching response. Identify the exact phase and task first. A vague statement such as the serve is wrong does not locate an issue. The learner’s stage, environmental demands and feedback needs determine how a proposed change should be introduced.","html":"A strong skill analysis joins observation, mechanical explanation and a suitable coaching response. Identify the exact phase and task first. A vague statement such as the serve is wrong does not locate an issue. The learner’s stage, environmental demands and <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> needs determine how a proposed change should be introduced.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"For a throwing action, describe relevant joint actions, segment motion and projection conditions. For balance, identify support and centre-of-gravity position. Use only the information actually available. A single image cannot establish timing through an entire action, and a successful outcome can occur despite a technique limitation.","html":"For a throwing action, describe relevant joint actions, segment motion and projection conditions. For balance, identify support and centre-of-gravity position. Use only the information actually available. A single image cannot establish timing through an entire action, and a successful outcome can occur despite a technique limitation.","diagram":"feedback"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Connect a proposed cue to the diagnosed feature. A novice may need a clear demonstration and one priority, while a more experienced athlete may use a precise video comparison. Select knowledge of performance when the movement itself needs adjustment and knowledge of results when the target outcome helps assess it.","html":"Connect a proposed cue to the diagnosed feature. A novice may need a clear demonstration and one priority, while a more experienced athlete may use a precise video comparison. Select knowledge of performance when the <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> itself needs adjustment and knowledge of results when the target outcome helps assess it.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"An integrated answer explains a causal chain, gives a relevant example and recognises limits. Repeated observation under similar conditions can test whether a change is associated with improvement. Confidence and goal strategies should support the skill task, not substitute for mechanical evidence. Practical implementation belongs within the teacher’s supervised programme.","html":"An integrated answer explains a causal chain, gives a relevant example and recognises limits. Repeated observation under similar conditions can test whether a change is associated with improvement. Confidence and goal strategies should support the skill task, not substitute for mechanical <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark>. Practical implementation belongs within the teacher’s supervised programme.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes11-unit2-apply-w1","prompt":"A novice’s pass misses the target and video shows inconsistent release direction. Build an explanation and coaching response without inferring an injury.","steps":["The observed issue concerns release direction and consistency, not a diagnosed medical problem.","Use a demonstration and a focused direction cue suited to cognitive learning, with terminal knowledge of performance.","Check repeated comparable attempts and target outcomes; confidence can be supported through a manageable process goal."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-unit2-apply-p1","prompt":"An athlete completes a movement quickly but repeatedly chooses a marked receiver. Explain why biomechanical speed work alone may not solve the problem.","steps":["The limiting feature may be stimulus identification or response selection in an open environment.","Use practice including scanning, changing defenders and decision feedback.","Preserve execution practice but evaluate tactical choices as well as completion time; speed alone does not ensure an appropriate decision."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes11-unit2-apply-p2","prompt":"Apply apply coaching and biomechanics to a skill to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A strong skill analysis joins observation, mechanical explanation and a suitable coaching response. Identify the exact phase and task first. A vague statement such as the serve is wrong does not locate an issue. The learner’s stage, environmental demands and feedback needs determine how a proposed change should be introduced.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Written analysis supports, but cannot verify, actual skill proficiency.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on apply coaching and biomechanics to a skill?","o":["The observed issue concerns release direction and consistency, not a diagnosed medical problem.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The observed issue concerns release direction and consistency, not a diagnosed medical problem. Written analysis supports, but cannot verify, actual skill proficiency."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Written analysis supports, but cannot verify, actual skill proficiency.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Written analysis supports, but cannot verify, actual skill proficiency. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-muscle-structure","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"From muscle belly to myofibril","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>From muscle belly to myofibril</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A skeletal muscle belly contains bundles called fascicles. A fascicle contains muscle fibres, which are long muscle cells. Each fibre contains myofibrils built from repeating sarcomeres. Myofilaments within these structures include actin and myosin. This hierarchy links the visible muscle to the microscopic structures involved in tension generation.","html":"A skeletal <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> belly contains bundles called fascicles. A fascicle contains muscle fibres, which are long muscle cells. Each fibre contains myofibrils built from repeating sarcomeres. Myofilaments within these structures include actin and myosin. This hierarchy links the visible muscle to the microscopic structures involved in <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> generation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Epimysium surrounds the whole muscle, perimysium surrounds fascicles and endomysium surrounds individual fibres. These connective tissues support organisation and transmission of force towards attachments. A muscle fibre is a cell, not simply another name for a bundle of cells. Myofibrils are within fibres, not the outer connective-tissue covering.","html":"Epimysium surrounds the whole <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark>, perimysium surrounds fascicles and endomysium surrounds individual fibres. These connective tissues support organisation and transmission of <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> towards attachments. A muscle fibre is a cell, not simply another name for a bundle of cells. Myofibrils are within fibres, not the outer connective-tissue covering.","diagram":"hierarchy"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A sarcomere extends between adjacent Z lines. Actin and myosin are organised into a pattern of bands and zones. Many sarcomeres shorten together as filaments slide; they do not need to become shorter filaments themselves. The organisation helps explain how a microscopic process contributes to an overall muscle action.","html":"A sarcomere extends between adjacent Z lines. Actin and myosin are organised into a pattern of bands and zones. Many sarcomeres shorten together as filaments slide; they do not need to become shorter filaments themselves. The organisation helps explain how a microscopic process contributes to an overall <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> action.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Use a nested diagram and trace one level inward at a time. Then reverse the explanation from filament interaction to tension transmitted through tissue and tendon. The drawing is not to scale and leaves out many cellular components. It supports terminology and relationships without pretending to show the full complexity of living muscle.","html":"Use a nested diagram and trace one level inward at a time. Then reverse the explanation from filament interaction to <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> transmitted through tissue and tendon. The drawing is not to scale and leaves out many cellular components. It supports terminology and relationships without pretending to show the full complexity of living <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark>.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-muscle-structure-w1","prompt":"Order whole muscle, fascicle, muscle fibre, myofibril and sarcomere from largest organisational level to smallest, with connective coverings.","steps":["Whole muscle → fascicle → muscle fibre → myofibril → sarcomere.","Epimysium surrounds muscle, perimysium surrounds fascicles and endomysium surrounds fibres.","A fibre is a muscle cell; a myofibril is a contractile structure within it."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-muscle-structure-p1","prompt":"Correct: perimysium covers every myofibril and muscle fibres are bundles of fascicles. Explain how the hierarchy relates to movement.","steps":["Perimysium surrounds fascicles, not individual myofibrils.","Fascicles contain fibres, which contain myofibrils.","Sarcomere interactions contribute tension that is transmitted through the muscle’s connective structures and tendons to attachments."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-muscle-structure-p2","prompt":"Apply from muscle belly to myofibril to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A skeletal muscle belly contains bundles called fascicles. A fascicle contains muscle fibres, which are long muscle cells. Each fibre contains myofibrils built from repeating sarcomeres. Myofilaments within these structures include actin and myosin. This hierarchy links the visible muscle to the microscopic structures involved in tension generation.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Nested boxes show organisation, not anatomical scale.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on from muscle belly to myofibril?","o":["Whole muscle → fascicle → muscle fibre → myofibril → sarcomere.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Whole muscle → fascicle → muscle fibre → myofibril → sarcomere. Nested boxes show organisation, not anatomical scale."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Nested boxes show organisation, not anatomical scale.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Nested boxes show organisation, not anatomical scale. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-sarcomere","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Sliding filaments and sarcomere bands","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Sliding filaments and sarcomere bands</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"The sarcomere is the functional repeating unit between Z lines. Thin actin filaments extend from the Z-line region and overlap thick myosin filaments. The A band corresponds to the length of the thick filaments, the I band to thin-filament-only regions and the H zone to a thick-filament-only central region when present.","html":"The sarcomere is the functional repeating unit between Z lines. Thin actin filaments extend from the Z-line region and overlap thick myosin filaments. The A band corresponds to the length of the thick filaments, the I band to thin-filament-only regions and the H zone to a thick-filament-only central region when present.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"During shortening, actin slides further between myosin filaments and the Z lines move closer together. The I band and H zone become narrower, while the A band remains approximately constant. The filaments themselves do not become shorter. Changing overlap, repeated across many sarcomeres, helps explain shortening of the contractile structures.","html":"During shortening, actin slides further between myosin filaments and the Z lines move closer together. The I band and H zone become narrower, while the A band remains approximately constant. The filaments themselves do not become shorter. Changing overlap, repeated across many sarcomeres, helps explain shortening of the contractile structures.","diagram":"sarcomere"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Myosin heads interact with exposed actin binding sites in cross-bridge cycling. Calcium regulation and ATP availability permit the cycle, rather than calcium being an alternative energy currency. A muscle can develop tension without visible overall shortening if the load and internal behaviour produce an isometric condition. Sarcomere diagrams usually illustrate one simplified shortening state.","html":"Myosin heads interact with exposed actin binding sites in cross-bridge cycling. <mark class=\"pes-key\">calcium</mark> regulation and <mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark> availability permit the cycle, rather than calcium being an alternative energy currency. A <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> can develop <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> without visible overall shortening if the load and internal behaviour produce an isometric condition. Sarcomere diagrams usually illustrate one simplified shortening state.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Compare resting and shortened diagrams by tracking the same labelled structures. Identify what changed and what stayed constant. Avoid saying every band shrinks or that actin dissolves. Then connect the microscopic changes to a sporting example, while recognising that overall force depends on recruitment, length, velocity and external loading as well as overlap.","html":"Compare resting and shortened diagrams by tracking the same labelled structures. Identify what changed and what stayed constant. Avoid saying every band shrinks or that actin dissolves. Then connect the microscopic changes to a sporting example, while recognising that overall <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> depends on recruitment, length, <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> and external loading as well as overlap.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-sarcomere-w1","prompt":"During sarcomere shortening, predict changes in Z-line distance, I band, H zone, A band and filament lengths.","steps":["Z lines move closer; I band and H zone narrow.","A band stays approximately constant because thick-filament length stays constant.","Actin and myosin lengths do not shorten; their overlap increases as filaments slide."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-sarcomere-p1","prompt":"A student says myosin shrinks and all bands disappear during a jump. Correct the mechanism and state one additional factor influencing muscle force.","steps":["Filaments slide relative to one another; thick and thin filament lengths are approximately unchanged.","A band remains constant; I band and H zone narrow rather than every band simply disappearing.","Force also depends on recruitment, muscle length, contraction velocity and loading conditions."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-sarcomere-p2","prompt":"Apply sliding filaments and sarcomere bands to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: The sarcomere is the functional repeating unit between Z lines. Thin actin filaments extend from the Z-line region and overlap thick myosin filaments. The A band corresponds to the length of the thick filaments, the I band to thin-filament-only regions and the H zone to a thick-filament-only central region when present.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Band drawings are conceptual and not microscopic measurements.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on sliding filaments and sarcomere bands?","o":["Z lines move closer; I band and H zone narrow.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Z lines move closer; I band and H zone narrow. Band drawings are conceptual and not microscopic measurements."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Band drawings are conceptual and not microscopic measurements.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Band drawings are conceptual and not microscopic measurements. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-cross-bridge","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Calcium and ATP in cross-bridge cycling","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Calcium and ATP in cross-bridge cycling</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A muscle action potential triggers calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium binds to troponin, leading to a change in tropomyosin position that exposes actin binding sites. This allows myosin heads to form cross-bridges. Calcium is a regulatory signal; it is not the molecule that supplies the energy normally attributed to ATP.","html":"A <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> action potential triggers <mark class=\"pes-key\">calcium</mark> release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium binds to troponin, leading to a change in tropomyosin position that exposes actin binding sites. This allows myosin heads to form cross-bridges. Calcium is a regulatory signal; it is not the molecule that supplies the energy normally attributed to <mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark>.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"ATP binding permits myosin to detach from actin. ATP hydrolysis energises and positions the head for a further cycle. With binding sites exposed, a head can bind; phosphate release is associated with the force-generating power stroke, followed by ADP release. The sequence is repeated while conditions allow, rather than one head pulling once for an entire contraction.","html":"<mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark> binding permits myosin to detach from actin. ATP hydrolysis energises and positions the head for a further cycle. With binding sites exposed, a head can bind; phosphate release is associated with the <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>-generating power stroke, followed by ADP release. The sequence is repeated while conditions allow, rather than one head pulling once for an entire contraction.","diagram":"crossbridge"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Relaxation involves reducing available calcium through active reuptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. ATP also supports this pumping. When calcium no longer exposes binding sites, cross-bridge formation reduces. Saying ATP simply starts contraction misses its roles in detachment and calcium handling as well as energising cycling.","html":"Relaxation involves reducing available <mark class=\"pes-key\">calcium</mark> through active reuptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. <mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark> also supports this pumping. When calcium no longer exposes binding sites, cross-bridge formation reduces. Saying ATP simply starts contraction misses its roles in detachment and calcium handling as well as energising cycling.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain the sequence with separate regulatory and energy roles. The school syllabus’s shorthand linking calcium and ATP should not be read as calcium releasing ATP from nowhere. Connect neural activation, calcium regulation, cross-bridge cycling and tension in an ordered account. The diagram simplifies molecular processes and does not show every intermediate biochemical state.","html":"Explain the sequence with separate regulatory and energy roles. The school syllabus’s shorthand linking <mark class=\"pes-key\">calcium</mark> and <mark class=\"pes-key\">ATP</mark> should not be read as calcium releasing ATP from nowhere. Connect neural activation, calcium regulation, cross-bridge cycling and <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> in an ordered account. The diagram simplifies molecular processes and does not show every intermediate biochemical state.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-cross-bridge-w1","prompt":"Explain why calcium and ATP have different roles in contraction, including detachment and relaxation.","steps":["Calcium binds troponin, shifting tropomyosin so actin binding sites are available.","ATP binding permits detachment; hydrolysis energises the myosin head for further cycling.","ATP also supports calcium reuptake during relaxation; calcium is regulation, not the direct ATP energy source."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-cross-bridge-p1","prompt":"Correct: myosin cannot detach until calcium turns into ATP. Outline a more accurate sequence.","steps":["Calcium and ATP are different substances and calcium does not turn into ATP.","Calcium exposes sites via troponin and tropomyosin; ATP binding detaches myosin and hydrolysis prepares further cycling.","Binding and the force-generating stroke recur while sites are exposed; calcium reuptake helps end activation."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-cross-bridge-p2","prompt":"Apply calcium and atp in cross-bridge cycling to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A muscle action potential triggers calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium binds to troponin, leading to a change in tropomyosin position that exposes actin binding sites. This allows myosin heads to form cross-bridges. Calcium is a regulatory signal; it is not the molecule that supplies the energy normally attributed to ATP.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The cycle is a simplified molecular explanation, not a clinical assessment.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on calcium and atp in cross-bridge cycling?","o":["Calcium binds troponin, shifting tropomyosin so actin binding sites are available.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Calcium binds troponin, shifting tropomyosin so actin binding sites are available. The cycle is a simplified molecular explanation, not a clinical assessment."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The cycle is a simplified molecular explanation, not a clinical assessment.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The cycle is a simplified molecular explanation, not a clinical assessment. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-force-relations","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Force–length and concentric force–velocity","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Force–length and concentric force–velocity</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"In a concentric force–velocity relationship, maximal force capacity generally decreases as shortening velocity increases. A rapidly shortening muscle does not generate the same maximum force as it can at a slower shortening speed under comparable conditions. This does not mean every fast movement has low total sporting performance; power depends on both force and velocity.","html":"In a concentric <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>–<mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> relationship, maximal force capacity generally decreases as shortening velocity increases. A rapidly shortening <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> does not generate the same maximum force as it can at a slower shortening speed under comparable conditions. This does not mean every fast <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> has low total sporting performance; power depends on both force and velocity.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Active force–length behaviour depends partly on filament overlap. At very short or very long lengths, effective cross-bridge conditions reduce active force capacity. An intermediate optimal region supports greater active force. Passive tension can increase as tissue stretches, so total force and active contractile force are not identical curves.","html":"Active <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>–length behaviour depends partly on filament overlap. At very short or very long lengths, effective cross-bridge conditions reduce active force capacity. An intermediate optimal region supports greater active force. Passive <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> can increase as tissue stretches, so total force and active contractile force are not identical curves.","diagram":"force-length"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"These relationships refer to muscle conditions, not a direct universal prediction from a single joint angle. Muscle architecture, tendon behaviour, activation and changing moment arms affect joint torque. A longer visible limb position is not proof that the muscle is at its ideal sarcomere length.","html":"These relationships refer to <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> conditions, not a direct universal prediction from a single joint angle. Muscle architecture, tendon behaviour, activation and changing moment arms affect joint torque. A longer visible limb position is not proof that the muscle is at its ideal sarcomere length.","diagram":"force-velocity"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Apply the concepts by distinguishing force, speed and power, and by stating which curve is considered. A fictional graph can illustrate trends without providing an athlete’s real maximum. Avoid the claim that longer always means stronger or that the optimum force condition necessarily gives optimum sport technique. A skilled action balances speed, control and force transfer.","html":"Apply the concepts by distinguishing <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>, speed and power, and by stating which curve is considered. A fictional graph can illustrate trends without providing an athlete’s real maximum. Avoid the claim that longer always means stronger or that the optimum force condition necessarily gives optimum sport technique. A skilled action balances speed, control and force <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark>.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-force-relations-w1","prompt":"Explain why longer muscle length does not always mean greater active force and why faster shortening does not imply greater maximal force.","steps":["Active force depends on useful actin–myosin overlap and has an intermediate optimal region.","Excessively short or long conditions can reduce active force capacity.","For concentric shortening, maximal force capacity generally falls as shortening velocity rises; power still depends on force × velocity."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-force-relations-p1","prompt":"A graph shows active force peaking in the middle and passive force rising at longer lengths. Explain why adding the curves changes interpretation.","steps":["Active force reflects contractile contributions; passive force reflects stretched elastic structures.","Total force can include both, so it need not have the same shape as active force alone.","Joint torque also depends on activation and moment arms; the graph is not a direct technique prescription."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-force-relations-p2","prompt":"Apply force–length and concentric force–velocity to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: In a concentric force–velocity relationship, maximal force capacity generally decreases as shortening velocity increases. A rapidly shortening muscle does not generate the same maximum force as it can at a slower shortening speed under comparable conditions. This does not mean every fast movement has low total sporting performance; power depends on both force and velocity.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Illustrative curves have no athlete-specific force scale.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on force–length and concentric force–velocity?","o":["Active force depends on useful actin–myosin overlap and has an intermediate optimal region.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Active force depends on useful actin–myosin overlap and has an intermediate optimal region. Illustrative curves have no athlete-specific force scale."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Illustrative curves have no athlete-specific force scale.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Illustrative curves have no athlete-specific force scale. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-neurons","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Neural pathways and motor units","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Neural pathways and motor units</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A neuron includes a cell body with nucleus, dendrites that receive signals and an axon that conducts signals away from the cell body. Sensory pathways convey information towards the central nervous system, while motor pathways carry commands towards effectors. The brain and spinal cord participate in processing and coordination; not every response requires the same conscious pathway.","html":"A neuron includes a cell body with nucleus, dendrites that receive signals and an axon that conducts signals away from the cell body. Sensory pathways convey information towards the central nervous system, while motor pathways carry commands towards effectors. The brain and spinal cord participate in processing and coordination; not every response requires the same conscious pathway.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A motor unit consists of one motor neuron and the skeletal muscle fibres it innervates. When the neuron activates its fibres under normal conditions, they respond as a unit. The all-or-none principle concerns an action potential or recruited unit, not a claim that the entire muscle always produces either zero force or maximum force.","html":"A motor unit consists of one motor neuron and the skeletal <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> fibres it innervates. When the neuron activates its fibres under normal conditions, they respond as a unit. The all-or-none principle concerns an action potential or recruited unit, not a claim that the entire muscle always produces either zero <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> or maximum force.","diagram":"neuron"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Whole-muscle force can be graded by recruiting more motor units and by changing firing frequency. Smaller, lower-threshold units are generally recruited before larger units as demand rises. Coordination, feedback and spinal processing also influence movement. A motor unit is not one sensory neuron attached to one whole muscle.","html":"Whole-<mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> can be graded by recruiting more motor units and by changing firing frequency. Smaller, lower-threshold units are generally recruited before larger units as demand rises. Coordination, <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> and spinal processing also influence <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark>. A motor unit is not one sensory neuron attached to one whole muscle.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Trace a sensory observation to processing and motor output, then link the motor neuron to its fibres. Identify where information is travelling rather than defining sensory and motor by the shape of a drawing. Sport examples should distinguish detecting a ball, deciding a response and generating muscle force, while recognising that these processes interact continuously.","html":"Trace a sensory observation to processing and motor output, then link the motor neuron to its fibres. Identify where information is travelling rather than defining sensory and motor by the shape of a drawing. Sport examples should distinguish detecting a ball, deciding a response and generating <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>, while recognising that these processes interact continuously.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-neurons-w1","prompt":"Define a motor unit and explain how whole-muscle force can vary despite an all-or-none principle.","steps":["A motor unit is one motor neuron and all the muscle fibres it innervates.","Individual activation is all-or-none in the relevant sense, but additional units can be recruited.","Changes in firing frequency also grade force, so whole-muscle force is not restricted to zero or maximum."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-neurons-p1","prompt":"Trace noticing a ball to a movement response, distinguishing sensory neurons, brain/spinal cord and motor neurons.","steps":["Sensory pathways convey information towards the central nervous system.","Brain and spinal cord process and coordinate the response; the exact pathways depend on the action.","Motor neurons carry output to their muscle fibres, producing activation and coordinated movement."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-neurons-p2","prompt":"Apply neural pathways and motor units to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A neuron includes a cell body with nucleus, dendrites that receive signals and an axon that conducts signals away from the cell body. Sensory pathways convey information towards the central nervous system, while motor pathways carry commands towards effectors. The brain and spinal cord participate in processing and coordination; not every response requires the same conscious pathway.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The diagram shows a simplified pathway, not every neural connection.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on neural pathways and motor units?","o":["A motor unit is one motor neuron and all the muscle fibres it innervates.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: A motor unit is one motor neuron and all the muscle fibres it innervates. The diagram shows a simplified pathway, not every neural connection."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The diagram shows a simplified pathway, not every neural connection.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The diagram shows a simplified pathway, not every neural connection. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-recruitment","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Recruitment and impulse frequency","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Recruitment and impulse frequency</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Force demand can be met by changing the number and size of active motor units. Preferential recruitment generally follows the size principle: lower-threshold units activate before higher-threshold units as demand rises. Higher-force tasks can involve a greater number of units and units with larger force capacities. This ordering is a useful general principle, not a rule that every complex movement is identical.","html":"<mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> demand can be met by changing the number and size of active motor units. Preferential recruitment generally follows the size principle: lower-threshold units activate before higher-threshold units as demand rises. Higher-force tasks can involve a greater number of units and units with larger force capacities. This ordering is a useful general principle, not a rule that every complex <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> is identical.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Increasing motor-neuron firing frequency can increase force through temporal summation of muscle responses. Recruitment and rate coding are distinct mechanisms and can both contribute. More impulses per second is not the same as recruiting more neurons, although the two can occur together when force demand changes.","html":"Increasing motor-neuron firing frequency can increase <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> through temporal summation of <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> responses. Recruitment and rate coding are distinct mechanisms and can both contribute. More impulses per second is not the same as recruiting more neurons, although the two can occur together when force demand changes.","diagram":"recruitment"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"The all-or-none principle should not be translated into one fixed muscle force. A muscle contains many units, and their activation patterns can vary. A light controlled movement and an explosive task differ in recruitment demand, timing and coordination. External sport performance also depends on mechanics and technique.","html":"The all-or-none principle should not be translated into one fixed <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>. A muscle contains many units, and their activation patterns can vary. A light controlled <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> and an explosive task differ in recruitment demand, timing and coordination. External sport performance also depends on mechanics and technique.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain a change in force through one or both mechanisms, with accurate terminology. Avoid claiming that athletes can consciously activate every fibre independently or that soreness proves all units were recruited. A classroom account of recruitment describes normal physiology and is not an instruction to attempt unsupervised maximal force tests.","html":"Explain a change in <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> through one or both mechanisms, with accurate terminology. Avoid claiming that athletes can consciously activate every fibre independently or that soreness proves all units were recruited. A classroom account of recruitment describes normal physiology and is not an instruction to attempt unsupervised maximal force tests.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-recruitment-w1","prompt":"A muscle produces more force first by adding units and then by increasing their firing rate. Name and explain both mechanisms.","steps":["Adding active units is recruitment, increasing the active fibre contribution.","Increasing impulses per second is rate coding, supporting temporal summation and greater force.","These allow graded whole-muscle output without contradicting the relevant all-or-none principle."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-recruitment-p1","prompt":"Critique: a maximal effort is stronger only because each nerve impulse is bigger.","steps":["Action potentials are not normally graded as simply bigger impulses to produce more force.","Force can increase through recruitment of more or larger units and changed firing frequency.","Coordination and mechanical conditions also influence external force, so effort alone does not measure output."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-recruitment-p2","prompt":"Apply recruitment and impulse frequency to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Force demand can be met by changing the number and size of active motor units. Preferential recruitment generally follows the size principle: lower-threshold units activate before higher-threshold units as demand rises. Higher-force tasks can involve a greater number of units and units with larger force capacities. This ordering is a useful general principle, not a rule that every complex movement is identical.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The size principle is a general recruitment pattern, not a maximal-testing instruction.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on recruitment and impulse frequency?","o":["Adding active units is recruitment, increasing the active fibre contribution.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Adding active units is recruitment, increasing the active fibre contribution. The size principle is a general recruitment pattern, not a maximal-testing instruction."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The size principle is a general recruitment pattern, not a maximal-testing instruction.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The size principle is a general recruitment pattern, not a maximal-testing instruction. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-fibres","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Functional anatomy","title":"Type I, IIa and fast glycolytic fibres","subtitle":"Functional anatomy · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Functional anatomy · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Functional anatomy"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Type I, IIa and fast glycolytic fibres</h3><p>Functional anatomy · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Type I fibres generally have slower shortening characteristics, high oxidative capacity and comparatively good fatigue resistance. Type IIa fibres have faster characteristics and substantial oxidative as well as glycolytic capacity. Fast glycolytic fibres have high-rate force and power characteristics with lower fatigue resistance in sustained work. Real muscles contain mixed populations.","html":"Type I fibres generally have slower shortening characteristics, high oxidative capacity and comparatively good fatigue resistance. Type IIa fibres have faster characteristics and substantial oxidative as well as glycolytic capacity. Fast glycolytic fibres have high-rate <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> and power characteristics with lower fatigue resistance in sustained work. Real muscles contain mixed populations.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"The syllabus uses Type IIb for the fast glycolytic category. In adult human muscle, the comparable fast myosin category is usually described as IIx in modern physiology. Use IIb as the syllabus label while recognising this naming distinction; do not imply that every educational label maps perfectly to current human molecular terminology.","html":"The syllabus uses Type IIb for the fast glycolytic category. In adult human <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark>, the comparable fast myosin category is usually described as IIx in modern physiology. Use IIb as the syllabus label while recognising this naming distinction; do not imply that every educational label maps perfectly to current human molecular terminology.","diagram":"fibres"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"An endurance task benefits from fatigue-resistant oxidative characteristics, while a brief explosive action places high demands on rapid force production. All fibre types may contribute depending on recruitment and demand. A sprinter does not use only fast fibres, and a distance athlete does not contain only slow fibres. Training can influence metabolic characteristics and some fibre-type expression.","html":"An endurance task benefits from fatigue-resistant oxidative characteristics, while a brief explosive action places high demands on rapid <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> production. All fibre types may contribute depending on recruitment and demand. A sprinter does not use only fast fibres, and a distance athlete does not contain only slow fibres. Training can influence metabolic characteristics and some fibre-type expression.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Compare contraction speed, metabolic capacity and fatigue resistance rather than assigning an entire person to one fibre box. Fibre characteristics contribute to performance alongside technique, training, motivation and other physiological factors. Appearance or one race result cannot establish an individual’s fibre distribution. The lesson does not recommend testing or attempts to change fibre type through drugs.","html":"Compare contraction speed, metabolic capacity and fatigue resistance rather than assigning an entire person to one fibre box. Fibre characteristics contribute to performance alongside technique, training, motivation and other physiological factors. Appearance or one race result cannot establish an individual’s fibre distribution. The lesson does not recommend testing or attempts to change fibre type through drugs.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-fibres-w1","prompt":"Compare Type I and the syllabus’s fast glycolytic Type IIb category for an endurance task and explosive effort.","steps":["Type I generally has slower characteristics, high oxidative capacity and good fatigue resistance, supporting sustained work.","The fast glycolytic category has rapid high-force/power characteristics and lower sustained fatigue resistance, supporting brief intense demands.","Modern human terminology usually calls the comparable category IIx; actual muscles and tasks involve mixed fibre contributions."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-fibres-p1","prompt":"Correct: distance runners only use Type I and sprinters have no slow fibres. Include Type IIa in your answer.","steps":["Human muscles contain mixed fibre types and recruitment depends on demand.","Type IIa has fast characteristics with both oxidative and glycolytic capacity.","Performance cannot be inferred solely from one category; training, mechanics and coordination also matter."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-fibres-p2","prompt":"Apply type i, iia and fast glycolytic fibres to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Type I fibres generally have slower shortening characteristics, high oxidative capacity and comparatively good fatigue resistance. Type IIa fibres have faster characteristics and substantial oxidative as well as glycolytic capacity. Fast glycolytic fibres have high-rate force and power characteristics with lower fatigue resistance in sustained work. Real muscles contain mixed populations.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: IIb is retained as a syllabus label; human physiology commonly uses IIx.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on type i, iia and fast glycolytic fibres?","o":["Type I generally has slower characteristics, high oxidative capacity and good fatigue resistance, supporting sustained work.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Type I generally has slower characteristics, high oxidative capacity and good fatigue resistance, supporting sustained work. IIb is retained as a syllabus label; human physiology commonly uses IIx."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["IIb is retained as a syllabus label; human physiology commonly uses IIx.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"IIb is retained as a syllabus label; human physiology commonly uses IIx. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-competition-food","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Nutrition before, during and after competition","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Nutrition before, during and after competition</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Before competition, nutrition should support energy availability and gastrointestinal comfort. Carbohydrate availability can support glycogen-dependent demands; fat and protein have important roles but may alter meal digestion and comfort depending on amount and timing. An athlete’s established plan, event duration and individual tolerance matter more than one universal meal rule.","html":"Before competition, nutrition should support energy availability and gastrointestinal comfort. Carbohydrate availability can support glycogen-dependent demands; fat and protein have important roles but may alter meal digestion and comfort depending on amount and timing. An athlete’s established plan, event duration and individual tolerance matter more than one universal meal rule.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"During prolonged demanding activity, carbohydrate availability and hydration can help sustain performance, but needs depend on duration, intensity, conditions and access. After activity, carbohydrate can help replenish glycogen and protein supplies amino acids for repair and adaptation. Recovery also includes adequate overall energy, fluid and rest; a supplement is not automatically necessary.","html":"During prolonged demanding activity, carbohydrate availability and hydration can help sustain performance, but needs depend on duration, intensity, conditions and access. After activity, carbohydrate can help replenish glycogen and protein supplies amino acids for repair and adaptation. <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> also includes adequate overall energy, fluid and rest; a supplement is not automatically necessary.","diagram":"nutrition"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Glycaemic index describes the relative blood-glucose response to a standard amount of available carbohydrate in a food under test conditions. It is not a complete health rating or proof of how a mixed meal will behave for every athlete. Food amount, meal composition and timing affect practical responses, so high or low GI alone cannot dictate every decision.","html":"Glycaemic index describes the relative blood-glucose response to a standard amount of available carbohydrate in a food under test conditions. It is not a complete health rating or proof of how a mixed meal will behave for every athlete. Food amount, meal composition and timing affect practical responses, so high or low GI alone cannot dictate every decision.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Use pre-, during- and post-activity roles to explain a fictional case. Separate general principles from an individual diet prescription. When a real athlete needs a personal plan, their school programme and qualified sports-nutrition support can account for age, health, preferences and training demands. Classroom responses should not prescribe restriction or supplement doses.","html":"Use pre-, during- and post-activity roles to explain a fictional case. Separate general principles from an individual diet prescription. When a real athlete needs a personal plan, their school programme and qualified sports-nutrition support can account for age, health, preferences and training demands. Classroom responses should not prescribe restriction or supplement doses.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-competition-food-w1","prompt":"A player has repeated matches across a day. Explain different carbohydrate and protein roles and one limit of GI-only planning.","steps":["Carbohydrate supports fuel availability and replenishment of glycogen between demanding efforts.","Protein supplies amino acids for repair and adaptation within adequate overall intake.","GI alone omits food amount, mixed-meal composition, tolerance and timing, so it is not a complete plan."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-competition-food-p1","prompt":"Critique: all athletes must eat the same high-GI meal before every event and a recovery shake replaces sleep.","steps":["Pre-event needs vary with duration, timing, tolerance and established dietary plans.","GI is one food-response measure, not a universal meal rule.","Recovery depends on energy, nutrients, fluid and rest; a shake cannot replace sleep or automatically correct a dietary need."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-competition-food-p2","prompt":"Apply nutrition before, during and after competition to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Before competition, nutrition should support energy availability and gastrointestinal comfort. Carbohydrate availability can support glycogen-dependent demands; fat and protein have important roles but may alter meal digestion and comfort depending on amount and timing. An athlete’s established plan, event duration and individual tolerance matter more than one universal meal rule.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Education about nutrient roles is not a personalised dietary plan.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on nutrition before, during and after competition?","o":["Carbohydrate supports fuel availability and replenishment of glycogen between demanding efforts.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Carbohydrate supports fuel availability and replenishment of glycogen between demanding efforts. Education about nutrient roles is not a personalised dietary plan."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Education about nutrient roles is not a personalised dietary plan.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Education about nutrient roles is not a personalised dietary plan. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-hydration","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Hydration and interpreting fluid balance","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Hydration and interpreting fluid balance</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Water supports circulation, temperature regulation and many cellular functions. Exercise can produce fluid losses through sweating and breathing. The effect depends on workload, environment, clothing and individual sweat patterns. Being thirsty, having a high heart rate or sweating visibly is not by itself a complete assessment of hydration.","html":"Water supports circulation, temperature regulation and many cellular functions. Exercise can produce fluid losses through sweating and breathing. The effect depends on workload, environment, clothing and individual sweat patterns. Being thirsty, having a high heart rate or sweating visibly is not by itself a complete assessment of hydration.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A classroom estimate of sweat-related fluid balance can compare pre- and post-activity mass with recorded intake and output, under stated assumptions. Mass changes can also reflect food, clothing moisture and other processes. Approximate one kilogram of water as one litre only for the simplified calculation; do not interpret the model as a prescription to drink the computed amount immediately.","html":"A classroom estimate of sweat-related fluid balance can compare pre- and post-activity mass with recorded intake and output, under stated assumptions. Mass changes can also reflect food, clothing moisture and other processes. Approximate one kilogram of water as one litre only for the simplified calculation; do not interpret the model as a prescription to drink the computed amount immediately.","diagram":"heat"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Both insufficient fluid replacement and excessive drinking can be problematic. Hydration should be individualised within appropriate school and professional guidance. Evaporative cooling depends on sweat actually evaporating; high humidity can reduce the effectiveness of evaporation even when sweating is substantial. Fluid balance and heat exposure therefore need to be considered together.","html":"Both insufficient fluid replacement and excessive drinking can be problematic. Hydration should be individualised within appropriate school and professional guidance. Evaporative cooling depends on sweat actually evaporating; high humidity can reduce the effectiveness of evaporation even when sweating is substantial. Fluid balance and heat exposure therefore need to be considered together.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain the likely performance mechanism and identify what data would improve interpretation. Keep educational arithmetic distinct from treatment of symptoms. Practical tasks should use fictional values or teacher-approved procedures. The site does not provide a diagnosis, mandatory water quota or advice for managing a medical condition.","html":"Explain the likely performance mechanism and identify what data would improve interpretation. Keep educational arithmetic distinct from treatment of symptoms. Practical tasks should use fictional values or teacher-approved procedures. The site does not provide a diagnosis, mandatory water quota or advice for managing a medical condition.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-hydration-w1","prompt":"Fictional pre-mass is 70.0 kg, post-mass 69.4 kg, intake 0.4 L and measured urine 0.1 L. Estimate fluid loss using 1 kg ≈ 1 L.","steps":["Mass decrease = 0.6 kg ≈ 0.6 L in the simplified water model.","Estimated sweat/other unrecorded loss = 0.6 + 0.4 − 0.1 = 0.9 L.","Clothing, food and other mass changes can affect the estimate. It is not an individual drinking prescription."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-hydration-p1","prompt":"Explain why sweating heavily in humid weather does not prove effective cooling and why a fixed large drinking target is not universally suitable.","steps":["High humidity can reduce evaporation, so sweat may drip without removing the same heat as evaporated sweat.","Fluid needs depend on losses, duration, conditions and individual factors.","Excessive drinking can also be harmful; use the school’s appropriate guidance rather than a universal quota."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-hydration-p2","prompt":"Apply hydration and interpreting fluid balance to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Water supports circulation, temperature regulation and many cellular functions. Exercise can produce fluid losses through sweating and breathing. The effect depends on workload, environment, clothing and individual sweat patterns. Being thirsty, having a high heart rate or sweating visibly is not by itself a complete assessment of hydration.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Fictional arithmetic cannot diagnose or treat hydration status.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on hydration and interpreting fluid balance?","o":["Mass decrease = 0.6 kg ≈ 0.6 L in the simplified water model.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Mass decrease = 0.6 kg ≈ 0.6 L in the simplified water model. Fictional arithmetic cannot diagnose or treat hydration status."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Fictional arithmetic cannot diagnose or treat hydration status.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Fictional arithmetic cannot diagnose or treat hydration status. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-heat","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Heat transfer and hot, humid performance","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Heat transfer and hot, humid performance</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Radiation transfers thermal energy through electromagnetic emission between surfaces. Convection transfers heat through moving air or fluid. Conduction involves direct contact. Evaporation removes heat when liquid sweat changes to vapour. These mechanisms can transfer heat into or out of a body depending on the environment, except that evaporation of sweat at the skin removes heat from that surface.","html":"Radiation transfers thermal energy through electromagnetic emission between surfaces. Convection transfers heat through moving air or fluid. Conduction involves direct contact. Evaporation removes heat when liquid sweat changes to vapour. These mechanisms can <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark> heat into or out of a body depending on the environment, except that evaporation of sweat at the skin removes heat from that surface.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"In hot conditions, smaller temperature gradients can reduce dry heat loss; very hot air or radiant surroundings can contribute heat gain. Humidity reduces the vapour-pressure gradient supporting evaporation. Air movement can improve convective and evaporative exchange, but the outcome depends on temperature and humidity rather than wind always cooling identically.","html":"In hot conditions, smaller temperature gradients can reduce dry heat loss; very hot air or radiant surroundings can contribute heat gain. Humidity reduces the vapour-<mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> gradient supporting evaporation. Air <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> can improve convective and evaporative exchange, but the outcome depends on temperature and humidity rather than wind always cooling identically.","diagram":"heat"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Heat exposure can increase skin blood flow and sweating demands, adding cardiovascular strain during exercise. Acclimatisation through appropriately managed repeated exposure can change sweating and circulatory responses. It is not immediate and does not eliminate all heat risk. Schools should adjust activity to current conditions and applicable procedures.","html":"Heat exposure can increase skin blood flow and sweating demands, adding cardiovascular strain during exercise. Acclimatisation through appropriately managed repeated exposure can change sweating and circulatory responses. It is not immediate and does not eliminate all heat risk. Schools should adjust activity to current conditions and applicable procedures.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain mechanism, consequence and a context-appropriate management consideration such as scheduling, shade, clothing or planned breaks. Do not prescribe deliberate heat exposure or treat symptoms through this lesson. A fictional case can evaluate a written plan; actual heat decisions belong to the teacher and relevant current guidance.","html":"Explain mechanism, consequence and a context-appropriate management consideration such as scheduling, shade, clothing or planned breaks. Do not prescribe deliberate heat exposure or treat symptoms through this lesson. A fictional case can evaluate a written plan; actual heat decisions belong to the teacher and relevant current guidance.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-heat-w1","prompt":"A match is moved from a dry mild morning to a hot humid afternoon. Explain two heat-loss challenges and a planning response.","steps":["A smaller or reversed temperature gradient can reduce convection/radiation loss or add heat.","High humidity reduces effective sweat evaporation.","Review scheduling, breaks, shade and the school’s heat procedures; acclimatisation is not instant protection."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-heat-p1","prompt":"Classify touching a cool surface, moving air, emitted thermal radiation and sweat becoming vapour. Explain why more visible sweat may not mean more cooling.","steps":["Direct contact: conduction; moving air: convection; emitted energy: radiation; phase change: evaporation.","Sweat must evaporate to provide the relevant heat loss.","Humidity and dripping sweat limit the link between visible sweat quantity and actual cooling."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-heat-p2","prompt":"Apply heat transfer and hot, humid performance to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Radiation transfers thermal energy through electromagnetic emission between surfaces. Convection transfers heat through moving air or fluid. Conduction involves direct contact. Evaporation removes heat when liquid sweat changes to vapour. These mechanisms can transfer heat into or out of a body depending on the environment, except that evaporation of sweat at the skin removes heat from that surface.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The lesson does not prescribe heat exposure or manage heat illness.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on heat transfer and hot, humid performance?","o":["A smaller or reversed temperature gradient can reduce convection/radiation loss or add heat.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: A smaller or reversed temperature gradient can reduce convection/radiation loss or add heat. The lesson does not prescribe heat exposure or manage heat illness."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The lesson does not prescribe heat exposure or manage heat illness.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The lesson does not prescribe heat exposure or manage heat illness. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-cold-altitude","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Cold and altitude: mechanisms and adaptation","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Cold and altitude: mechanisms and adaptation</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Cold environments increase the potential for heat loss through radiation, convection and conduction, especially with wind or wet clothing. The body can reduce skin blood flow and generate heat through muscular activity such as shivering. Cold conditions can affect dexterity and comfort, but effects depend on exposure, clothing and activity rather than temperature alone.","html":"Cold environments increase the potential for heat loss through radiation, convection and conduction, especially with wind or wet clothing. The body can reduce skin blood flow and generate heat through muscular activity such as shivering. Cold conditions can affect dexterity and comfort, but effects depend on exposure, clothing and activity rather than temperature alone.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"At altitude, reduced atmospheric pressure lowers inspired oxygen partial pressure even though the oxygen fraction remains approximately similar. Less oxygen pressure can challenge oxygen loading and aerobic performance. Saying altitude air contains no oxygen confuses proportion with pressure. Initial responses can include increased ventilation and cardiovascular adjustments.","html":"At altitude, reduced atmospheric <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> lowers inspired <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark> partial pressure even though the oxygen fraction remains approximately similar. Less oxygen pressure can challenge oxygen loading and aerobic performance. Saying altitude air contains no oxygen confuses proportion with pressure. Initial responses can include increased ventilation and cardiovascular adjustments.","diagram":"environment"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"With appropriate time and exposure, acclimatisation can include changes in ventilation and red-cell production, but the response varies and does not immediately restore sea-level performance. Altitude training is not an automatic performance benefit for every athlete. Practical exposure and health decisions need qualified management, not a classroom travel recommendation.","html":"With appropriate time and exposure, acclimatisation can include changes in ventilation and red-cell production, but the response varies and does not immediately restore sea-level performance. Altitude training is not an automatic performance benefit for every athlete. Practical exposure and health decisions need qualified management, not a classroom travel recommendation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Compare mechanisms: cold mainly changes thermal demands, while altitude primarily changes oxygen-pressure conditions. Clothing and shelter address some cold constraints; altitude plans need realistic expectations and current professional guidance. A written response should explain cause and consequence without providing treatment or an unsupervised acclimatisation schedule.","html":"Compare mechanisms: cold mainly changes thermal demands, while altitude primarily changes <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark>-<mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> conditions. Clothing and shelter address some cold constraints; altitude plans need realistic expectations and current professional guidance. A written response should explain cause and consequence without providing treatment or an unsupervised acclimatisation schedule.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-cold-altitude-w1","prompt":"Correct: altitude reduces the oxygen percentage to zero, so an athlete adapts immediately by growing more red cells.","steps":["Altitude lowers atmospheric and inspired oxygen partial pressure; oxygen fraction is not zero.","Initial responses can include increased ventilation.","Red-cell-related adaptation requires time and varies; it is not an immediate guarantee of restored performance."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-cold-altitude-p1","prompt":"Compare a cold windy venue and a high-altitude venue using one mechanism, performance consequence and management consideration for each.","steps":["Cold wind increases convective heat loss, potentially affecting comfort and dexterity; suitable clothing and shelter planning matter.","Altitude reduces oxygen partial pressure, challenging aerobic supply; realistic timing and qualified exposure planning matter.","Do not equate thermal acclimatisation with oxygen-pressure adaptation or promise universal benefit."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-cold-altitude-p2","prompt":"Apply cold and altitude: mechanisms and adaptation to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Cold environments increase the potential for heat loss through radiation, convection and conduction, especially with wind or wet clothing. The body can reduce skin blood flow and generate heat through muscular activity such as shivering. Cold conditions can affect dexterity and comfort, but effects depend on exposure, clothing and activity rather than temperature alone.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: No travel, exposure schedule or treatment is prescribed.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on cold and altitude: mechanisms and adaptation?","o":["Altitude lowers atmospheric and inspired oxygen partial pressure; oxygen fraction is not zero.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Altitude lowers atmospheric and inspired oxygen partial pressure; oxygen fraction is not zero. No travel, exposure schedule or treatment is prescribed."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["No travel, exposure schedule or treatment is prescribed.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"No travel, exposure schedule or treatment is prescribed. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-supplements","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Protein powders, caffeine and creatine claims","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Protein powders, caffeine and creatine claims</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A supplement claim should be evaluated through purpose, evidence, safety and permitted status. Protein powders can offer a convenient protein source when there is an identified need, but they are not inherently superior to an adequate food-based intake. Extra protein beyond need does not automatically produce extra strength without an appropriate overall programme.","html":"A supplement claim should be evaluated through purpose, <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark>, safety and permitted status. Protein powders can offer a convenient protein source when there is an identified need, but they are not inherently superior to an adequate food-based intake. Extra protein beyond need does not automatically produce extra strength without an appropriate overall programme.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Caffeine can influence alertness and perceived effort and has evidence of performance effects in some contexts, with variable responses and possible sleep, anxiety or gastrointestinal effects. Creatine supports phosphocreatine-related availability and can benefit some repeated high-intensity demands. Neither is a universal endurance or skill solution, and individual effects differ.","html":"Caffeine can influence alertness and perceived effort and has <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> of performance effects in some contexts, with variable responses and possible sleep, anxiety or gastrointestinal effects. Creatine supports phosphocreatine-related availability and can benefit some repeated high-intensity demands. Neither is a universal endurance or skill solution, and individual effects differ.","diagram":"enhancers"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"AIS guidance emphasises assessing evidence and risk rather than trusting marketing. Products can contain undeclared or prohibited ingredients; a legal ingredient does not establish the safety or purity of every product. Caffeine, creatine and protein are not themselves examples of the prohibited anabolic or EPO categories in the current list, but products and rules require checking.","html":"AIS guidance emphasises assessing <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> and risk rather than trusting marketing. Products can contain undeclared or prohibited ingredients; a legal ingredient does not establish the safety or purity of every product. Caffeine, creatine and protein are not themselves examples of the prohibited anabolic or EPO categories in the current list, but products and rules require checking.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"This lesson compares mechanisms and claims without giving doses or recommending student supplement use. In an answer, link a plausible effect to the specific demand, acknowledge limits and identify product risk. Use current AIS and Sport Integrity Australia resources for status and policy, with qualified support for real decisions. More expensive is not evidence of more effective.","html":"This lesson compares mechanisms and claims without giving doses or recommending student supplement use. In an answer, link a plausible effect to the specific demand, acknowledge limits and identify product risk. Use current AIS and Sport Integrity Australia resources for status and policy, with qualified support for real decisions. More expensive is not <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> of more effective.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-supplements-w1","prompt":"A seller says a protein powder guarantees strength, caffeine guarantees calm sleep and creatine improves every sporting skill. Evaluate each claim.","steps":["Protein can support adequate amino-acid availability but does not guarantee strength or replace training and overall nutrition.","Caffeine effects vary and can disrupt sleep or increase unwanted activation.","Creatine can support some repeated high-intensity demands, not every skill or universal performance outcome; product risk still needs evaluation."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-supplements-p1","prompt":"Why does a permitted ingredient not prove a supplement product is safe or permitted? Use one relevant performance mechanism.","steps":["Products may have undeclared or contaminated ingredients and inaccurate labels.","Creatine’s mechanism concerns phosphocreatine-related availability, which may suit repeated intense work more than a claim of instant technique improvement.","Evaluate evidence, individual need and current rules through authoritative sources; this is not a use recommendation."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-supplements-p2","prompt":"Apply protein powders, caffeine and creatine claims to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A supplement claim should be evaluated through purpose, evidence, safety and permitted status. Protein powders can offer a convenient protein source when there is an identified need, but they are not inherently superior to an adequate food-based intake. Extra protein beyond need does not automatically produce extra strength without an appropriate overall programme.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: No supplement dose, product recommendation or student-use instruction is supplied.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on protein powders, caffeine and creatine claims?","o":["Protein can support adequate amino-acid availability but does not guarantee strength or replace training and overall nutrition.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Protein can support adequate amino-acid availability but does not guarantee strength or replace training and overall nutrition. No supplement dose, product recommendation or student-use instruction is supplied."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["No supplement dose, product recommendation or student-use instruction is supplied.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"No supplement dose, product recommendation or student-use instruction is supplied. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-doping","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Anabolic agents, EPO and blood doping","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Anabolic agents, EPO and blood doping</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Anabolic androgenic steroids can affect tissue-building processes and have been misused for strength or muscle-development aims. Misuse carries serious health risks, including cardiovascular and endocrine effects. A physiological effect does not establish acceptable, safe or fair use. Therapeutic medical decisions are separate from performance-doping claims.","html":"Anabolic androgenic steroids can affect tissue-building processes and have been misused for strength or <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark>-development aims. Misuse carries serious health risks, including cardiovascular and endocrine effects. A physiological effect does not establish acceptable, safe or fair use. Therapeutic medical decisions are separate from performance-doping claims.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"EPO stimulates red-cell production; misuse may seek increased oxygen-carrying capacity for aerobic demands. Blood doping manipulates blood or blood components for a similar performance aim. Increasing red-cell-related capacity can also increase serious risks, including circulatory complications. The body’s natural EPO physiology is not itself a prohibited act.","html":"EPO stimulates red-cell production; misuse may seek increased <mark class=\"pes-key\">oxygen</mark>-carrying capacity for aerobic demands. Blood doping manipulates blood or blood components for a similar performance aim. Increasing red-cell-related capacity can also increase serious risks, including circulatory complications. The body’s natural EPO physiology is not itself a prohibited act.","diagram":"enhancers"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"The current WADA list prohibits anabolic agents, EPO-related substances and specified blood-manipulation methods. Rules change, and exact substances, methods and medical exceptions must be checked through current authoritative resources. Protein powder, caffeine and creatine are not interchangeable with these categories; product contamination remains a separate risk.","html":"The current WADA list prohibits anabolic agents, EPO-related substances and specified blood-manipulation methods. Rules change, and exact substances, methods and medical exceptions must be checked through current authoritative resources. Protein powder, caffeine and creatine are not interchangeable with these categories; product contamination remains a separate risk.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"An exam response should state the proposed physiological effect, connect it to a sporting demand, identify risks and distinguish mechanism from ethical and regulatory status. This is analysis of prohibited practices, not instructions for obtaining, administering or concealing them. Do not present a performance benefit as a reason for a student to use a drug or procedure.","html":"An exam response should state the proposed physiological effect, connect it to a sporting demand, identify risks and distinguish mechanism from ethical and regulatory status. This is analysis of prohibited practices, not instructions for obtaining, administering or concealing them. Do not present a performance benefit as a reason for a student to use a drug or procedure.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-doping-w1","prompt":"Compare a claimed anabolic-steroid effect with an EPO-related effect, including sporting demand, risk and current status.","steps":["Anabolic misuse may seek tissue-building or strength effects; EPO misuse seeks increased red-cell production and oxygen transport for aerobic demands.","Both carry serious health risks; examples include endocrine/cardiovascular effects for steroid misuse and circulatory complications for blood-related misuse.","The relevant anabolic and EPO categories are prohibited in the current WADA list; check current authoritative rules for specifics."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-doping-p1","prompt":"Critique: because natural EPO exists, injecting any EPO product is permitted, and blood doping is only a nutrition strategy.","steps":["A natural hormone’s existence does not determine the permitted status of administered substances.","EPO-related substances and specified blood-manipulation methods are prohibited categories.","Blood doping is not ordinary nutrition; mechanism, medical context and current rules must be distinguished. No administration method is provided here."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-doping-p2","prompt":"Apply anabolic agents, epo and blood doping to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Anabolic androgenic steroids can affect tissue-building processes and have been misused for strength or muscle-development aims. Misuse carries serious health risks, including cardiovascular and endocrine effects. A physiological effect does not establish acceptable, safe or fair use. Therapeutic medical decisions are separate from performance-doping claims.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Current-rule links are provided; no doping or treatment instructions appear.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on anabolic agents, epo and blood doping?","o":["Anabolic misuse may seek tissue-building or strength effects; EPO misuse seeks increased red-cell production and oxygen transport for aerobic demands.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Anabolic misuse may seek tissue-building or strength effects; EPO misuse seeks increased red-cell production and oxygen transport for aerobic demands. Current-rule links are provided; no doping or treatment instructions appear."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Current-rule links are provided; no doping or treatment instructions appear.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Current-rule links are provided; no doping or treatment instructions appear. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-periodisation","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Microcycles, mesocycles and macrocycles","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Microcycles, mesocycles and macrocycles</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Periodisation organises training over time to manage changing demands and important performance periods. A macrocycle is the broad planning period, a mesocycle a block within it and a microcycle a shorter recurring planning unit. Their lengths depend on the programme; a microcycle is often a week but the terms are not fixed universal calendar units.","html":"Periodisation organises training over time to manage changing demands and important performance periods. A macrocycle is the broad planning period, a mesocycle a block within it and a microcycle a shorter recurring planning unit. Their lengths depend on the programme; a microcycle is often a week but the terms are not fixed universal calendar units.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Pre-season generally develops preparation for upcoming competition. In-season balances performance, practice, recovery and maintenance. Off-season or transition permits recovery and a changed training emphasis before the next cycle. A calendar label alone does not show which adaptations are being targeted or whether the workload is appropriate.","html":"Pre-season generally develops preparation for upcoming competition. In-season balances performance, practice, <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> and maintenance. Off-season or transition permits recovery and a changed training emphasis before the next cycle. A calendar label alone does not show which adaptations are being targeted or whether the workload is appropriate.","diagram":"periodisation"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Peaking aims to align readiness with a priority performance period. Tapering usually reduces training load while preserving useful stimulus before an event. It is not the same as abandoning all training for a long period. A programme can fail if it ignores competition congestion or repeatedly treats every event as requiring a maximal peak.","html":"Peaking aims to align readiness with a priority performance period. Tapering usually reduces training load while preserving useful stimulus before an event. It is not the same as abandoning all training for a long period. A programme can fail if it ignores competition congestion or repeatedly treats every event as requiring a maximal peak.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Interpret a fictional calendar by identifying nested cycles and changes in volume, intensity and recovery. Justify a change through demand and timing, then explain what monitoring would guide adjustment. Do not copy a generic elite schedule directly to students; the classroom task evaluates planning principles, not prescribes individual loads.","html":"Interpret a fictional calendar by identifying nested cycles and changes in volume, intensity and <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark>. Justify a change through demand and timing, then explain what monitoring would guide adjustment. Do not copy a generic elite schedule directly to students; the classroom task evaluates planning principles, not prescribes individual loads.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-periodisation-w1","prompt":"A season plan contains 12 weeks, three 4-week blocks and weekly schedules. Identify macro-, meso- and microcycle in this model.","steps":["The 12-week season plan is the stated macrocycle.","Each 4-week block is a mesocycle; each weekly schedule is a microcycle.","These are contextual labels, not universal required durations."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-periodisation-p1","prompt":"Explain why reducing volume before a priority event can differ from stopping all activity, and why in-season recovery matters.","steps":["A taper reduces load while preserving useful performance stimulus and readiness.","Complete prolonged cessation may remove stimulus and differ from a planned taper.","In-season competition adds workload, so recovery and maintenance must be balanced with practice and monitored readiness."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-periodisation-p2","prompt":"Apply microcycles, mesocycles and macrocycles to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Periodisation organises training over time to manage changing demands and important performance periods. A macrocycle is the broad planning period, a mesocycle a block within it and a microcycle a shorter recurring planning unit. Their lengths depend on the programme; a microcycle is often a week but the terms are not fixed universal calendar units.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Calendar lengths and volume bars are fictional examples.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on microcycles, mesocycles and macrocycles?","o":["The 12-week season plan is the stated macrocycle.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The 12-week season plan is the stated macrocycle. Calendar lengths and volume bars are fictional examples."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Calendar lengths and volume bars are fictional examples.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Calendar lengths and volume bars are fictional examples. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-recovery","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Exercise physiology","title":"Recovery, maintenance and overtraining evidence","subtitle":"Exercise physiology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Exercise physiology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Exercise physiology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Recovery, maintenance and overtraining evidence</h3><p>Exercise physiology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Recovery allows restoration and adaptation after training or competition. Rest, sleep, adequate nutrition and an appropriately planned load are foundational considerations. A fashionable recovery method cannot compensate automatically for excessive training or insufficient rest. Maintenance aims to preserve important adaptations when total training emphasis changes.","html":"<mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> allows restoration and adaptation after training or competition. Rest, sleep, adequate nutrition and an appropriately planned load are foundational considerations. A fashionable recovery method cannot compensate automatically for excessive training or insufficient rest. Maintenance aims to preserve important adaptations when total training emphasis changes.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Overtraining concerns a prolonged maladaptive state involving performance and recovery problems; it is not diagnosed by one tiring session. Possible warning patterns include persistent performance decline, unusual fatigue, disturbed mood or sleep and poor recovery. These signs are non-specific and can have other causes, so students should not self-diagnose from a checklist.","html":"Overtraining concerns a prolonged maladaptive state involving performance and <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> problems; it is not diagnosed by one tiring session. Possible warning patterns include persistent performance decline, unusual fatigue, disturbed mood or sleep and poor recovery. These signs are non-specific and can have other causes, so students should not self-diagnose from a checklist.","diagram":"periodisation"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A coach can use a diary, comparable performance measures and discussion to detect patterns needing review. Sudden unexplained changes warrant appropriate school and qualified support. A fictional dataset can show declining performance despite higher load, but it does not prove a medical explanation. Association and diagnosis are different claims.","html":"A coach can use a diary, comparable performance measures and discussion to detect patterns needing review. Sudden unexplained changes warrant appropriate school and qualified support. A fictional dataset can show declining performance despite higher load, but it does not prove a medical explanation. Association and diagnosis are different claims.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Evaluate a plan by linking load, competition and recovery across time. Explain why reducing or adjusting load might be considered within supervision, identify what information is missing and avoid guaranteeing a remedy. Maintaining fitness during a busy season can involve focused stimulus rather than adding every possible activity. Practical decisions should follow the school programme.","html":"Evaluate a plan by linking load, competition and <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> across time. Explain why reducing or adjusting load might be considered within supervision, identify what information is missing and avoid guaranteeing a remedy. Maintaining fitness during a busy season can involve focused stimulus rather than adding every possible activity. Practical decisions should follow the school programme.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-recovery-w1","prompt":"A fictional player records worsening comparable performance, poor sleep and rising workload over several weeks. What can and cannot be concluded?","steps":["The pattern suggests a need to review load and recovery.","It is consistent with possible maladaptation but does not diagnose overtraining; other causes may exist.","Use teacher and qualified support, more context and monitoring rather than treating one checklist as a medical conclusion."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-recovery-p1","prompt":"Explain maintenance during a busy competition period and why one hard session is not proof of overtraining.","steps":["Maintenance seeks to preserve key adaptations with an appropriate stimulus while balancing competition and recovery.","Acute tiredness after one session differs from a sustained maladaptive pattern.","Interpret duration, comparable performance and context; the task does not diagnose or prescribe treatment."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-recovery-p2","prompt":"Apply recovery, maintenance and overtraining evidence to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Recovery allows restoration and adaptation after training or competition. Rest, sleep, adequate nutrition and an appropriately planned load are foundational considerations. A fashionable recovery method cannot compensate automatically for excessive training or insufficient rest. Maintenance aims to preserve important adaptations when total training emphasis changes.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Warning signs are non-specific and do not form a diagnostic tool.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on recovery, maintenance and overtraining evidence?","o":["The pattern suggests a need to review load and recovery.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The pattern suggests a need to review load and recovery. Warning signs are non-specific and do not form a diagnostic tool."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Warning signs are non-specific and do not form a diagnostic tool.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Warning signs are non-specific and do not form a diagnostic tool. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-advanced-tactics","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Muscle function, performance and planning","area":"Skills and tactics","title":"Adapt tactics to opposition and conditions","subtitle":"Skills and tactics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Skills and tactics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Skills and tactics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Adapt tactics to opposition and conditions</h3><p>Skills and tactics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Advanced tactical decisions combine skill selection with opponent information, environmental constraints and phase of play. An opponent’s strengths and weaknesses can influence risk, spacing and target selection. The best response is not simply repeating a favourite action; it depends on whether that action remains effective in the current situation.","html":"Advanced tactical decisions combine skill selection with opponent information, environmental constraints and phase of play. An opponent’s strengths and weaknesses can influence risk, spacing and target selection. The best response is not simply repeating a favourite action; it depends on whether that action remains effective in the current situation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"In tennis, repeatedly attacking a weaker side may be useful until the opponent adjusts position. In an invasion game, a change in wind, surface or opponent pressure can alter passing and support decisions. The stated evidence must support the recommendation. A claim about an opponent’s weakness needs observation rather than assumption from appearance.","html":"In tennis, repeatedly attacking a weaker side may be useful until the opponent adjusts position. In an invasion game, a change in wind, surface or opponent <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> can alter passing and support decisions. The stated <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> must support the recommendation. A claim about an opponent’s weakness needs observation rather than assumption from appearance.","diagram":"tactics"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Phases of play include possession, defence and transition, with sport-specific distinctions. A turnover can require rapidly shifting from attack to recovery positioning. The same space that helps an attack can expose a defence. Explain how individual choices contribute to the team’s coordinated response rather than analysing a player in isolation.","html":"Phases of play include possession, defence and transition, with sport-specific distinctions. A turnover can require rapidly shifting from attack to <mark class=\"pes-key\">recovery</mark> positioning. The same space that helps an attack can expose a defence. Explain how individual choices contribute to the team’s coordinated response rather than analysing a player in isolation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Propose at least two options, select one with a reason and state a condition that would change it. Then identify a way to evaluate the decision through comparable observation. The site supports written tactical reasoning; competitive skill execution and the school-based practical external assessment need actual teacher-managed participation in the school’s selected sport.","html":"Propose at least two options, select one with a reason and state a condition that would change it. Then identify a way to evaluate the decision through comparable observation. The site supports written tactical reasoning; competitive skill execution and the school-based practical external assessment need actual teacher-managed participation in the school’s selected sport.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-advanced-tactics-w1","prompt":"A soccer team repeatedly loses possession through long aerial passes in strong wind. Propose and justify an adaptation and a limitation.","steps":["Shorter controlled passes with closer support may reduce exposure to unpredictable aerial flight.","The choice responds to wind and observed possession losses.","It can also attract pressure or slow progress; opponent positioning and phase may justify a different option."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-advanced-tactics-p1","prompt":"A tennis opponent initially struggles with wide backhand shots but changes position to cover them. Explain an appropriate next decision and how to test it.","steps":["Reassess open space rather than continuing the same target automatically.","An alternative placement can exploit the newly opened area if execution and risk are suitable.","Observe comparable rallies and opponent response, separating tactical decision from outcome and technical error."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-advanced-tactics-p2","prompt":"Apply adapt tactics to opposition and conditions to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Advanced tactical decisions combine skill selection with opponent information, environmental constraints and phase of play. An opponent’s strengths and weaknesses can influence risk, spacing and target selection. The best response is not simply repeating a favourite action; it depends on whether that action remains effective in the current situation.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Apply to the prescribed sport selected by the school, with practical feedback.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on adapt tactics to opposition and conditions?","o":["Shorter controlled passes with closer support may reduce exposure to unpredictable aerial flight.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Shorter controlled passes with closer support may reduce exposure to unpredictable aerial flight. Apply to the prescribed sport selected by the school, with practical feedback."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Apply to the prescribed sport selected by the school, with practical feedback.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Apply to the prescribed sport selected by the school, with practical feedback. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-transfer","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Positive, negative and zero transfer","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Positive, negative and zero transfer</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Transfer of learning concerns the influence of previous learning on a new or later task. Positive transfer helps the target task; negative transfer interferes; zero transfer has little detectable effect in the stated conditions. Similar-looking skills do not necessarily transfer positively if timing, equipment or rules differ.","html":"<mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark> of learning concerns the influence of previous learning on a new or later task. Positive transfer helps the target task; negative transfer interferes; zero transfer has little detectable effect in the stated conditions. Similar-looking skills do not necessarily transfer positively if timing, equipment or rules differ.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Categories include skill-to-skill, theory-to-practice and training-to-competition transfer. These categories identify where learning moves, while positive, negative or zero identifies the observed effect. A theory lesson can transfer positively when it supports a useful practical decision, but simply remembering a definition does not establish improved performance.","html":"Categories include skill-to-skill, theory-to-practice and training-to-competition <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark>. These categories identify where learning moves, while positive, negative or zero identifies the observed effect. A theory lesson can transfer positively when it supports a useful practical decision, but simply remembering a definition does not establish improved performance.","diagram":"transfer"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Practice identical to a predictable drill may fail to represent competitive information demands. Transfer can improve when practice includes relevant perception, decisions and realistic constraints. However, adding complexity before a learner can perform the core action can make practice unmanageable. Selection should reflect current needs rather than copying competition in every detail at every stage.","html":"Practice identical to a predictable drill may fail to represent competitive information demands. <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark> can improve when practice includes relevant perception, decisions and realistic constraints. However, adding complexity before a learner can perform the core action can make practice unmanageable. Selection should reflect current needs rather than copying competition in every detail at every stage.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Describe the source task, target task and specific feature expected to transfer. Then use evidence to judge its effect. Negative transfer is not simply any poor performance after practice; other causes must be considered. A well-designed comparison includes similar conditions and repeated observations, with a clear criterion for improvement.","html":"Describe the source task, target task and specific feature expected to <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark>. Then use <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> to judge its effect. Negative transfer is not simply any poor performance after practice; other causes must be considered. A well-designed comparison includes similar conditions and repeated observations, with a clear criterion for improvement.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-transfer-w1","prompt":"A player applies a familiar racket timing to a new sport but contacts too late. Identify category and possible effect, with a qualification.","steps":["The category is skill-to-skill transfer.","The old timing may interfere, suggesting negative transfer.","Repeated evidence and other factors are needed; one late contact does not prove the old skill caused it."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-transfer-p1","prompt":"A team’s passing drill is accurate without defenders but decisions fail in games. Analyse training-to-competition transfer and a practice adjustment.","steps":["The predictable drill may omit perception and response-selection demands.","Add appropriate defender movement and decision constraints through a manageable progression.","Evaluate game-relevant decisions as well as drill accuracy; category and effect are separate labels."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-transfer-p2","prompt":"Apply positive, negative and zero transfer to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Transfer of learning concerns the influence of previous learning on a new or later task. Positive transfer helps the target task; negative transfer interferes; zero transfer has little detectable effect in the stated conditions. Similar-looking skills do not necessarily transfer positively if timing, equipment or rules differ.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Transfer effects require evidence, not similarity alone.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on positive, negative and zero transfer?","o":["The category is skill-to-skill transfer.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The category is skill-to-skill transfer. Transfer effects require evidence, not similarity alone."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Transfer effects require evidence, not similarity alone.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Transfer effects require evidence, not similarity alone. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-analysis-model","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Prepare, observe, evaluate, intervene, re-observe","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Prepare, observe, evaluate, intervene, re-observe</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"The Knudson and Morrison approach structures qualitative movement analysis through preparation, observation, evaluation, intervention and re-observation. Preparation identifies the task, relevant movement features and how evidence will be collected. Observation records what occurs without immediately replacing it with a judgement.","html":"The Knudson and Morrison approach structures qualitative <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> analysis through preparation, observation, evaluation, intervention and re-observation. Preparation identifies the task, relevant movement features and how <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> will be collected. Observation records what occurs without immediately replacing it with a judgement.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Evaluation compares the observations with meaningful criteria, identifies priorities and considers possible explanations. Intervention introduces an appropriate cue, practice condition or correction. Re-observation checks whether the change improved the priority feature or produced an unintended effect. The cycle is iterative rather than one final diagnosis from one image.","html":"Evaluation compares the observations with meaningful criteria, identifies priorities and considers possible explanations. Intervention introduces an appropriate cue, practice condition or correction. Re-observation checks whether the change improved the priority feature or produced an unintended effect. The cycle is iterative rather than one final diagnosis from one image.","diagram":"analysis"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Good observation uses a suitable viewing angle and enough comparable attempts. A camera may hide a joint or distort apparent angles. A checklist can focus attention, but its criteria must be relevant and observable. Movement analysis should avoid claiming an injury, exact muscle force or private mental state from a video alone.","html":"Good observation uses a suitable viewing angle and enough comparable attempts. A camera may hide a joint or distort apparent angles. A checklist can focus <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark>, but its criteria must be relevant and observable. <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> analysis should avoid claiming an injury, exact <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> or private mental state from a video alone.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Choose one justified priority instead of correcting everything at once. Match the intervention to the learner and allow time to use it. Compare before and after under similar conditions and distinguish association from certainty of cause. This is a coaching-analysis model, not a clinical assessment method.","html":"Choose one justified priority instead of correcting everything at once. Match the intervention to the learner and allow time to use it. Compare before and after under similar conditions and distinguish association from certainty of cause. This is a coaching-analysis model, not a clinical assessment method.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-analysis-model-w1","prompt":"Outline the five stages for analysing inconsistent serve contact, including evidence needed before intervention.","steps":["Prepare: define contact criteria and a useful camera/viewing position. Observe: record several comparable attempts.","Evaluate: identify the most supported priority. Intervene: use a suitable focused cue or practice condition.","Re-observe: compare the same feature and outcome under similar conditions, checking unintended changes."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-analysis-model-p1","prompt":"A coach judges one blurry clip and gives five corrections without another observation. Evaluate the missing parts of the model.","steps":["Preparation and observation are weak because viewing quality and repeated comparable evidence are lacking.","Evaluation cannot reliably prioritise from that evidence; five simultaneous corrections may overload the learner.","Re-observation is missing, so the effect of intervention has not been tested."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-analysis-model-p2","prompt":"Apply prepare, observe, evaluate, intervene, re-observe to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: The Knudson and Morrison approach structures qualitative movement analysis through preparation, observation, evaluation, intervention and re-observation. Preparation identifies the task, relevant movement features and how evidence will be collected. Observation records what occurs without immediately replacing it with a judgement.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Qualitative coaching analysis is not a medical diagnosis.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on prepare, observe, evaluate, intervene, re-observe?","o":["Prepare: define contact criteria and a useful camera/viewing position. Observe: record several comparable attempts.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Prepare: define contact criteria and a useful camera/viewing position. Observe: record several comparable attempts. Qualitative coaching analysis is not a medical diagnosis."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Qualitative coaching analysis is not a medical diagnosis.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Qualitative coaching analysis is not a medical diagnosis. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-practice-progressions","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Shaping, chaining and static-to-dynamic progression","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Shaping, chaining and static-to-dynamic progression</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Shaping gradually develops a skill by modifying difficulty or conditions towards the target action. Chaining links component actions into a sequence. A static-to-dynamic progression moves from comparatively stable practice towards movement and changing conditions. These approaches can overlap but emphasise different features of learning.","html":"Shaping gradually develops a skill by modifying difficulty or conditions towards the target action. Chaining links component actions into a sequence. A static-to-dynamic progression moves from comparatively stable practice towards <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> and changing conditions. These approaches can overlap but emphasise different features of learning.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A learner may first practise a simplified whole serve, then progressively approach full conditions: shaping. Linking footwork, preparation and contact into a coordinated sequence illustrates chaining. Performing a pass stationary, then while moving and then with opposition illustrates static-to-dynamic progression. The labels should be explained through the actual practice design.","html":"A learner may first practise a simplified whole serve, then progressively approach full conditions: shaping. Linking footwork, preparation and contact into a coordinated sequence illustrates chaining. Performing a pass stationary, then while moving and then with opposition illustrates static-to-dynamic progression. The labels should be explained through the actual practice design.","diagram":"learning"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Breaking a skill into parts is useful only if the parts can be reconnected meaningfully. Some skills depend strongly on rhythm and segment interaction, so isolated practice can distort the target action. Progressions should preserve relevant information and increase difficulty when the learner is ready, not simply on a fixed calendar.","html":"Breaking a skill into parts is useful only if the parts can be reconnected meaningfully. Some skills depend strongly on rhythm and segment interaction, so isolated practice can distort the target action. Progressions should preserve relevant information and increase difficulty when the learner is ready, not simply on a fixed calendar.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Justify the chosen method through learner needs and task structure. State a criterion for progressing, such as consistent execution and appropriate decisions under the current conditions. Then explain how the final activity supports transfer. A written progression is a planning exercise; actual drills and loads require the teacher’s practical judgement.","html":"Justify the chosen method through learner needs and task structure. State a criterion for progressing, such as consistent execution and appropriate decisions under the current conditions. Then explain how the final activity supports <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark>. A written progression is a planning exercise; actual drills and loads require the teacher’s practical judgement.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-practice-progressions-w1","prompt":"Classify simplifying a whole serve then increasing difficulty, linking three movement parts, and passing stationary then while moving.","steps":["Simplifying then progressively developing the whole serve is shaping.","Linking parts is chaining.","Moving from stationary to moving passing is static-to-dynamic progression; methods may overlap in one plan."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-practice-progressions-p1","prompt":"Why can practising every part of a throw separately fail to preserve the whole skill? Propose a criterion for reconnecting parts.","steps":["The throw depends on rhythm and sequential segment interaction.","Isolating parts can alter timing, so reconnect them into relevant whole actions.","Use an observable consistency and timing criterion, then add manageable game constraints; do not assume more parts always improve learning."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-practice-progressions-p2","prompt":"Apply shaping, chaining and static-to-dynamic progression to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Shaping gradually develops a skill by modifying difficulty or conditions towards the target action. Chaining links component actions into a sequence. A static-to-dynamic progression moves from comparatively stable practice towards movement and changing conditions. These approaches can overlap but emphasise different features of learning.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Progression examples are planning concepts, not mandatory drills.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on shaping, chaining and static-to-dynamic progression?","o":["Simplifying then progressively developing the whole serve is shaping.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Simplifying then progressively developing the whole serve is shaping. Progression examples are planning concepts, not mandatory drills."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Progression examples are planning concepts, not mandatory drills.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Progression examples are planning concepts, not mandatory drills. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-leadership","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Choose a leadership style for the situation","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Choose a leadership style for the situation</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Autocratic leadership concentrates decisions with the leader and can provide clear rapid direction. Democratic leadership involves participants in suitable decisions and can build shared understanding and commitment. Laissez-faire leadership provides substantial autonomy and limited direct control. None is automatically best for every athlete or every situation.","html":"Autocratic leadership concentrates decisions with the leader and can provide clear rapid direction. Democratic leadership involves participants in suitable decisions and can build shared understanding and commitment. Laissez-faire leadership provides substantial autonomy and limited direct control. None is automatically best for every athlete or every situation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A new group facing a complex unfamiliar task may need clear instruction. An experienced team reviewing tactics may benefit from discussion and shared decisions. A capable independent athlete may use autonomy effectively, but unclear expectations or insufficient support can make a laissez-faire approach ineffective. The actual needs and constraints matter.","html":"A new group facing a complex unfamiliar task may need clear instruction. An experienced team reviewing tactics may benefit from discussion and shared decisions. A capable independent athlete may use autonomy effectively, but unclear expectations or insufficient support can make a laissez-faire approach ineffective. The actual needs and constraints matter.","diagram":"cohesion"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Leadership affects communication, motivation and cohesion. Democratic involvement does not mean every decision must be voted on, and autocratic instruction does not justify disrespect. A leader can change style across tasks while retaining consistent standards and a supportive relationship. Effective autonomy still needs agreed goals and responsibilities.","html":"Leadership affects communication, motivation and cohesion. Democratic involvement does not mean every decision must be voted on, and autocratic instruction does not justify disrespect. A leader can change style across tasks while retaining consistent standards and a supportive relationship. Effective autonomy still needs agreed goals and responsibilities.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Compare advantages and limitations with a concrete scenario. Identify urgency, experience, task complexity and the need for commitment. Explain why the style fits and what would prompt adjustment. Avoid assigning styles from stereotypes about age, gender or sport; use observed readiness and the decision context.","html":"Compare advantages and limitations with a concrete scenario. Identify urgency, experience, task complexity and the need for commitment. Explain why the style fits and what would prompt adjustment. Avoid assigning styles from stereotypes about age, gender or sport; use observed readiness and the decision context.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-leadership-w1","prompt":"Compare leadership for introducing an unfamiliar activity and reviewing tactics with an experienced team.","steps":["Clear direct instruction can suit an unfamiliar task where shared basic understanding is needed.","Democratic discussion can draw on experienced players’ observations during tactical review.","Both require respectful communication and clear goals; suitability depends on context, not a universally superior style."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-leadership-p1","prompt":"Evaluate laissez-faire leadership when a team has no agreed roles and inconsistent attendance. Suggest a context where autonomy may work better.","steps":["Limited direction can worsen ambiguity when expectations and responsibility are unclear.","Establish agreed roles, communication and goals before relying on autonomy.","A capable self-managing athlete with clear expectations may benefit from greater independence and access to support."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-leadership-p2","prompt":"Apply choose a leadership style for the situation to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Autocratic leadership concentrates decisions with the leader and can provide clear rapid direction. Democratic leadership involves participants in suitable decisions and can build shared understanding and commitment. Laissez-faire leadership provides substantial autonomy and limited direct control. None is automatically best for every athlete or every situation.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Leadership style is a context-dependent choice, not a fixed character label.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on choose a leadership style for the situation?","o":["Clear direct instruction can suit an unfamiliar task where shared basic understanding is needed.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Clear direct instruction can suit an unfamiliar task where shared basic understanding is needed. Leadership style is a context-dependent choice, not a fixed character label."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Leadership style is a context-dependent choice, not a fixed character label.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Leadership style is a context-dependent choice, not a fixed character label. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-evidence-tools","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Motor learning and coaching","title":"Video, checklists and feedback evidence","subtitle":"Motor learning and coaching · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Motor learning and coaching · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Motor learning and coaching"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Video, checklists and feedback evidence</h3><p>Motor learning and coaching · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Video can preserve movement for repeated observation and comparison. Camera angle, frame rate, perspective and occlusion affect what can be judged. A two-dimensional view cannot automatically show a three-dimensional joint angle accurately. Select evidence appropriate to the question rather than treating any slow-motion clip as a complete analysis.","html":"Video can preserve <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> for repeated observation and comparison. Camera angle, frame rate, perspective and occlusion affect what can be judged. A two-dimensional view cannot automatically show a three-dimensional joint angle accurately. Select <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> appropriate to the question rather than treating any slow-motion clip as a complete analysis.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A checklist makes selected criteria explicit and can improve consistency across observations. Criteria should be observable and task-relevant. Peer, mentor and coach feedback supply different perspectives, but all need evidence and respectful communication. A questionnaire can reveal perceived experience or confidence, not directly measure muscle activation or actual skill quality.","html":"A checklist makes selected criteria explicit and can improve consistency across observations. Criteria should be observable and task-relevant. Peer, mentor and coach <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> supply different perspectives, but all need <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> and respectful communication. A questionnaire can reveal perceived experience or confidence, not directly measure <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> activation or actual skill quality.","diagram":"analysis"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Combine evidence sources when they answer different parts of the question. Video may show an execution feature, a results log an outcome pattern and a questionnaire the learner’s interpretation. Agreement strengthens an account but does not remove all uncertainty. Disagreement can identify a need for better data rather than proof that one participant is dishonest.","html":"Combine <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> sources when they answer different parts of the question. Video may show an execution feature, a results log an outcome pattern and a questionnaire the learner’s interpretation. Agreement strengthens an account but does not remove all uncertainty. Disagreement can identify a need for better data rather than proof that one participant is dishonest.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Plan comparable before-and-after observations and avoid measuring many irrelevant variables. Report what was observed, how it was collected and the limit. Student-created analysis should respect school consent and recording procedures. The site uses fictional scenarios and does not request uploads or record practical performance.","html":"Plan comparable before-and-after observations and avoid measuring many irrelevant variables. Report what was observed, how it was collected and the limit. Student-created analysis should respect school consent and recording procedures. The site uses fictional scenarios and does not request uploads or record practical performance.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-evidence-tools-w1","prompt":"A side-on clip hides the contact point while a checklist rates contact accuracy. Explain the validity problem and an improvement.","steps":["The camera does not show the feature needed for the criterion.","Use an appropriate viewing position and several comparable attempts, with permission under school procedures.","A checklist does not fix missing evidence; state uncertainty rather than assigning a confident rating."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-evidence-tools-p1","prompt":"Compare what a performance video, outcome log and confidence questionnaire can establish.","steps":["Video can show visible execution features within its viewing limits.","An outcome log records results but does not alone identify causes.","A questionnaire reports perceived confidence, not direct muscle activity or proven skill quality; combine sources for different questions."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-evidence-tools-p2","prompt":"Apply video, checklists and feedback evidence to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Video can preserve movement for repeated observation and comparison. Camera angle, frame rate, perspective and occlusion affect what can be judged. A two-dimensional view cannot automatically show a three-dimensional joint angle accurately. Select evidence appropriate to the question rather than treating any slow-motion clip as a complete analysis.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: No student recording or personal data upload is required.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on video, checklists and feedback evidence?","o":["The camera does not show the feature needed for the criterion.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The camera does not show the feature needed for the criterion. No student recording or personal data upload is required."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["No student recording or personal data upload is required.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"No student recording or personal data upload is required. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-impulse","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Impulse and momentum","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Impulse and momentum</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Linear momentum equals mass multiplied by velocity and includes direction. Impulse equals the change in momentum. For a constant or average net force over an interval, impulse equals force multiplied by time. On a force–time graph, signed area gives impulse. State the body and direction before substituting numbers.","html":"Linear momentum equals mass multiplied by <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> and includes direction. Impulse equals the change in momentum. For a constant or average net <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> over an interval, impulse equals force multiplied by time. On a force–time graph, signed area gives impulse. State the body and direction before substituting numbers.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A greater impulse can produce a larger velocity change for the same mass. For a fixed change in momentum, increasing stopping time can reduce average net force. Catching with a longer controlled deceleration is an example of this principle, although actual technique and peak forces are more complex than a rectangular force–time model.","html":"A greater impulse can produce a larger <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> change for the same mass. For a fixed change in momentum, increasing stopping time can reduce average net <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>. Catching with a longer controlled deceleration is an example of this principle, although actual technique and peak forces are more complex than a rectangular force–time model.","diagram":"impulse"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Applied force and net force are not always the same. Gravity and other forces may contribute during a contact. A force–time graph can have a varying shape, so average force does not reveal peak force. Two curves with equal area can have equal impulse but different peaks and durations.","html":"Applied <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> and net force are not always the same. Gravity and other forces may contribute during a contact. A force–time graph can have a varying shape, so average force does not reveal peak force. Two curves with equal area can have equal impulse but different peaks and durations.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Use units of kg·m/s for momentum and N·s for impulse. Explain the result as a change in velocity or momentum, not simply an increase in speed regardless of direction. A ball reversed in direction can experience a large momentum change. The calculation supports conceptual analysis rather than prescribing contact forces for students.","html":"Use units of kg·m/s for momentum and N·s for impulse. Explain the result as a change in <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> or momentum, not simply an increase in speed regardless of direction. A ball reversed in direction can experience a large momentum change. The calculation supports conceptual analysis rather than prescribing contact forces for students.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"impulse"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-impulse-w1","prompt":"A 0.5 kg ball moves at +8 m/s and is stopped in 0.20 s. Calculate change in momentum and average net force.","steps":["Δp = 0.5 × (0 − 8) = −4 kg·m/s.","Average net force = −4 ÷ 0.20 = −20 N.","The force acts opposite the initial motion. The value is an average, not the peak contact force."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-impulse-p1","prompt":"The same momentum change occurs over 0.40 s. Calculate average force and explain what remains unchanged.","steps":["Average force = −4 ÷ 0.40 = −10 N.","The longer interval halves average force for this fixed impulse.","Momentum change remains −4 kg·m/s; peak force and actual force shape are not specified."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-impulse-p2","prompt":"Apply impulse and momentum to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Linear momentum equals mass multiplied by velocity and includes direction. Impulse equals the change in momentum. For a constant or average net force over an interval, impulse equals force multiplied by time. On a force–time graph, signed area gives impulse. State the body and direction before substituting numbers.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Force calculations use fictional net-force conditions and are not practical contact advice.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on impulse and momentum?","o":["Δp = 0.5 × (0 − 8) = −4 kg·m/s.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Δp = 0.5 × (0 − 8) = −4 kg·m/s. Force calculations use fictional net-force conditions and are not practical contact advice."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Force calculations use fictional net-force conditions and are not practical contact advice.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Force calculations use fictional net-force conditions and are not practical contact advice. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-restitution","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Coefficient of restitution and interacting bodies","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Coefficient of restitution and interacting bodies</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Coefficient of restitution describes the relative speed of separation compared with relative speed of approach along the collision direction. It concerns both interacting bodies, not only a permanent property of one ball. Temperature, material, equipment, surface and impact speed can affect collision behaviour.","html":"Coefficient of restitution describes the relative speed of separation compared with relative speed of approach along the collision direction. It concerns both interacting bodies, not only a permanent property of one ball. Temperature, material, equipment, surface and impact speed can affect collision behaviour.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"In a simplified vertical drop and rebound from a fixed surface, with negligible drag and the same height reference, e can be estimated as the square root of rebound height divided by drop height. The square root matters because speed relates to the square root of height in this energy model. The rebound-to-drop height ratio alone is not e.","html":"In a simplified vertical drop and rebound from a fixed surface, with negligible drag and the same height reference, e can be estimated as the square root of rebound height divided by drop height. The square root matters because speed relates to the square root of height in this energy model. The rebound-to-drop height ratio alone is not e.","diagram":"bounce"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"An ideal elastic collision has e = 1 in the simple model, while a collision with no separation speed along the impact direction has e = 0. Real sport collisions can be more complicated through spin, deformation and surface motion. A ball that rebounds lower does not show that all its lost mechanical energy vanished; energy can become heat, sound and deformation.","html":"An ideal elastic collision has e = 1 in the simple model, while a collision with no separation speed along the impact direction has e = 0. Real sport collisions can be more complicated through spin, deformation and surface motion. A ball that rebounds lower does not show that all its lost mechanical energy vanished; energy can become heat, sound and deformation.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Compare controlled conditions rather than changing ball, temperature and surface simultaneously. Identify which feature was varied and what the evidence supports. A higher rebound can affect sport response and timing, but does not automatically make equipment better or permitted. Practical equipment choices should follow school and sport rules.","html":"Compare controlled conditions rather than changing ball, temperature and surface simultaneously. Identify which feature was varied and what the <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> supports. A higher rebound can affect sport response and timing, but does not automatically make equipment better or permitted. Practical equipment choices should follow school and sport rules.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-restitution-w1","prompt":"An idealised ball drops 1.00 m and rebounds 0.64 m from a fixed surface. Estimate e and explain the height-ratio error.","steps":["e = √(0.64 ÷ 1.00) = 0.8.","The height ratio is 0.64, not e, because collision speed relates to the square root of height.","The estimate assumes vertical flight, fixed surface, negligible drag and a common reference."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-restitution-p1","prompt":"Two balls rebound differently on different surfaces at different temperatures. Can the difference be attributed only to ball material?","steps":["No: ball material, surface and temperature changed together.","Control other conditions and compare repeated impacts if investigating material.","Restitution concerns interacting bodies and impact conditions; rebound height alone does not identify one cause."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-restitution-p2","prompt":"Apply coefficient of restitution and interacting bodies to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Coefficient of restitution describes the relative speed of separation compared with relative speed of approach along the collision direction. It concerns both interacting bodies, not only a permanent property of one ball. Temperature, material, equipment, surface and impact speed can affect collision behaviour.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The square-root model applies only under its stated vertical-bounce assumptions.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on coefficient of restitution and interacting bodies?","o":["e = √(0.64 ÷ 1.00) = 0.8.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: e = √(0.64 ÷ 1.00) = 0.8. The square-root model applies only under its stated vertical-bounce assumptions."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The square-root model applies only under its stated vertical-bounce assumptions.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The square-root model applies only under its stated vertical-bounce assumptions. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-torque","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Third-class levers and joint torque","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Third-class levers and joint torque</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Torque is the turning effect of a force about an axis. Its magnitude is force multiplied by the perpendicular moment arm. A joint can balance external load torque with muscle torque in a simplified static condition. The force applied near a joint may need to be much larger than a distal load when its moment arm is short.","html":"Torque is the turning effect of a <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> about an axis. Its magnitude is force multiplied by the perpendicular moment arm. A joint can balance external load torque with <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> torque in a simplified static condition. The force applied near a joint may need to be much larger than a distal load when its moment arm is short.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"In a third-class lever, muscle effort acts between the joint axis and resistance. The short effort arm often creates a force disadvantage but permits useful distal speed and range. As joint angle changes, the perpendicular moment arm can change. Distance along the bone is not automatically the moment arm of an angled force.","html":"In a third-class lever, <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> effort acts between the joint axis and resistance. The short effort arm often creates a <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> disadvantage but permits useful distal speed and range. As joint angle changes, the perpendicular moment arm can change. Distance along the bone is not automatically the moment arm of an angled force.","diagram":"lever"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"For static equilibrium, opposing torques balance when all relevant forces and moments are included. A simplified model may omit limb weight and co-contraction; state those omissions. Dynamic movement requires net torque to change rotational motion, so a static calculation cannot be applied unchanged during acceleration.","html":"For static equilibrium, opposing torques balance when all relevant forces and moments are included. A simplified model may omit limb weight and co-contraction; state those omissions. Dynamic <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> requires net torque to change rotational motion, so a static calculation cannot be applied unchanged during acceleration.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Show the force lines, perpendicular distances, units and balance equation. Explain why a longer load arm increases required balancing torque. Do not turn a classroom model into an unsupervised lifting recommendation. Real muscle force depends on anatomy, posture and other forces that the simple model leaves out.","html":"Show the <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> lines, perpendicular distances, units and balance equation. Explain why a longer load arm increases required balancing torque. Do not turn a classroom model into an unsupervised lifting recommendation. Real <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> force depends on anatomy, posture and other forces that the simple model leaves out.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"torque"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-torque-w1","prompt":"A 20 N load acts with a 0.30 m moment arm. Muscle moment arm is 0.03 m. Ignoring other forces, find balancing muscle force.","steps":["Load torque = 20 × 0.30 = 6 N·m.","Muscle force × 0.03 = 6, so force = 200 N.","The short effort arm creates a force disadvantage. This static model omits limb weight and other forces."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-torque-p1","prompt":"The load moment arm becomes 0.15 m while force remains 20 N. Calculate torque and explain the meaning of perpendicular distance.","steps":["Torque = 20 × 0.15 = 3 N·m.","The moment arm is the shortest perpendicular distance from the axis to the force’s line of action.","An angled force can have a moment arm different from distance along the limb; static and dynamic cases differ."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-torque-p2","prompt":"Apply third-class levers and joint torque to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Torque is the turning effect of a force about an axis. Its magnitude is force multiplied by the perpendicular moment arm. A joint can balance external load torque with muscle torque in a simplified static condition. The force applied near a joint may need to be much larger than a distal load when its moment arm is short.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The model omits limb mass, other muscles and changing joint geometry.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on third-class levers and joint torque?","o":["Load torque = 20 × 0.30 = 6 N·m.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Load torque = 20 × 0.30 = 6 N·m. The model omits limb mass, other muscles and changing joint geometry."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The model omits limb mass, other muscles and changing joint geometry.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The model omits limb mass, other muscles and changing joint geometry. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-angular","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Moment of inertia and angular momentum","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Moment of inertia and angular momentum</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Moment of inertia describes resistance to changes in rotational motion and depends on both mass and its distribution relative to the axis. Moving mass closer to the axis can reduce moment of inertia without changing total mass. Angular velocity describes the rate of rotation, commonly in radians per second.","html":"Moment of inertia describes resistance to changes in rotational motion and depends on both mass and its distribution relative to the axis. Moving mass closer to the axis can reduce moment of inertia without changing total mass. Angular <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> describes the rate of rotation, commonly in radians per second.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Angular momentum in a simple rigid-axis model is L = Iω. If net external torque is negligible, angular momentum is conserved. Reducing I then increases ω to preserve L. The faster spin does not mean angular momentum increased; the distribution changed. External torque from contact or aerodynamic effects can alter the conservation condition.","html":"Angular momentum in a simple rigid-axis model is L = Iω. If net external torque is negligible, angular momentum is conserved. Reducing I then increases ω to preserve L. The faster spin does not mean angular momentum increased; the distribution changed. External torque from contact or aerodynamic effects can alter the conservation condition.","diagram":"angular"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A rotating athlete bringing limbs closer to the axis illustrates this relationship conceptually. Opening the shape increases I and can reduce ω under the same conservation assumption. A real sporting action may have complex three-dimensional axes, changing body configuration and external forces. The scalar formula simplifies those conditions.","html":"A rotating athlete bringing limbs closer to the axis illustrates this relationship conceptually. Opening the shape increases I and can reduce ω under the same conservation assumption. A real sporting action may have complex three-dimensional axes, changing body configuration and external forces. The scalar formula simplifies those conditions.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain axis, distribution and conservation assumption before calculation. Distinguish angular momentum from angular velocity and avoid saying compact shape creates energy or momentum from nothing. A classroom model does not teach aerial rotation technique or certify safety. Practical gymnastics or diving instruction needs appropriate facilities and qualified supervision.","html":"Explain axis, distribution and conservation assumption before calculation. Distinguish angular momentum from angular <mark class=\"pes-key\">velocity</mark> and avoid saying compact shape creates energy or momentum from nothing. A classroom model does not teach aerial rotation technique or certify safety. Practical gymnastics or diving instruction needs appropriate facilities and qualified supervision.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-angular-w1","prompt":"Initial I is 4 kg·m² and ω is 3 rad/s. I changes to 2 kg·m² with negligible external torque. Find L and new ω.","steps":["Initial L = 4 × 3 = 12 kg·m²/s.","Conservation gives 12 = 2ω, so new ω = 6 rad/s.","Angular velocity doubles while angular momentum remains 12; mass distribution changed."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-angular-p1","prompt":"A student says tucking creates extra angular momentum. Correct this and state when the conservation model may fail.","steps":["Tucking can reduce I, increasing ω for unchanged L when net external torque is negligible.","It does not automatically increase angular momentum.","External torque from ground contact or other interactions can change L, so identify the phase and system."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-angular-p2","prompt":"Apply moment of inertia and angular momentum to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Moment of inertia describes resistance to changes in rotational motion and depends on both mass and its distribution relative to the axis. Moving mass closer to the axis can reduce moment of inertia without changing total mass. Angular velocity describes the rate of rotation, commonly in radians per second.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Conceptual rotation models are not instructions for aerial skills.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on moment of inertia and angular momentum?","o":["Initial L = 4 × 3 = 12 kg·m²/s.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Initial L = 4 × 3 = 12 kg·m²/s. Conceptual rotation models are not instructions for aerial skills."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Conceptual rotation models are not instructions for aerial skills.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Conceptual rotation models are not instructions for aerial skills. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-force-summation","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Simultaneous and sequential force contributions","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Simultaneous and sequential force contributions</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Sporting force production often involves several joints and segments. Simultaneous contributions occur when actions overlap in time, as in parts of a vertical push. Sequential contributions organise movement across segments, often from larger proximal segments towards smaller distal ones, as in a throw. The purpose is effective transfer and timing rather than merely activating every muscle at once.","html":"Sporting <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> production often involves several joints and segments. Simultaneous contributions occur when actions overlap in time, as in parts of a vertical push. Sequential contributions organise <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> across segments, often from larger proximal segments towards smaller distal ones, as in a throw. The purpose is effective <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark> and timing rather than merely activating every <mark class=\"pes-key\">muscle</mark> at once.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Segmental interaction means movement of one segment affects others. A well-timed sequence can increase distal speed, while a poorly timed action can disrupt transfer. This is not equivalent to adding all individual forces as scalar numbers regardless of direction or time. Forces and torques act on specified bodies and have directions.","html":"Segmental interaction means <mark class=\"pes-key\">movement</mark> of one segment affects others. A well-timed sequence can increase distal speed, while a poorly timed action can disrupt <mark class=\"pes-key\">transfer</mark>. This is not equivalent to adding all individual forces as scalar numbers regardless of direction or time. Forces and torques act on specified bodies and have directions.","diagram":"sequence"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A throw may use legs, trunk, upper arm, forearm and hand in an organised sequence. The observable timing is more informative than listing muscles alone. A jump may show coordinated overlapping extension, but its exact pattern depends on the task. Avoid a rigid universal timetable or assuming that maximum speed at every joint simultaneously is optimal.","html":"A throw may use legs, trunk, upper arm, forearm and hand in an organised sequence. The observable timing is more informative than listing muscles alone. A jump may show coordinated overlapping extension, but its exact pattern depends on the task. Avoid a rigid universal timetable or assuming that maximum speed at every joint simultaneously is optimal.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Use video or a phase diagram to describe what happens when and how it contributes. A proposed cue should address the observed timing issue and suit the learner. Evaluate the result through comparable attempts and outcome as well as execution. Written explanation supports coaching analysis but does not itself establish practical technique quality.","html":"Use video or a phase diagram to describe what happens when and how it contributes. A proposed cue should address the observed timing issue and suit the learner. Evaluate the result through comparable attempts and outcome as well as execution. Written explanation supports coaching analysis but does not itself establish practical technique quality.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-force-summation-w1","prompt":"Why might a throw lose distal speed when the arm acts before the trunk contribution is effectively organised?","steps":["The timing can disrupt useful segmental interaction and transfer.","Sequential organisation coordinates proximal and distal actions rather than making every segment peak simultaneously.","Observation across phases is needed; the explanation is a plausible mechanism, not proof from one still image."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-force-summation-p1","prompt":"Compare overlapping extension in a jump and a proximal-to-distal throw. Correct: sum all muscle forces at different times to obtain ball force.","steps":["A jump can involve simultaneous or overlapping contributions; a throw often uses a sequence across segments.","Forces have direction and act on specific bodies and intervals.","Adding unrelated scalar muscle-force values ignores timing, geometry and interaction; analyse the actual phase and system."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-force-summation-p2","prompt":"Apply simultaneous and sequential force contributions to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Sporting force production often involves several joints and segments. Simultaneous contributions occur when actions overlap in time, as in parts of a vertical push. Sequential contributions organise movement across segments, often from larger proximal segments towards smaller distal ones, as in a throw. The purpose is effective transfer and timing rather than merely activating every muscle at once.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Phase diagrams illustrate timing, not athlete-specific measured forces.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on simultaneous and sequential force contributions?","o":["The timing can disrupt useful segmental interaction and transfer.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: The timing can disrupt useful segmental interaction and transfer. Phase diagrams illustrate timing, not athlete-specific measured forces."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Phase diagrams illustrate timing, not athlete-specific measured forces.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Phase diagrams illustrate timing, not athlete-specific measured forces. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-optimal-flight","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Optimise projection for a real target","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Optimise projection for a real target</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Optimal projection is determined by the goal and constraints. A maximum-distance throw, a basketball shot and a tennis ball clearing a net do not share one universal angle. Release speed, angle and height interact with the target, technique and air forces. The word optimal requires a stated criterion such as range, accuracy or suitable entry path.","html":"Optimal projection is determined by the goal and constraints. A maximum-distance throw, a basketball shot and a tennis ball clearing a net do not share one universal angle. Release speed, angle and height interact with the target, technique and air forces. The word optimal requires a stated criterion such as range, accuracy or suitable entry path.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"In the ideal equal-height no-drag model, range is v² sin(2θ)/g. This explains both the 45-degree result and the equal ranges of complementary angles at fixed speed. When release is above landing, flight time changes and the ideal maximum-range angle can differ. Real release speed may also vary with angle.","html":"In the ideal equal-height no-drag model, range is v² sin(2θ)/g. This explains both the 45-degree result and the equal ranges of complementary angles at fixed speed. When release is above landing, flight time changes and the ideal maximum-range angle can differ. Real release speed may also vary with angle.","diagram":"projectile"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A target-based analysis should consider obstacle clearance and arrival height as well as horizontal displacement. A spinning ball can deviate from the ideal parabola. Improving one variable may worsen another: a faster release can reduce control, or a high arc can allow an opponent more time. Evaluate the actual demand.","html":"A target-based analysis should consider obstacle clearance and arrival height as well as horizontal displacement. A spinning ball can deviate from the ideal parabola. Improving one variable may worsen another: a faster release can reduce control, or a high arc can allow an opponent more time. Evaluate the actual demand.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"State assumptions, identify what evidence supports the adjustment and discuss one trade-off. A simulation lets students vary ideal parameters and see the model’s behaviour, but it is not an instruction to copy an exact release angle in practice. Actual sport technique must be assessed with appropriate coaching and realistic conditions.","html":"State assumptions, identify what <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> supports the adjustment and discuss one trade-off. A simulation lets students vary ideal parameters and see the model’s behaviour, but it is not an instruction to copy an exact release angle in practice. Actual sport technique must be assessed with appropriate coaching and realistic conditions.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"projectile"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-optimal-flight-w1","prompt":"In the equal-height ideal model, v = 10 m/s, θ = 45°, g = 10 m/s². Calculate range and identify two assumptions.","steps":["R = 10² × sin(90°) ÷ 10 = 10 m.","Assumptions include equal release/landing height and no air resistance, with fixed release speed.","The result is not a universal target-shooting angle or real-ball prediction."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-optimal-flight-p1","prompt":"Explain why a higher release point and top spin can invalidate a simple 45-degree recommendation.","steps":["Different release/landing heights change flight time and the ideal range optimum.","Top spin introduces aerodynamic effects absent from the no-drag model.","The goal may require a target or net clearance rather than maximum range, and release speed may change with technique."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-optimal-flight-p2","prompt":"Apply optimise projection for a real target to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Optimal projection is determined by the goal and constraints. A maximum-distance throw, a basketball shot and a tennis ball clearing a net do not share one universal angle. Release speed, angle and height interact with the target, technique and air forces. The word optimal requires a stated criterion such as range, accuracy or suitable entry path.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Interactive outputs are ideal model results, not sport-technique recommendations.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on optimise projection for a real target?","o":["R = 10² × sin(90°) ÷ 10 = 10 m.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: R = 10² × sin(90°) ÷ 10 = 10 m. Interactive outputs are ideal model results, not sport-technique recommendations."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Interactive outputs are ideal model results, not sport-technique recommendations.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Interactive outputs are ideal model results, not sport-technique recommendations. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-fluid-flow","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Laminar, turbulent and drag forces","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Laminar, turbulent and drag forces</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Laminar flow has relatively orderly layers; turbulent flow has irregular fluctuations and mixing. Flow around a sporting object depends on shape, speed, surface and fluid conditions. A real flow can include both orderly and turbulent regions, so describing the whole situation with one label may hide important detail.","html":"Laminar flow has relatively orderly layers; turbulent flow has irregular fluctuations and mixing. Flow around a sporting object depends on shape, speed, surface and fluid conditions. A real flow can include both orderly and turbulent regions, so describing the whole situation with one label may hide important detail.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Pressure drag results from pressure differences around the object and associated wake behaviour. Surface drag concerns frictional effects along the object’s surface. Wave drag is relevant to producing waves at a free surface, such as a swimmer near the water surface. The categories identify different mechanisms that can act together.","html":"<mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> drag results from pressure differences around the object and associated wake behaviour. Surface drag concerns frictional effects along the object’s surface. Wave drag is relevant to producing waves at a free surface, such as a swimmer near the water surface. The categories identify different mechanisms that can act together.","diagram":"drag"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Streamlining can reduce pressure drag in appropriate conditions, but turbulence is not always simply worse. A golf ball’s surface can influence boundary-layer behaviour and flow separation, reducing pressure drag under suitable conditions despite adding surface disturbance. The total drag depends on the combined effects, not one universal smooth is best rule.","html":"Streamlining can reduce <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> drag in appropriate conditions, but turbulence is not always simply worse. A golf ball’s surface can influence boundary-layer behaviour and flow separation, reducing pressure drag under suitable conditions despite adding surface disturbance. The total drag depends on the combined effects, not one universal smooth is best rule.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain relative motion between object and fluid and identify the mechanism relevant to the sport. Equipment design must also comply with rules. A classroom explanation can compare shapes and likely effects but cannot infer an exact drag coefficient without data. Do not claim that less drag is always the only performance goal when control, lift and stability also matter.","html":"Explain relative motion between object and fluid and identify the mechanism relevant to the sport. Equipment design must also comply with rules. A classroom explanation can compare shapes and likely effects but cannot infer an exact drag coefficient without data. Do not claim that less drag is always the only performance goal when control, lift and stability also matter.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-fluid-flow-w1","prompt":"Compare pressure drag, surface drag and wave drag using a ball and swimmer example.","steps":["Pressure drag concerns pressure distribution and wake around an object such as a moving ball.","Surface drag arises from frictional effects along surfaces, including a swimmer’s body.","Wave drag concerns energy associated with creating surface waves, relevant to swimming near the free surface."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-fluid-flow-p1","prompt":"Critique: turbulence always increases total drag and a completely smooth golf ball must travel furthest.","steps":["Turbulence and boundary-layer behaviour can change separation and pressure drag.","A textured ball can reduce total drag in suitable regimes despite altered skin-friction effects.","Total drag depends on speed, shape and flow conditions; one surface label cannot prove range."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-fluid-flow-p2","prompt":"Apply laminar, turbulent and drag forces to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Laminar flow has relatively orderly layers; turbulent flow has irregular fluctuations and mixing. Flow around a sporting object depends on shape, speed, surface and fluid conditions. A real flow can include both orderly and turbulent regions, so describing the whole situation with one label may hide important detail.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The flow diagram illustrates mechanisms, not measured drag coefficients.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on laminar, turbulent and drag forces?","o":["Pressure drag concerns pressure distribution and wake around an object such as a moving ball.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Pressure drag concerns pressure distribution and wake around an object such as a moving ball. The flow diagram illustrates mechanisms, not measured drag coefficients."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The flow diagram illustrates mechanisms, not measured drag coefficients.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The flow diagram illustrates mechanisms, not measured drag coefficients. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-bernoulli","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Pressure differences and lift","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Pressure differences and lift</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Bernoulli’s principle relates pressure, speed and height along a streamline under suitable ideal flow assumptions. A faster local flow can correspond to lower static pressure in that model. Shape and orientation influence the surrounding flow and pressure distribution, which can produce a net force. This is not the claim that two air parcels must reunite after travelling around different sides.","html":"Bernoulli’s principle relates <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark>, speed and height along a streamline under suitable ideal flow assumptions. A faster local flow can correspond to lower static pressure in that model. Shape and orientation influence the surrounding flow and pressure distribution, which can produce a net <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark>. This is not the claim that two air parcels must reunite after travelling around different sides.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Lift is a force perpendicular to relative flow and need not be upward in every situation. Drag acts along the relative-flow direction opposing motion. A ball or implement can experience pressure forces, viscous effects and wake behaviour together. A simplified pressure explanation should not exclude the role of momentum change in the surrounding fluid.","html":"Lift is a <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> perpendicular to relative flow and need not be upward in every situation. Drag acts along the relative-flow direction opposing motion. A ball or implement can experience <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> forces, viscous effects and wake behaviour together. A simplified pressure explanation should not exclude the role of momentum change in the surrounding fluid.","diagram":"drag"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A sporting shape may generate a pressure difference that changes trajectory or support. The magnitude and direction depend on geometry, speed and conditions. An arrow diagram must state the relative flow and which region is higher or lower pressure. An unlabelled faster air slogan does not provide a complete physical account.","html":"A sporting shape may generate a <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> difference that changes trajectory or support. The magnitude and direction depend on geometry, speed and conditions. An arrow diagram must state the relative flow and which region is higher or lower pressure. An unlabelled faster air slogan does not provide a complete physical account.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Use the principle as one part of a reasoned model and name its limits. Avoid equal-transit-time myths or assuming every curved surface guarantees useful upward lift. Real flows can be viscous, unsteady and separated. For a spinning ball, connect the orientation of spin and travel to the resulting force rather than using shape alone.","html":"Use the principle as one part of a reasoned model and name its limits. Avoid equal-transit-time myths or assuming every curved surface guarantees useful upward lift. Real flows can be viscous, unsteady and separated. For a spinning ball, connect the orientation of spin and travel to the resulting <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> rather than using shape alone.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-bernoulli-w1","prompt":"Correct: air on the longer path must speed up to meet the air on the shorter path at the back.","steps":["There is no requirement for paired air parcels to meet again at the back.","Flow and pressure depend on shape, boundary conditions and interactions.","Bernoulli can relate local speed and pressure under suitable assumptions, but equal transit time is not the cause."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-bernoulli-p1","prompt":"Why is lift not always upward? Distinguish it from drag in a stated relative-flow model.","steps":["Lift is defined perpendicular to relative flow, so its direction depends on orientation and pressure distribution.","Drag acts along the relative-flow direction opposing the object’s motion through the fluid.","State flow direction and geometry; pressure and momentum descriptions should be consistent."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-bernoulli-p2","prompt":"Apply pressure differences and lift to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Bernoulli’s principle relates pressure, speed and height along a streamline under suitable ideal flow assumptions. A faster local flow can correspond to lower static pressure in that model. Shape and orientation influence the surrounding flow and pressure distribution, which can produce a net force. This is not the claim that two air parcels must reunite after travelling around different sides.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The schematic does not model full viscous or separated airflow.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on pressure differences and lift?","o":["There is no requirement for paired air parcels to meet again at the back.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: There is no requirement for paired air parcels to meet again at the back. The schematic does not model full viscous or separated airflow."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The schematic does not model full viscous or separated airflow.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The schematic does not model full viscous or separated airflow. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-magnus","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Biomechanics","title":"Top spin, back spin and side spin","subtitle":"Biomechanics · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Biomechanics · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Biomechanics"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Top spin, back spin and side spin</h3><p>Biomechanics · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"A spinning ball can experience an aerodynamic force that curves its path: the Magnus effect. Its direction depends on both travel and spin orientation. For a ball moving to the right in the diagram, top spin has the upper surface moving to the right relative to the centre; back spin has the upper surface moving left. In a common regime, top spin gives a downward force and back spin an upward force.","html":"A spinning ball can experience an aerodynamic <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> that curves its path: the Magnus effect. Its direction depends on both travel and spin orientation. For a ball moving to the right in the diagram, top spin has the upper surface moving to the right relative to the centre; back spin has the upper surface moving left. In a common regime, top spin gives a downward force and back spin an upward force.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Side spin gives a lateral force whose direction depends on the axis and sense of rotation. A side-view drawing cannot establish every three-dimensional curve. State the viewing direction and spin before assigning left or right. Saying clockwise always curves left is meaningless without those references and the travel direction.","html":"Side spin gives a lateral <mark class=\"pes-key\">force</mark> whose direction depends on the axis and sense of rotation. A side-view drawing cannot establish every three-dimensional curve. State the viewing direction and spin before assigning left or right. Saying clockwise always curves left is meaningless without those references and the travel direction.","diagram":"magnus"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"The effect involves flow asymmetry, pressure distribution and momentum change around the spinning ball. Real magnitude and even behaviour can depend on speed, surface, spin and flow regime. Gravity still acts, and drag can also alter speed; upward lift does not mean a back-spinning ball necessarily rises indefinitely.","html":"The effect involves flow asymmetry, <mark class=\"pes-key\">pressure</mark> distribution and momentum change around the spinning ball. Real magnitude and even behaviour can depend on speed, surface, spin and flow regime. Gravity still acts, and drag can also alter speed; upward lift does not mean a back-spinning ball necessarily rises indefinitely.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain the expected path relative to an otherwise comparable non-spinning ball, then discuss a sporting use and limitation. A top-spinning tennis shot may dip more strongly, helping combine speed with court clearance in suitable conditions. The schematic illustrates physics, not the racket technique needed to produce the spin or an exact trajectory prediction.","html":"Explain the expected path relative to an otherwise comparable non-spinning ball, then discuss a sporting use and limitation. A top-spinning tennis shot may dip more strongly, helping combine speed with court clearance in suitable conditions. The schematic illustrates physics, not the racket technique needed to produce the spin or an exact trajectory prediction.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-magnus-w1","prompt":"A ball travels right. In the stated diagram its upper surface rotates right for top spin and left for back spin. Predict common vertical Magnus directions.","steps":["Top spin: commonly downward Magnus force.","Back spin: commonly upward Magnus force.","Gravity and drag still act; real magnitude depends on speed, surface and spin. The prediction uses the stated viewing and travel directions."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-magnus-p1","prompt":"Critique: clockwise spin always makes a ball curve left and back spin removes gravity.","steps":["Clockwise needs a viewing direction, and curve direction also needs travel and spin-axis information.","Back spin can create upward aerodynamic force in common conditions but does not remove gravity.","Resultant motion depends on all forces and conditions; the model is not a universal exact flight prediction."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-magnus-p2","prompt":"Apply top spin, back spin and side spin to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: A spinning ball can experience an aerodynamic force that curves its path: the Magnus effect. Its direction depends on both travel and spin orientation. For a ball moving to the right in the diagram, top spin has the upper surface moving to the right relative to the centre; back spin has the upper surface moving left. In a common regime, top spin gives a downward force and back spin an upward force.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Travel and spin orientation are labelled; force magnitudes are not to scale.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on top spin, back spin and side spin?","o":["Top spin: commonly downward Magnus force.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Top spin: commonly downward Magnus force. Travel and spin orientation are labelled; force magnitudes are not to scale."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Travel and spin orientation are labelled; force magnitudes are not to scale.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Travel and spin orientation are labelled; force magnitudes are not to scale. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-mental-strategies","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Sport psychology","title":"Self-talk, relaxation, imagery and routines","subtitle":"Sport psychology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Sport psychology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Sport psychology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Self-talk, relaxation, imagery and routines</h3><p>Sport psychology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Performance strategies aim to manage relevant attention, confidence and activation. Self-talk can use a concise task-focused cue rather than a prediction of guaranteed success. Relaxation strategies can help reduce unnecessary tension or activation in suitable contexts, while energising cues may be more useful when activation is too low.","html":"Performance strategies aim to manage relevant <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark>, confidence and activation. Self-talk can use a concise task-focused cue rather than a prediction of guaranteed success. Relaxation strategies can help reduce unnecessary <mark class=\"pes-key\">tension</mark> or activation in suitable contexts, while energising cues may be more useful when activation is too low.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"A performance routine organises preparation into a familiar sequence. Goal setting can focus controllable actions and progress. Imagery mentally rehearses relevant aspects of performance, but it does not replace physical practice or guarantee the same result. Strategies before performance and during a rapid game phase face different time and attention constraints.","html":"A performance routine organises preparation into a familiar sequence. Goal setting can focus controllable actions and progress. Imagery mentally rehearses relevant aspects of performance, but it does not replace physical practice or guarantee the same result. Strategies before performance and during a rapid game phase face different time and <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark> constraints.","diagram":"attention"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"A free-throw routine might bring attention to a selected target and one action cue. During a rally, a brief reset cue may be more feasible than a long internal analysis. Individual preferences and task demands matter. A strategy that helps one athlete can distract another, so evaluate effects through discussion and repeated observations.","html":"A free-throw routine might bring <mark class=\"pes-key\">attention</mark> to a selected target and one action cue. During a rally, a brief reset cue may be more feasible than a long internal analysis. Individual preferences and task demands matter. A strategy that helps one athlete can distract another, so evaluate effects through discussion and repeated observations.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Explain the problem, strategy, mechanism and how its usefulness will be reviewed. Do not treat normal competition nerves as a clinical diagnosis or promise that a phrase eliminates anxiety. The site offers educational planning for sport performance; mental-health concerns require appropriate support outside an automated lesson score.","html":"Explain the problem, strategy, mechanism and how its usefulness will be reviewed. Do not treat normal competition nerves as a clinical diagnosis or promise that a phrase eliminates anxiety. The site offers educational planning for sport performance; mental-health concerns require appropriate support outside an automated lesson score.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-mental-strategies-w1","prompt":"A player becomes distracted after one error. Propose a brief during-performance strategy and explain its mechanism and limit.","steps":["A concise reset cue can redirect attention to the next relevant action.","A familiar routine or task-focused self-talk can reduce unproductive rumination and restore a controllable focus.","Effect varies; test with the athlete and teacher rather than guaranteeing removal of stress."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-mental-strategies-p1","prompt":"Compare imagery before performance and a brief cue during a fast rally. Why should relaxation not be prescribed identically to every athlete?","steps":["Pre-performance imagery can rehearse relevant action and decision sequences with more time available.","A rally needs a short cue that preserves attention to changing external information.","Some athletes need greater activation rather than calming; strategy depends on task and observed response."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-mental-strategies-p2","prompt":"Apply self-talk, relaxation, imagery and routines to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Performance strategies aim to manage relevant attention, confidence and activation. Self-talk can use a concise task-focused cue rather than a prediction of guaranteed success. Relaxation strategies can help reduce unnecessary tension or activation in suitable contexts, while energising cues may be more useful when activation is too low.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Performance planning does not diagnose or treat mental-health conditions.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on self-talk, relaxation, imagery and routines?","o":["A concise reset cue can redirect attention to the next relevant action.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: A concise reset cue can redirect attention to the next relevant action. Performance planning does not diagnose or treat mental-health conditions."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Performance planning does not diagnose or treat mental-health conditions.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Performance planning does not diagnose or treat mental-health conditions. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-cohesion","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Sport psychology","title":"Task and social cohesion","subtitle":"Sport psychology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Sport psychology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Sport psychology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Task and social cohesion</h3><p>Sport psychology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Task cohesion concerns shared commitment to the group’s performance aims and coordinated work. Social cohesion concerns interpersonal connection and enjoyment of being part of the group. They can support each other but are not identical. A team can be friendly yet poorly organised, or task-focused without close friendships among every member.","html":"Task cohesion concerns shared commitment to the group’s performance aims and coordinated work. Social cohesion concerns interpersonal connection and enjoyment of being part of the group. They can support each other but are not identical. A team can be friendly yet poorly organised, or task-focused without close friendships among every member.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Leadership, communication, shared goals and agreed roles can improve coordination. Team-building activities are useful when connected to actual needs rather than assumed to solve every conflict. Role clarity can help members understand what they contribute and how their actions relate to teammates’ responsibilities.","html":"Leadership, communication, shared goals and agreed roles can improve coordination. Team-building activities are useful when connected to actual needs rather than assumed to solve every conflict. Role clarity can help members understand what they contribute and how their actions relate to teammates’ responsibilities.","diagram":"cohesion"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Cohesion changes over time and can be affected by success, conflict, fairness perceptions and team dynamics. A single win does not prove strong cohesion, and a loss does not prove it is absent. Effective communication should allow issues to be raised respectfully and expectations to be reviewed, not demand unquestioning agreement.","html":"Cohesion changes over time and can be affected by success, conflict, fairness perceptions and team dynamics. A single win does not prove strong cohesion, and a loss does not prove it is absent. Effective communication should allow issues to be raised respectfully and expectations to be reviewed, not demand unquestioning agreement.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"Analyse the stated problem: is it unclear task coordination, social exclusion or both? Select a strategy addressing that mechanism and identify evidence to monitor. A shared goal needs agreement and practical responsibilities; a slogan alone may not alter behaviour. Individual goals should align with team aims without erasing legitimate individual needs.","html":"Analyse the stated problem: is it unclear task coordination, social exclusion or both? Select a strategy addressing that mechanism and identify <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> to monitor. A shared goal needs agreement and practical responsibilities; a slogan alone may not alter behaviour. Individual goals should align with team aims without erasing legitimate individual needs.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-cohesion-w1","prompt":"A team enjoys social events but repeatedly duplicates defensive assignments. Identify the cohesion distinction and a targeted strategy.","steps":["Social cohesion may be strong while task cohesion or coordination remains weak.","Clarify roles, communication calls and shared defensive goals through an appropriate team review.","Monitor comparable game situations; more social events alone may not solve assignment confusion."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-cohesion-p1","prompt":"A team performs coordinated tactics but one member feels excluded. Explain why task success does not resolve the social issue.","steps":["Task cohesion and social cohesion are different dimensions.","Good tactical coordination does not prove everyone feels included.","Use respectful communication and appropriate leadership to address inclusion while maintaining clear performance roles; one result is not a full cohesion measure."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-cohesion-p2","prompt":"Apply task and social cohesion to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Task cohesion concerns shared commitment to the group’s performance aims and coordinated work. Social cohesion concerns interpersonal connection and enjoyment of being part of the group. They can support each other but are not identical. A team can be friendly yet poorly organised, or task-focused without close friendships among every member.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Cohesion is not measured reliably by winning alone.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on task and social cohesion?","o":["Social cohesion may be strong while task cohesion or coordination remains weak.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Social cohesion may be strong while task cohesion or coordination remains weak. Cohesion is not measured reliably by winning alone."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["Cohesion is not measured reliably by winning alone.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"Cohesion is not measured reliably by winning alone. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-loafing","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Sport psychology","title":"Social loafing and team accountability","subtitle":"Sport psychology · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Sport psychology · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Sport psychology"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Social loafing and team accountability</h3><p>Sport psychology · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"Social loafing describes reduced individual effort when working in a group compared with an appropriate individual context. It can occur when contributions are hard to identify, responsibility is diffuse or a member feels their effort makes little difference. It should not be assigned from one mistake or a stereotype about a person.","html":"Social loafing describes reduced individual effort when working in a group compared with an appropriate individual context. It can occur when contributions are hard to identify, responsibility is diffuse or a member feels their effort makes little difference. It should not be assigned from one mistake or a stereotype about a person.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Role clarity, meaningful individual responsibility and feedback can support engagement. Shared goals and recognition of useful contributions can help members understand why effort matters. Excessive public blame can damage trust and motivation, so accountability should use evidence and respectful expectations rather than humiliation.","html":"Role clarity, meaningful individual responsibility and <mark class=\"pes-key\">feedback</mark> can support engagement. Shared goals and recognition of useful contributions can help members understand why effort matters. Excessive public blame can damage trust and motivation, so accountability should use <mark class=\"pes-key\">evidence</mark> and respectful expectations rather than humiliation.","diagram":"cohesion"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Team dynamics include relationships, communication, conflict and leadership patterns. A low contribution might also reflect unclear instruction, fatigue, injury or an inappropriate role; these are different explanations from social loafing. A coach should investigate context before treating all reduced output as unwillingness.","html":"Team dynamics include relationships, communication, conflict and leadership patterns. A low contribution might also reflect unclear instruction, fatigue, injury or an inappropriate role; these are different explanations from social loafing. A coach should investigate context before treating all reduced output as unwillingness.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"In a case response, describe the behaviour, propose plausible causes and justify an intervention. Use observation across comparable situations and conversation with members to test the explanation. A successful strategy should strengthen both responsibility and coordinated support. The site’s fictional cases do not classify real classmates or evaluate their character.","html":"In a case response, describe the behaviour, propose plausible causes and justify an intervention. Use observation across comparable situations and conversation with members to test the explanation. A successful strategy should strengthen both responsibility and coordinated support. The site’s fictional cases do not classify real classmates or evaluate their character.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Concept check 2","q":1},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-loafing-w1","prompt":"In a fictional group drill, responsibilities are unclear and some members reduce effort. Explain possible social loafing and one alternative cause.","steps":["Diffuse responsibility and invisible contribution can support social loafing.","Unclear instructions may also cause low contribution without deliberate effort reduction.","Clarify meaningful roles and feedback, then observe comparable tasks and discuss needs rather than blame from one example."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-loafing-p1","prompt":"Evaluate public criticism as the sole response to low group effort. Propose a more evidence-based accountability strategy.","steps":["Public blame can damage trust and may misidentify the cause.","Establish clear roles, agreed expectations and feedback on observable contributions.","Investigate alternative explanations and monitor changes; accountability should support useful effort and team coordination."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-loafing-p2","prompt":"Apply social loafing and team accountability to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: Social loafing describes reduced individual effort when working in a group compared with an appropriate individual context. It can occur when contributions are hard to identify, responsibility is diffuse or a member feels their effort makes little difference. It should not be assigned from one mistake or a stereotype about a person.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: The lesson does not label real students from a single behaviour.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is supported by this lesson on social loafing and team accountability?","o":["Diffuse responsibility and invisible contribution can support social loafing.","The same conclusion applies without considering the task, phase or conditions.","One successful result proves the cause and excludes every alternative explanation.","A browser concept score certifies practical skill and an official exam grade."],"a":0,"why":"The relevant principle is: Diffuse responsibility and invisible contribution can support social loafing. The lesson does not label real students from a single behaviour."},{"q":"Which qualification matters when applying this lesson?","o":["The lesson does not label real students from a single behaviour.","No model assumptions or measurement limits need to be stated.","A single image supplies every force, mental state and physiological cause.","A fictional classroom dataset is an individual training or treatment prescription."],"a":0,"why":"The lesson does not label real students from a single behaviour. Connect evidence with mechanism and identify what the model cannot establish."}]},{"id":"pes12-written-exam","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Coaching, advanced mechanics and application","area":"Integrated application","title":"Written exam design and original rehearsal","subtitle":"Integrated application · Mechanism, evidence and sport application","scope":"Integrated application · Explain · Apply · Evaluate","outcomes":["Integrated application"],"icon":"🏃","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and practice route","html":"<h3>Written exam design and original rehearsal</h3><p>Integrated application · Understand the mechanism, explain its sporting application and qualify your conclusion.</p><p>Read four focused explanations, explore the labelled model, attempt the worked case before its steps, then complete independent and sport-context practice. Allow about 25–40 minutes, with extra time for extended cases. All numerical data are fictional.</p><p>Written work is self-reviewed against suggested points. Concept mastery, screen visits and plans are not a practical assessment or predicted ATAR.</p>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the concept","text":"The current Year 12 written examination allows ten minutes reading and two and a half hours working. Section One has twenty multiple-choice questions worth twenty per cent, with a suggested thirty minutes. Section Two has six to ten short-answer questions worth fifty per cent, with a suggested seventy minutes. Section Three requires two of four extended-answer questions worth thirty per cent, with a suggested fifty minutes.","html":"The current Year 12 written examination allows ten minutes reading and two and a half hours working. Section One has twenty multiple-choice questions worth twenty per cent, with a suggested thirty minutes. Section Two has six to ten short-answer questions worth fifty per cent, with a suggested seventy minutes. Section Three requires two of four extended-answer questions worth thirty per cent, with a suggested fifty minutes.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the mechanism","text":"Read the actual paper instructions because syllabus and examination versions can change. The written component and practical component are separate: the combined Year 12 assessment weighting is seventy per cent written and thirty per cent practical. The written school-assessment types have their own weights, which must not be confused with the exam’s section percentages.","html":"Read the actual paper instructions because syllabus and examination versions can change. The written component and practical component are separate: the combined Year 12 assessment weighting is seventy per cent written and thirty per cent practical. The written school-assessment types have their own weights, which must not be confused with the exam’s section percentages.","diagram":"exam"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to sport","text":"Explain questions need a connected mechanism, not just a named term. Analysis uses the supplied data and identifies relationships. Evaluation makes a judgement using criteria and recognises a limit. Numerical work should show formula, substitution, units and a contextual conclusion. A diagram needs labels and explanatory notes connected to the answer.","html":"Explain questions need a connected mechanism, not just a named term. Analysis uses the supplied data and identifies relationships. Evaluation makes a judgement using criteria and recognises a limit. Numerical work should show formula, substitution, units and a contextual conclusion. A diagram needs labels and explanatory notes connected to the answer.","diagram":null},{"kind":"learn","title":"Qualify and evaluate","text":"This course includes original practice and suggested points, not an official SCSA paper or key. A self-review score and concept mastery cannot predict a grade. Use official papers alongside school tasks and timed practice. Practical skill and tactical execution require the school’s teacher-managed programme; completing a browser lesson is not completion of the practical assessment.","html":"This course includes original practice and suggested points, not an official SCSA paper or key. A self-review score and concept mastery cannot predict a grade. Use official papers alongside school tasks and timed practice. Practical skill and tactical execution require the school’s teacher-managed programme; completing a browser lesson is not completion of the practical assessment.","diagram":null},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the model and explain the evidence","model":"analysis"},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked case · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Worked case · step-by-step model","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent short-answer practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Transfer to your selected sport","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Two · Neural activation and force","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Two · Muscle model","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Two · Environment case","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Two · Periodisation data","q":6},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Two · Impulse calculation","q":7},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Two · Collision and torque","q":8},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Two · Flight diagram","q":9},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Two · Coaching evidence","q":10},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Three option · Repeated-effort tournament","q":11},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Three option · Skill improvement programme","q":12},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Three option · Biomechanical performance case","q":13},{"kind":"question","title":"Section Three option · Psychology and performance claims","q":14},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 1","q":0},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 2","q":1},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 3","q":2},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 4","q":3},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 5","q":4},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 6","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 7","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 8","q":7},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 9","q":8},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 10","q":9},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 11","q":10},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 12","q":11},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 13","q":12},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 14","q":13},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 15","q":14},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 16","q":15},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 17","q":16},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 18","q":17},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 19","q":18},{"kind":"check","title":"Original Section One question 20","q":19},{"kind":"sources","title":"Official resources and practical transfer","html":"<h3>Continue with school practice</h3><p>Apply the concepts through your teacher’s selected sport and supervised programme. This site does not record or grade competitive performance. Year 12 uses a school-based practical (performance) external assessment; complete the actual school requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/health-and-physical-education/physical-education-studies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official SCSA PE Studies syllabuses and assessments</a> · <a href=\"PE-STUDIES-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Coverage, sources and practice guide</a></p><p>All scenarios, numerical data and questions are original teaching material. Suggested points are not official SCSA keys. Actual paper and school instructions take priority.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"pes12-written-exam-w1","prompt":"A question asks why an athlete spins faster after reducing moment of inertia. Write an explanation with the necessary assumption.","steps":["With negligible net external torque, angular momentum L = Iω is conserved.","Reducing I requires ω to increase to maintain L.","The athlete spins faster without creating extra angular momentum; if external torque is significant the simple conservation account needs revision."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-p1","prompt":"A question asks whether a fictional training intervention caused improvement when conditions and opponents also changed. Write an evaluative conclusion.","steps":["The observed improvement is compatible with benefit but does not isolate the intervention as the cause.","Changed conditions and opposition are confounding factors.","Use comparable repeated observations or a suitable design before a stronger causal claim; identify what the data support and what remains uncertain."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"mode":"Short-answer skill practice"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-p2","prompt":"Apply written exam design and original rehearsal to your school’s selected sport. Use one specific teacher-approved observation or clearly labelled fictional scenario. Explain the principle through a labelled diagram or causal sequence, propose a relevant performance or coaching implication, and identify one limitation. This task requires your chosen context; it does not assess actual skill execution.","steps":["Identify the sport, action, phase and specific observation; distinguish actual evidence from an invented case.","Explain the relevant mechanism accurately. Useful lesson principles: The current Year 12 written examination allows ten minutes reading and two and a half hours working. Section One has twenty multiple-choice questions worth twenty per cent, with a suggested thirty minutes. Section Two has six to ten short-answer questions worth fifty per cent, with a suggested seventy minutes. Section Three requires two of four extended-answer questions worth thirty per cent, with a suggested fifty minutes.","Connect the principle to a justified implication. Use the worked model as a reasoning example, not as evidence of your own performance.","Label a relevant diagram or causal sequence, with directions and units where appropriate.","State a meaningful limit: Practice points are authored suggestions, not official marking or ATAR prediction.","Identify what comparable observation or appropriate teacher feedback could test the explanation. A plan is not a practical assessment grade."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Sport-context transfer · needs chosen context"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-short1","prompt":"Explain a sensory-to-motor pathway for reacting to a ball and two mechanisms for grading muscle force.","steps":["Sensory pathways convey relevant information towards the central nervous system.","Brain and spinal cord processing coordinate response; exact pathways depend on task.","Motor neurons supply output to their innervated fibres.","A motor unit is one motor neuron and its muscle fibres.","Recruitment increases active units and rate coding changes firing frequency.","All-or-none unit activation does not make whole-muscle force all-or-none."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-short2","prompt":"Compare the A band, I band and H zone during shortening, then distinguish calcium and ATP roles.","steps":["Z lines move closer and overlap increases.","A band remains approximately constant because thick-filament length stays constant.","I band and H zone narrow; the filaments do not shrink.","Calcium binds troponin and shifts tropomyosin to expose sites.","ATP binding supports detachment; hydrolysis prepares further cycling.","ATP also supports calcium pumping; calcium is not an ATP substitute."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-short3","prompt":"A fictional tournament moves to a hot humid afternoon. Explain three mechanisms affecting performance and two planning considerations.","steps":["Temperature gradients can reduce dry heat loss or produce heat gain.","Humidity can reduce sweat evaporation, so visible sweat does not prove effective cooling.","Skin blood flow and sweating demands can add cardiovascular strain.","Review scheduling, shade and planned breaks within current school procedures.","Review the established individual hydration arrangements without prescribing a universal amount.","Acclimatisation takes time and does not eliminate risk; this is planning analysis, not treatment."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-short4","prompt":"A fictional 12-week macrocycle contains three 4-week blocks and weekly schedules. Before a priority event, volume falls while some intensity is retained. Identify the cycles and explain the change.","steps":["The stated 12-week plan is a macrocycle.","The 4-week blocks are mesocycles; weekly schedules are microcycles.","Durations are contextual, not universal definitions.","The pre-event change illustrates tapering.","Reducing load while retaining useful stimulus can support readiness.","Competition and recovery monitoring should guide adjustments; the plan is not a personal prescription."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-short5","prompt":"A 0.4 kg ball changes from +6 to −4 m/s during 0.05 s. Calculate Δp and average net force; compare a 0.10 s interval for the same change.","steps":["Δp = 0.4 × (−4 − 6).","Δp = −4 kg·m/s.","Average net force = −4 / 0.05 = −80 N.","For 0.10 s, average net force = −40 N.","Longer time reduces average force for the same impulse.","These are net average values, not peak contact force; state the reference direction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-short6","prompt":"A fixed-surface drop is 1.44 m and rebound 0.81 m. Estimate e. A separate static lever has 15 N load at 0.4 m and effort arm 0.05 m. Find balancing effort.","steps":["e = √(0.81 / 1.44) = √0.5625.","e = 0.75, under no-drag vertical-bounce assumptions.","Load torque = 15 × 0.4 = 6 N·m.","Effort = 6 / 0.05 = 120 N.","Use perpendicular arms and static balance.","The lever calculation omits other forces and limb weight; restitution depends on both bodies and conditions."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-short7","prompt":"Compare ideal no-spin flight with top-spin and back-spin flight for rightward travel in a stated common regime. Identify gravity, drag and the ideal-angle limitation.","steps":["Gravity continues acting downward in all cases.","Drag opposes relative motion; the ideal no-drag model omits it.","For rightward travel, top surface right identifies top spin, commonly adding downward Magnus force.","Top surface left identifies back spin, commonly adding upward Magnus force.","Real force depends on speed, surface, spin and flow regime.","45 degrees is an ideal equal-height fixed-speed range result, not a universal sport target angle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-short8","prompt":"A learner’s stationary passing is accurate but decisions fail against defenders. Apply transfer and the analysis cycle, and explain one feedback choice.","steps":["The issue concerns training-to-competition transfer and omitted changing information demands.","Prepare relevant decision and execution criteria, then observe comparable attempts.","Evaluate the supported priority rather than assuming technique is the only cause.","Introduce a manageable progression with defenders and a useful scanning cue.","Provide terminal feedback distinguishing result and decision/process where appropriate.","Re-observe and compare; improved outcome alone does not prove the cause."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1,1],"marks":6,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-extended1","prompt":"A fictional player faces repeated short rallies across a tournament, with a hot humid afternoon and little time between matches. Analyse energy supply, nutrition/hydration principles, environmental demands and recovery planning. Do not prescribe personal amounts or treatment.","steps":["Begin by separating short high-intensity rally demands from lower-intensity movement and recovery between rallies. ATP–CP can contribute substantially to rapid efforts, but glycolytic and aerobic contributions remain present. Aerobic processes also support recovery and repeated-work capacity; do not describe three timed switches.","Carbohydrate availability can support demanding repeated activity and glycogen restoration. Protein contributes amino acids for repair and adaptation within adequate total intake. Pre-event food needs include established tolerance; during- and post-event needs depend on actual duration and access. GI alone cannot specify a complete diet.","Heat can reduce dry heat loss and humidity limits evaporation. Sweating visibly is not proof that sufficient cooling occurs. Increased thermal and circulatory demands can affect readiness across matches. Appropriate scheduling, shade, breaks and existing school procedures should be reviewed without treating acclimatisation as immediate protection.","Hydration needs depend on individual losses and conditions. Avoid a universal large drinking target or using fictional arithmetic as a prescription. Recovery includes rest, adequate energy, fluid and a realistic competition/training balance; a supplement cannot replace these foundations.","Evaluate the written plan using actual match timing, comparable performance and recovery observations. A persistent adverse pattern requires appropriate support and review, but one tired match does not diagnose overtraining. State what information is missing before making a stronger judgement."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1],"marks":5,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Three · choose two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-extended2","prompt":"A fictional netball team performs passes accurately without defenders, loses possession under pressure and has unclear support roles. Design and justify a coaching-analysis and team strategy, including transfer, feedback, leadership and cohesion.","steps":["Prepare criteria for relevant scanning, pass selection, execution and support positioning. Observe several comparable game-like situations before assigning the cause. Accurate isolated execution alongside poor contested decisions suggests a possible transfer gap, not proof that every technical feature is already adequate.","Evaluate whether the main priority is stimulus identification, response selection, support spacing or execution. Use a manageable static-to-dynamic progression with changing defenders, preserving useful timing and decision information. Shaping can adjust difficulty; chaining can link components when the parts remain meaningful.","Provide focused terminal feedback explaining the observed process and result. A learner can use intrinsic outcome and balance information, supplemented by coach or video evidence. Avoid simultaneous corrections to every feature and use a suitable camera/checklist rather than unsupported claims from one image.","Use direct instruction where the task needs shared initial clarity and democratic discussion where experienced players can contribute useful observations. Agree communication calls, individual roles and a shared tactical goal. These address task cohesion; social friendship alone does not guarantee coordinated support.","Re-observe under comparable conditions, review decision quality as well as possession outcomes and adjust the plan. Unclear responsibilities can reduce contribution, but social loafing should not be diagnosed from one error. The written strategy supports actual supervised practice rather than certifying competitive proficiency."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1],"marks":5,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Three · choose two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-extended3","prompt":"A fictional athlete throws a ball, another rotates with changing body shape, and a catcher stops a ball. Explain how segment timing, projection, angular momentum and impulse can be used in analysis. Include equations and assumptions; no practical aerial or contact instruction is required.","steps":["A throw depends on coordinated segmental interaction, often involving proximal-to-distal sequencing. Timing matters more than simply making every segment peak together. Identify phases from suitable observation; forces on different bodies and at different times cannot simply be added as unrelated scalars.","Projection depends on speed, angle, height and the target. The ideal equal-height no-drag range equation R = v² sin(2θ)/g gives 45 degrees only at fixed speed under those assumptions. Real release height, drag, spin and technique can change the optimum; accuracy or net clearance may be the actual criterion.","For a simplified rotation, L = Iω is conserved only if net external torque is negligible. Moving mass closer to the axis reduces I and can increase ω while L remains constant. For example I changing from 4 to 2 with L = 12 changes ω from 3 to 6. This creates no extra angular momentum.","For stopping, impulse equals Δp = m(vf − vi), and average net force is Δp/Δt. Increasing the stopping interval for the same momentum change reduces average force. It does not establish peak force or provide an exact safe-contact technique.","Use labelled directions, units and model boundaries. Lever torque requires perpendicular moment arms and additional forces in a realistic system. The analysis supports explanation and teacher feedback; it cannot certify practical technique, safety or athlete-specific maximum values."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1],"marks":5,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Three · choose two"},{"id":"pes12-written-exam-extended4","prompt":"A fictional team is worried before competition, uses only a winning goal and receives a supplement advertisement claiming guaranteed performance. Evaluate a psychological plan and the advertisement using evidence, risks, team dynamics and permitted status.","steps":["Distinguish activation from perceived stress and confidence. The inverted-U is a hypothesis whose useful region depends on task, experience and individual response. Do not assume every athlete needs calming or diagnose a condition from competition nerves.","Add controllable process goals and measurable performance goals alongside the outcome aim. Define a SMARTER version and include evaluation and review. A familiar routine, task-focused self-talk or appropriate imagery can support attention; effectiveness should be checked rather than promised.","Clarify team roles, communication and shared aims to support task cohesion. Address inclusion and interpersonal needs separately as social cohesion. Reduced contribution may relate to diffuse responsibility, but alternative explanations should be considered before using a social-loafing label.","Evaluate a supplement through mechanism, relevant evidence, need, health risk and product contamination. Protein can support adequate amino-acid intake, caffeine has variable alertness/performance effects and possible unwanted effects, and creatine can support some repeated high-intensity demands. None guarantees technique, victory or recovery.","Anabolic agents, EPO-related substances and specified blood-manipulation methods are prohibited categories under current WADA rules. Natural physiology does not establish the legality of an administered substance. Check current AIS and Sport Integrity Australia sources; no student product use, dose or administration is recommended."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1],"marks":5,"practice":true,"mode":"Original rehearsal · Section Three · choose two"}],"bank":[{"q":"During sarcomere shortening, which stays approximately constant?","o":["A-band length","I-band width","Z-line separation","H-zone width"],"a":0,"why":"Thick-filament length remains approximately constant; actin slides into greater overlap."},{"q":"What exposes actin binding sites?","o":["Calcium regulation through troponin and tropomyosin","Calcium becoming ATP","Myosin filaments dissolving","A-band expansion"],"a":0,"why":"Calcium binds troponin and changes tropomyosin position."},{"q":"A motor unit consists of:","o":["One motor neuron and its innervated muscle fibres","One sensory neuron and an entire limb","All motor neurons in the body","A fascicle and its artery"],"a":0,"why":"Motor units are recruited to grade whole-muscle output."},{"q":"Which mechanism differs from recruitment?","o":["Increasing impulses per second in active units","Activating additional units","Including larger units","Adding active fibres through more units"],"a":0,"why":"Rate coding changes firing frequency; recruitment changes active units."},{"q":"Type I generally has:","o":["High oxidative capacity and fatigue resistance","No mitochondria","Only maximal sprint use","No role in sport"],"a":0,"why":"Characteristics are tendencies; muscles contain mixed fibre populations."},{"q":"Humidity particularly reduces the potential for:","o":["Sweat evaporation","Gravity","Oxygen fraction in air","All muscle recruitment"],"a":0,"why":"A smaller vapour-pressure gradient can limit effective evaporation."},{"q":"At altitude, the primary oxygen challenge is:","o":["Lower inspired oxygen partial pressure","No oxygen molecules remaining","Instant loss of all haemoglobin","Identical pressure with zero nitrogen"],"a":0,"why":"Oxygen fraction stays approximately similar while atmospheric pressure falls."},{"q":"Which is a mesocycle in a stated nested plan?","o":["A training block within the broad period","A single nerve impulse","The total body mass","An individual sarcomere"],"a":0,"why":"Cycle length depends on the programme rather than one universal calendar."},{"q":"A useful taper generally:","o":["Reduces load while preserving useful stimulus","Stops all training indefinitely","Doubles every variable immediately","Removes all recovery"],"a":0,"why":"The aim is readiness, not prolonged cessation."},{"q":"Which statement about supplements is sound?","o":["Ingredient status does not establish every product’s purity","Every protein powder guarantees strength","Creatine replaces technique learning","Caffeine guarantees improved sleep"],"a":0,"why":"Evidence, safety, need and contamination risk are separate considerations."},{"q":"Transfer that interferes with the target task is:","o":["Negative","Always positive","Defined only by indoor practice","The same as speed"],"a":0,"why":"State source and target and consider alternative explanations."},{"q":"Which follows intervention in the analysis cycle?","o":["Re-observation","Assuming permanent success","Ignoring outcomes","A medical diagnosis from one frame"],"a":0,"why":"Compare the priority feature again under suitable conditions."},{"q":"A coach links components into a sequence. This illustrates:","o":["Chaining","Blood redistribution","Wave drag","Rate coding"],"a":0,"why":"Shaping progressively modifies a skill; chaining links components."},{"q":"Democratic leadership involves:","o":["Suitable participant involvement in decisions","No agreed expectations","Every decision being urgent and unilateral","A guarantee of winning"],"a":0,"why":"Style should fit experience, context and task."},{"q":"A 0.5 kg ball changes from +4 to −4 m/s. Its Δp is:","o":["−4 kg·m/s","0 kg·m/s","+8 kg·m/s","−8 kg·m/s"],"a":0,"why":"0.5 × (−4 − 4) = −4 kg·m/s. Direction matters."},{"q":"Fixed-surface drop 1 m, rebound 0.49 m gives ideal e:","o":["0.7","0.49","1.49","2.04"],"a":0,"why":"e = square root of rebound/drop, under the stated assumptions."},{"q":"With conserved L, halving I gives angular velocity:","o":["Twice the initial value","Half the value","Zero regardless of L","Four times the value"],"a":0,"why":"Iω stays constant with negligible net external torque."},{"q":"A 10 N force with 0.2 m perpendicular arm gives torque:","o":["2 N·m","50 N·m","0.02 N·m","2 N/s"],"a":0,"why":"Torque = force × perpendicular moment arm."},{"q":"The equal-height ideal range optimum of 45° assumes:","o":["Fixed speed and no air resistance","All sports have ground-level targets","Spin cancels gravity","Release speed always rises with angle"],"a":0,"why":"Real target height, spin, drag and technique can change the optimum."},{"q":"A friendly team with unclear defensive roles may have:","o":["Social cohesion without effective task coordination","No possible social connection","Guaranteed tactical success","A proven diagnosis of social loafing"],"a":0,"why":"Task and social cohesion are different; investigate the actual issue."}]}];
